# SEK-08 canonical knowledge-graph projection (Turtle).
# Deterministic: sorted subjects/predicates/objects; fixed build date 2026-11-07.
# Source of truth: ten reviewed SEK-06/SEK-07 lane bundles + pinned model 0.3.0.

@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix se: <https://github.com/kaicreator-mm/tally#> .

se:ANT-07A-UNIVERSAL-LIFECYCLE-MANDATE a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07A-SYNTHESIS-UNIVERSAL-LIFECYCLE ;
     se:causes "Misconceptions about the chosen model (that Agile is a method in itself; that Agile is faster because no documents are needed; that Agile has a limited or unstructured practice set).", "Skipping the adaptation activity: no identification of characteristics, no deliberate selection of model/stages/processes, no documented rationale.", "Treating any process model as universally mandatory rather than as a paradigm to conform to and tailor." ;
     se:consequences "Long stretches without deliverables when large mandated processes produce nothing interim, increasing uncertainty.", "Process-to-project mismatch: a model's assumptions (e.g., closed requirement sets in predictive cycles) collide with the project's actual change profile.", "Wrong or uncertain estimations within the mismatched life cycle lead to failure, per the Guide's practical considerations." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-SYNTHESIS-UNIVERSAL-LIFECYCLE ;
     se:label "Universal life cycle mandate" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Select and adapt the life cycle per SWEBOK KA10: identify the system's differential characteristics and stakeholder needs, choose and tailor the model, stages and processes, document the decisions and rationale, and support the result with realistic estimations and measurements; treat model-family debates as empirical questions requiring measurement, not identity debates." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-level characterization grounded in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 sections 1.1, 2.3-2.5 and 2.9; the counterproductive nature of a universal mandate is a SEK-07A synthesis inference (see se:EVD-07A-SYNTHESIS-UNIVERSAL-LIFECYCLE), not a statement made by the Guide." ;
     se:symptoms "Life cycle adoption decisions exist without documented adaptation rationale.", "Model-choice debates (e.g., waterfall versus Agile) proceed without realistic estimations or measurements.", "One life cycle model (predictive or Agile) is mandated for all projects regardless of system characteristics or organizational context." .

se:ANT-07B-BIG-BANG-INTEGRATION a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07B-SYNTHESIS-BIG-BANG ;
     se:causes "Deferring integration of component parts until all parts intended for release in a version are complete (SWEBOK's definition of phased integration).", "No interim scaffolding (stubs, drivers, mock objects) or interim-version test points are planned.", "Treating integration as a terminal milestone rather than an ongoing construction activity." ;
     se:consequences "Delayed product delivery and customer feedback relative to incremental integration (the contrasts SWEBOK names as incremental's advantages).", "Harder fault isolation across simultaneously integrated parts.", "Late discovery of integration defects, when structural decisions are already fixed." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-SYNTHESIS-BIG-BANG ;
     se:label "Big bang (phased) integration as the default strategy" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Integrate incrementally: write and test small pieces, combine them one at a time with stubs, drivers and mock objects, and determine points at which interim versions are tested; or adopt continuous integration with an automated pipeline that builds and tests each integration, as described in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 3.7." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-level characterization (SEK-07B): the symptom/causes/consequences framing is a synthesis inversion of SWEBOK 3.7's stated advantages of incremental over phased integration (see se:EVD-07B-SYNTHESIS-BIG-BANG); the Guide names phased/big bang integration and the advantage list but does not itself label it an anti-pattern." ;
     se:symptoms "Error location is difficult because all parts land at once.", "Faults and interface mismatches surface together in one late integration event instead of progressively.", "Feedback to developers and customers arrives late.", "Progress between code-complete and integrated is hard to observe." .

se:ANT-07C-SECURITY-AFTERTHOUGHT a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07C-SYNTHESIS-AFTERTHOUGHT ;
     se:causes "Lifecycle stages owned without security linkage between requirements, design, implementation and verification.", "Mistaking patch capacity and incident response for a security practice.", "Treating security as a separable technical add-on rather than a lifecycle-crosscutting concern." ;
     se:consequences "Known-vulnerability checking has no requirements anchor, so compliance violations surface late or never.", "Maintenance cost grows as fixes bypass the design rationale that a design-in approach would have provided.", "Reactive patching replaces design mitigation; the same root causes recur because requirements, risks and design decisions were never recorded." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SYNTHESIS-AFTERTHOUGHT ;
     se:label "Security as a post-development afterthought" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Adopt design-in security: maintain a security-requirements baseline through the SDLC, threat-model at design, review the design against requirements and risk information, and treat vulnerability management as the handling of residual risk rather than the primary security mechanism." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-level characterization derived by marked synthesis (se:EVD-07C-SYNTHESIS-AFTERTHOUGHT) from SWEBOK KA13 section 1 and SSDF PO.1/PW.1; the sources assert the positive practice, the anti-pattern framing is this lane's inference." ;
     se:symptoms "No security requirements exist at design time; security work surfaces as patch requests after release.", "Threat assumptions are nowhere recorded, so design changes cannot be checked against them.", "Verification has no security-requirements baseline to verify against; only reactive vulnerability fixes occur." .

se:ANT-07D-HOPE-BASED-OPERATIONS a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07D-SYNTHESIS-HOPE-BASED-OPS ;
     se:causes "Confusing absence of reported problems with absence of problems.", "No owner for the monitoring/alerting/telemetry loop the curriculum and Guide both name.", "Treating measurement instrumentation as optional overhead rather than as the feedback mechanism for reliability." ;
     se:consequences "Capacity and availability claims cannot be evidenced against service-level targets, inviting disputes.", "Deviations are detected late, after they have already affected users.", "Reliability work loses its data foundation: failure data never accumulates, so reliability measurement and stop-testing decisions have nothing to draw on." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-MONITOR-EVIDENCE, se:EVD-07D-SYNTHESIS-HOPE-BASED-OPS ;
     se:label "Hope-based operations (no measurement feedback loop)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Build the measurement feedback loop the anchors name — production monitoring and product telemetry, monitoring and alerting, dashboards and release qualification metrics — surface real-time indicators to stakeholders, and route operational decisions through that evidence, per SWEBOK KA6 section 4.3's norm that hope should not be a strategy." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-level characterization grounded in SWEBOK KA6 section 4.3's evidence-over-hope norm combined with CS2023 SE-Reliability Non-core topic 11; the counterproductive framing is a SEK-07D synthesis inference (see se:EVD-07D-SYNTHESIS-HOPE-BASED-OPS), not a statement made by either source." ;
     se:symptoms "No real-time evidence surface (telemetry, dashboards, release qualification measurements) exists for the service's operational health.", "Operational decisions about reliability, capacity or availability are made from assumption or schedule pressure rather than observed indicators.", "Unplanned nonavailability, capacity exhaustion or degrading reliability is discovered by stakeholders rather than by monitoring or alerting." .

se:ANT-07E-MANUAL-HANDOFF-DEPLOYMENT a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:causes "Deployment steps (packaging, configuration, server and database setup, installation, launch, smoke testing) not automated.", "Organizational separation of development from deployment responsibility." ;
     se:consequences "Manual steps introduce variation that undermines verification results obtained earlier in the pipeline.", "SWEBOK KA6 3.2 characterizes the resulting processes as inefficient from both time and quality perspectives.", "Slow, irregular deployments delay the availability of fixes and features." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:label "Manual hand-off deployment" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Automate the deployment steps — packaging the code, generating configuration files, restarting servers, configuring servers and databases, installing the software, launching execution and executing smoke testing — as SWEBOK KA6 3.2 reports under DevOps, keeping the deployment executable by the same pipeline that built and verified the artifacts." ;
     se:scope_description "The counterproductive characterization is directly stated in SWEBOK KA6 section 3.2 (inefficient in time and quality); the anti-pattern packaging (symptoms/causes/remediation structure) is lane-level arrangement of that inspected contrast, not a named anti-pattern in the source." ;
     se:symptoms "A finished application is transferred from the development team to a separate operations team that performs the deployment.", "Deployment outcomes differ run to run and depend on who executes them.", "Deployment steps are executed mostly by hand rather than through automated steps." .

se:ANT-07F-UNMANAGED-TECH-DEBT a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT ;
     se:causes "Compromised maintainability: maintainability not specified, reviewed and controlled during development.", "Maintenance economics less visible than development economics, so debt cost is not quantified in management reporting.", "Time and comprehension constraints on maintainers producing immediate but under-considered solutions.", "Uncertain reward for debt work discouraging investment." ;
     se:consequences "Excess unplanned work grows, with team and process issues compounding the code-level debt.", "Program comprehension and subsequent impact analysis become harder as structure degrades.", "Technical debt accumulates and takes additional time and effort to address during later maintenance." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-ORG-PRIORITIES, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:label "Unmanaged technical-debt accumulation" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Measure the debt (size, complexity, flaws, practice violations) with available tooling; determine current quality, debt extent, potential savings and business impact; triage urgency (not all debt is urgent); balance organizational objectives with maintainability and engineering standards; and address root causes including team and process factors rather than handling debt in isolation — per SWEBOK KA7 2.1.4, 2.2.1 and 2.3.1, with refactoring (KA7 4.2, CS2023 topic 3) as the named remediation technique for code-level debt." ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in SWEBOK v4.0a KA7 sections 2.1.4, 2.2.1 and 2.3.1, whose text directly characterizes this accumulation pattern and its costs; severity of specific consequences is not evidenced by real cases in this lane." ;
     se:symptoms "New features are consistently prioritized over refactoring, security or performance improvement; code decay sets in.", "Quick fixes land without peer review under corrective, emergency and additive pressure.", "The codebase is more expensive to maintain than it has to be; maintenance burden on engineers keeps rising." .

se:ANT-07G-POINT-ESTIMATE-COMMITMENT a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07G-SYNTHESIS-POINT-ESTIMATE ;
     se:causes "Conflating an estimate (an uncertain prediction supporting a decision) with a target, bid or commitment.", "No comparison of past estimates against actuals, so miscalibration is invisible.", "Stakeholder or contractual pressure for certainty rewards a single confident number.", "Treating estimation as a one-time initiation artifact rather than an iterative, negotiated activity." ;
     se:consequences "Decisions are made on precision that never existed, invalidating their justification.", "Overruns and infeasibility surface late, when correction is most expensive.", "Schedule and cost pressure is redirected into quality shortcuts, undercutting quality planning.", "Trust erodes between engineering and stakeholders as 'the number' is missed." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SYNTHESIS-POINT-ESTIMATE ;
     se:label "Single-point estimate treated as a commitment" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Present estimates with an uncertainty assessment (ranges, sensitivity analysis) as the Code of Ethics clause quoted by SWEBOK KA15 section 8 requires; estimate with multiple techniques and reconcile divergences (KA15 section 8.5, KA9 section 2.3); delay final decisions where estimates diverge (KA15 section 2.6); and close the loop by comparing estimates to actual outcomes (KA15 section 2.7)." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane synthesis grounded in directly inspected SWEBOK KA15 section 8 (estimates inherently uncertain; uncertainty assessment required) and KA9 section 2.3 (estimation error-prone; multiple approaches advised); the counterproductive characterization is the lane's inference (see se:EVD-07G-SYNTHESIS-POINT-ESTIMATE), not a statement made by SWEBOK." ;
     se:symptoms "An early effort, cost or schedule estimate is communicated as one number with no range and no uncertainty assessment.", "Estimates from different techniques or estimators that diverge are averaged or ignored instead of investigated.", "The estimate functions as a promise or target; deviations are treated as failures of execution rather than of prediction.", "The estimate is never revisited when requirements or scope evolve." .

se:ANT-ARC-INCONSISTENT-API-SURFACE a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-ARC-0010 ;
     se:causes "API portions designed or evolved independently without a shared consistency review.", "No external-pattern baseline was adopted when the API surface was defined." ;
     se:consequences "The benefits the curriculum attributes to consistency - being easier to learn and less error-prone - are forfeited." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0010 ;
     se:label "Inconsistent API surface" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Apply API design consistency: align conventions across internal portions of the API and follow common external API patterns, per CS2023 SE-Design KA Core topic 5a." ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope; anti-pattern shape is a synthesis of the curriculum's consistency principle, not a curriculum-named anti-pattern." ;
     se:symptoms "Analogous operations in different portions of an API follow divergent conventions.", "Callers must relearn conventions for each portion of the API and make avoidable mistakes.", "The API ignores common external API patterns that its users already know." .

se:ANT-REQ-IMPLICIT-QUALITY-ATTRIBUTES a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-REQ-SYNTHESIS-QA-DEFERRED ;
     se:causes "Confusing software that works once with software that keeps working under quality constraints.", "No owner or method for capturing quality attributes alongside functional requirements.", "Treating non-functional requirements as separate from the 'real' (functional) requirements." ;
     se:consequences "Quality attributes surface as late, expensive rework in design and implementation.", "Security, usability and performance risks are discovered after structural decisions are fixed.", "Unstated quality expectations diverge between stakeholders, producing validation disputes." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-SYNTHESIS-QA-DEFERRED ;
     se:label "Implicit or deferred quality attributes" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Name quality attributes explicitly as requirements — following the CS2023 placement of non-functional requirements (Quality Attributes) as a core requirements topic with security, usability and performance as examples — and capture them with the same documentation discipline as functional requirements." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-level characterization grounded in the CS2023 SE-Requirements unit (KA Core topics 1 and 4); the counterproductive nature of this approach is a SEK-06A synthesis inference (see se:EVD-REQ-SYNTHESIS-QA-DEFERRED), not a statement made by the CS2023 report." ;
     se:symptoms "Design and implementation decisions lack explicit quality anchors to be checked against.", "Quality concerns such as security, usability and performance surface late as retrofit requests rather than stated requirements.", "The requirement set contains only functional items; quality expectations are assumed to be understood." .

se:ANT-TVV-COVERAGE-THEATER a se:AntiPattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-TVV-0014 ;
     se:causes "Equating test execution with correctness, and validation with acceptance or sign-off.", "Incentives anchored to coverage metrics rather than to testing objectives such as conformance to specification.", "Interpreting one coverage dimension (code coverage) as the whole of test coverage, contrary to the three-dimension framing." ;
     se:consequences "Behavior and environments relevant to defects remain untested despite high coverage numbers.", "False confidence: specification-conformance and fitness-for-purpose problems surface late.", "Testing throughput is consumed by low-value tests that inflate the metric." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0014 ;
     se:label "Coverage theater" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:remediation "Measure coverage across all three dimensions (test matrices, code coverage, environment coverage); select test kinds by explicit testing objectives (usability, reliability, conformance to specification, performance, security); treat coverage versus throughput/latency as an explicit tradeoff; and never read an executed, passing suite as a correctness proof or as acceptance." ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-synthesized anti-pattern over CS2023 SE-Validation coverage/objective/tradeoff topics and NIST's defect-specific regression example; not a named concept in either source." ;
     se:symptoms "A code-coverage percentage is pursued as the primary or sole testing goal.", "A green suite is treated as proof of correctness or as completed validation.", "Test matrices and environment coverage go unexamined while the coverage number rises." .

se:CLM-07A-ADAPTATION-DOCUMENTED a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION ;
     se:applicability "Projects and organizations defining a project life cycle in conformance with a selected model." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Describes generally accepted adaptation activities; it does not prescribe a mandatory procedure or evaluation criteria for the tailoring." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 section 2.8 (printed page 10-8); generally accepted adaptation activities, not a mandated tailoring procedure." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 presents software life cycle adaptation as identifying the relevant characteristics of the software system and the stakeholders' needs, selecting appropriate standards or organization-internal documents, selecting a development strategy/life cycle model, stages and processes, and documenting the decisions and rationale; the adaptation does not require keeping the provided names or including all of the processes." .

se:CLM-07A-ASSESSMENT-EMPIRICAL a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07A-PROCESS-ASSESSMENT ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-ASSESSMENT ;
     se:applicability "Teams assessing or improving software engineering processes, including in Agile life cycles." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section surveys the approaches without comparing their effectiveness empirically within the Guide." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 section 3 (printed pages 10-9 to 10-10); a survey of generally accepted assessment approaches." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 presents software process assessment and improvement approaches — the Shewhart-Deming PDCA paradigm, the Goal Question Metric approach, framework-based methods (CMM, CMMI, the ISO/IEC 33000 series) and Agile retrospectives — as resting on the need for empirical evidence to make decisions such as choosing, assessing or improving a process." .

se:CLM-07A-DEPENDABILITY-COMPOSITE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07A-KA12-SOFTWARE-QUALITY ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA12-DEPENDABILITY ;
     se:applicability "Model-level classification of dependability within the quality lens; the engineering of individual characteristics is out of this lane's scope." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Model-level claim only; reliability and performance engineering detail belongs to SEK-07 lane 07D." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 section 1.4 incl. 1.4.1-1.4.2 (printed pages 12-5 to 12-6); model-level composition of quality characteristics." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 presents system and software dependability as regrouping several related quality characteristics — availability, reliability, maintainability and supportability, safety, and security — and presents software integrity levels as a risk-management method based on project-unique characteristics such as complexity, criticality, risk, safety level, security level, desired performance and reliability." .

se:CLM-07A-ENG-PROCESS-ITERATIVE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA18-ENG-PROCESS ;
     se:applicability "Grounds software engineering process thinking in shared engineering foundations; usable where a foundations anchor for process iteration is needed." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The engineering-process summary is explicitly high-level; the fuller treatment lives in the Software Engineering Economics KA." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 18 introduction and section 1 (printed pages 18-1 to 18-2); engineering-foundations characterization." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA18 defines engineering, per IEEE, as the application of a systematic, disciplined, quantifiable approach to structures, machines, products, systems or processes, states that software engineering is based on skills and knowledge common to all engineering disciplines, and presents the engineering process as necessarily iterative — knowledge gained at any point may be relevant to earlier steps, triggering iteration — across the steps of understanding the real problem, defining selection criteria, identifying all reasonable technically feasible solutions, evaluating them against the criteria, selecting the preferred option, and monitoring the performance of the selected solution because estimates can be wrong." .

se:CLM-07A-LIFECYCLE-CATEGORIES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-STRUCTURE ;
     se:applicability "Reference mapping for classifying software engineering processes within the lifecycle lens; not a claim of exhaustive coverage." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The category enumeration follows the reference the Guide cites (ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017); the normative text itself was not admitted in this corpus (see se:GAP-07A-ISO-NORMATIVE-LIFECYCLE)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 2.1-2.2 (printed pages 10-3 to 10-4); the Guide's process-category taxonomy as presented." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 organizes software life cycle processes into four categories — technical processes; technical management processes; organizational project-enabling processes; and agreement processes — and frames a software system's life cycle as comprising all processes, activities and tasks from ideation to retirement, including production, operation and evolution, as well as acquisition and supply when needed." .

se:CLM-07A-LIFECYCLE-VS-MODEL a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS ;
     se:applicability "Terminology mapping for lifecycle-model discussions grounded in this lane; other sources may use the terms differently." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The model and paradigm characterizations are the Guide's survey of generally accepted knowledge, not a comparative effectiveness ranking." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 2.3-2.5 (printed pages 10-5 to 10-7); professional-BOK definitions and paradigm characterizations." ;
     se:statement "In SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10, a software life cycle model (SLCM) and a life cycle are distinct: a project-specific sequence of activities is created by mapping the activities of a standard onto a selected SLCM, so a life cycle is created in conformance with the life cycle model; named model families include waterfall, V-model, incremental, spiral and Agile, characterized by paradigms such as predictive, iterative, evolutionary and incremental." .

se:CLM-07A-MODELS-STRUCTURE-SE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA11-MODELING-PRINCIPLES ;
     se:applicability "Framing how models and methods relate to lifecycle phases within this lane's views; not evidence that any specific method is effective." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "A scope characterization of the knowledge area, not a comparative or empirical claim." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 11 introduction (printed page 11-1); the KA's own scope characterization." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA11 states that software engineering models and methods impose structure on software engineering to make it systematic, repeatable and ultimately more success-oriented — models provide an approach to problem-solving, a notation and procedures for model construction and analysis, while methods provide an approach to the systematic specification, design, construction, testing and verification of software — and that models and methods vary widely in scope, from addressing a single software life cycle phase to covering the complete life cycle, with the KA focusing on those encompassing multiple phases regardless of the type of life cycle process model." .

se:CLM-07A-NO-UNIVERSAL-PROCESS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07A-KA10-SWE-PROCESS ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-PROCESS-DEFINITION ;
     se:applicability "Bounds every life cycle model adoption and tailoring discussion in this lane; usable by practitioners as the professional-BOK position against universal process mandates." ;
     se:claim_kind "LIMITATION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Reports the Guide's position; it is not an empirical finding about process outcomes." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 1.1-1.2 (printed pages 10-1 to 10-3); generally accepted professional-BOK knowledge, not a uniform prescription." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 states that no ideal process, or set of processes, exists: software processes must be selected, adapted and applied as appropriate for each project and each organizational context, and software engineering process management should be supported by empirical measurement." .

se:CLM-07A-QUALITY-TERM-OVERLOAD a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting quality discussions grounded in this lane; other frameworks (including quality-model standards not admitted here) may define quality differently." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The definitions are attributed to their authors/organizations within the Guide; the claim is not an endorsement of any single definition." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 introduction and section 1 (printed pages 12-1 to 12-4); the KA's own terminology analysis." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 states the term software quality is overloaded — it may refer to the desirable characteristics of software products, to software product quality (the extent a particular product has those characteristics), or to software process quality (the processes, tools and techniques used to achieve those characteristics) — and surveys definitions (conformance to requirements; fitness for use; capability to satisfy stated and implied needs) that share a conformance-to-requirements premise while emphasizing that quality depends on how accurately established requirements represent stakeholder needs, wants and expectations." .

se:CLM-07A-QUALITY-VS-QUALITY-REQS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:applicability "Boundary-keeping between quality-model records and requirement-level records; normative quality-model standard content is not admitted in this lane." ;
     se:claim_kind "DOMAIN_BOUNDARY" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The boundary is drawn for this KA's presentation; quality-model standards (e.g., the SQuaRE series) may draw it differently, and their content is out of scope here." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 introduction and section 1 (printed pages 12-1 to 12-4); terminology boundary as drawn in this KA." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 distinguishes software quality from software quality requirements: quality requirements (\"the -ilities\") are attributes of, or constraints on, functional requirements, while software quality is used in the broad sense of meeting all requirements; software requirements are expected to define the required software quality attributes and influence the measurement methods and acceptance criteria for assessing how the software and its work products achieve desired quality levels." .

se:CLM-07A-STAGES-NOT-SEQUENTIAL a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting lifecycle-stage descriptions within the KA10 lens; guards against reading a stage list as a mandated sequence." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The stage list is a general framework; specific life cycles redefine stages within it." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 section 2.6 (printed pages 10-7 to 10-8); the Guide's general stage model." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10's six general life cycle stages — concept, development, production, utilization, support and retirement — are explicitly not presumed to be sequential: the specification of the life cycle for a software system includes the transitions between the stages, and specific life cycles have specific stages, meaningful to the project's stakeholders, that fit into the general stages." .

se:CLM-07B-CS2023-CODE-SMELLS-BOUNDARY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CODE-SMELLS ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting code-quality discussions within the CS2023 curriculum model; general architecture design remains SEK-06B scope." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The topic line names the category only; it neither defines nor enumerates code smells." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design CS Core topic 4; a curriculum classification statement about where code-quality indications sit, not a definition of any specific smell." ;
     se:statement "Within the CS2023 SE-Design unit, code smells and other indications of code quality are classified as a CS Core topic explicitly distinct from correctness, with a cross-reference to SEC-Engineering." .

se:CLM-07B-CS2023-CODING-STYLE-BEST-PRACTICES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching the CS2023 construction curriculum; the secure-coding subtopic's content detail belongs to SEC-Coding and lane SEK-07C." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum names the practice categories without ranking them or defining the practices' content." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Construction KA Core topics 3-4 with Illustrative Learning Outcomes 3-4; curriculum teaching scope, not a professional-consensus claim." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SE-Construction unit makes coding style (style guides, commenting, naming) a KA Core topic and pairs it with \"best practices\" for coding, naming defensive coding practices, secure coding practices and principles, and use of exception handling mechanisms for robustness and fault tolerance; its outcomes ask students to describe implementation techniques for reliability, efficiency and robustness and to write robust code using exception handling." .

se:CLM-07B-CS2023-TEST-FIRST-PSYCHOLOGICAL a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU ;
     se:applicability "Curriculum design and teaching of introductory construction; the pedagogical rationale is about student motivation, not about defect-density effects." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The rationale is pedagogical; no empirical TDD-effectiveness evidence is cited or claimed." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Construction CS Core topic 1b; a pedagogical rationale stated by the curriculum for students, not an industrial effectiveness claim." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SE-Construction unit presents test-driven development as particularly valuable for students psychologically: identifying challenging inputs for an API (edge cases, corner cases) a priori is easier to engage with constructively under a test-first approach, whereas after implementing, the instinct is often to avoid trying to crash the new creation, and test-first gives the satisfaction of spotting problem cases and watching more tests pass." .

se:CLM-07B-CS2023-TOOLS-ANALYSIS-INTEGRATION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-CS2023-SE-TOOLS-ANALYSIS ;
     se:applicability "Curriculum-side grounding for embedding analysis tooling in the development environment." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum prescribes no specific tools or integration mechanism." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Tools KA Core topic 3 with Illustrative Learning Outcome 4; curriculum expectation about tooling literacy, not a tool-selection standard." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SE-Tools unit names testing tools including static and dynamic analysis tools as a KA Core topic, and its learning outcome 4 asks students to describe how available static and dynamic test tools can be integrated into the software development environment." .

se:CLM-07B-SWEBOK-API-DESIGN-GOALS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-API-DESIGN ;
     se:applicability "Designing and evaluating program/library APIs at code level; not a claim about architectural service boundaries (SEK-06B scope)." ;
     se:claim_kind "NORMATIVE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The goals are stated as guidance; the inspected text cites no effectiveness evidence for them." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 4.1 (printed page 4-10); guide-level design guidance for library/framework APIs, not a certified standard requirement." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 states that an API should always include statements about the program's effects and behaviors (its semantics) besides its signatures, and that API design should make the API easy to learn and memorize, lead to readable code, be difficult to misuse, be easy to extend, be complete and maintain backward compatibility; because APIs usually outlast their implementations, they should be straightforward and stable." .

se:CLM-07B-SWEBOK-CONSTRUCTION-DEFINITION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-CONSTRUCTION-DEFINITION ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK characterization of the construction activity; anchors this lane's subdomain node and its boundaries." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The definition deliberately leaves the design/construction/testing boundary life-cycle dependent; it does not fix one process decomposition." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 Introduction (printed page 4-1); generally accepted KA content, not a universal process prescription." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 defines software construction as the detailed creation and maintenance of software through coding, verification, unit testing, integration testing and debugging; states that much design work is performed during construction; and states that boundaries among design, construction and testing, if any, vary depending on the software life cycle processes used." .

se:CLM-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION-APPROACHES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION ;
     se:applicability "Choosing and characterizing code-level integration strategy; CI delivery-pipeline mechanics beyond this characterization belong to SEK-07E." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The advantages of incremental integration are presented as believed, not measured, in the cited section." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 3.7 (printed pages 4-9 to 4-10); the advantage list is hedged ('thought to offer') and is not an empirical result." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 describes phased integration (also called big bang integration) as delaying integration of component parts until all parts intended for release in a version are complete, presents incremental integration (small pieces written, tested and combined one at a time, usually needing stubs, drivers and mock objects) as thought to offer many advantages over it, and notes continuous integration, with multiple integrations per day automated by a build-and-test pipeline, has been widely adopted in practice." .

se:CLM-07B-SWEBOK-MINIMIZE-COMPLEXITY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-MINIMIZE-COMPLEXITY ;
     se:applicability "Construction of code that people other than a single author must read, test and change." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The working-memory rationale and technique supports are asserted by the Guide without cited studies in the inspected text." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 1.1 (printed page 4-2); a description of generally accepted construction fundamentals, not an empirical effect claim." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 presents minimizing complexity as one of the key goals of software construction, grounded in limited human working memory, applying to essentially every aspect of construction and particularly critical to testing; reduced complexity is achieved by creating simple and readable code rather than clever code, supported by standards, modular design and other specific techniques." .

se:CLM-07B-SWEBOK-QUALITY-TECHNIQUES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-QUALITY-TECHNIQUES ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK scoping of code-level quality work; individual technique depth (for example testing detail) lives in other KAs and lanes." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The enumeration does not rank the techniques or assert that any subset is necessary." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 3.6 (printed pages 4-8 to 4-9); an enumeration of generally accepted techniques, not a comparative effectiveness claim." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 lists unit and integration testing, test-first development, assertions and defensive programming, debugging, inspections, technical reviews including security-oriented reviews, and static analysis as the primary techniques used to ensure construction quality, with the selection depending on the software constructed and the engineers' skill set, and construction quality activities focused on code-adjacent artifacts such as detailed design rather than requirements, high-level designs or plans." .

se:CLM-07C-CS2023-PROTECTION-PRINCIPLES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-CS2023-SEC-FOUNDATIONS ;
     se:applicability "Terminology mapping within CS2023-grounded records; other frameworks may enumerate protection principles differently." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-CS2023-SEC-FOUNDATIONS ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The topic lists principles as examples ('e.g.'); the enumeration is illustrative, not exhaustive." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 Security knowledge area, SEC-Foundations unit, CS Core topics 1, 5 and 6; curriculum terminology scope." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SEC-Foundations unit lists least privilege, open design, fail-safe defaults, defense in depth and zero trust as examples of principles and practices of protection and how they can be implemented, within a security mindset whose crosscutting concepts include confidentiality, integrity, availability, risk assessment and adversarial thinking, and alongside optimization considerations between security, privacy, performance and other design goals." .

se:CLM-07C-CS2023-SECURITY-TRADEOFFS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-CS2023-SEC-ENGINEERING ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching the CS2023 security curriculum; usable by practitioners only as curriculum guidance." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-CS2023-SEC-ENGINEERING ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum names the tradeoff factors without prescribing a decision procedure." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 Security knowledge area, SEC-Engineering unit, CS Core topics 1, 3 and 4; a curriculum teaching expectation, not a professional-consensus claim." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SEC-Engineering unit teaches, as CS Core, engineering tradeoff analysis for system security based on time, cost, risk tolerance, risk acceptance, return on investment and similar factors, within security engineering goals of building systems that remain dependable despite errors, accidents or malicious adversaries." .

se:CLM-07C-NIST-INDEPENDENT-DESIGN-REVIEW a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW2-DESIGN-REVIEW ;
     se:applicability "Design assurance in organizations adopting SSDF-style practices; review depth is organization- and risk-specific." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW2-DESIGN-REVIEW ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The recommendation does not define 'qualified person'; the publication leaves such terms to adopting organizations." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 Section 2, Table 1, practice PW.2 and task PW.2.1; a government recommendation." ;
     se:statement "NIST SSDF v1.1 practice PW.2 recommends reviewing the software design to verify compliance with security requirements and risk information, assigning the review to a qualified person (or people) not involved with the design and/or to automated processes instantiated in the toolchain, so that meeting all security requirements and satisfactorily addressing identified risks is confirmed and enforced, with findings recorded as artifacts." .

se:CLM-07C-NIST-SECURE-BY-DESIGN a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW1-SECURE-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:applicability "Design-stage security work in organizations adopting SSDF-style practices; depth of risk modeling remains risk-based." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW1-SECURE-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The recommendation does not prescribe a modeling notation or a fixed depth of analysis." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 Section 2, Table 1, practice PW.1 and tasks PW.1.1-PW.1.2; a government recommendation, not a mandate." ;
     se:statement "NIST SSDF v1.1 practice PW.1 recommends designing software to meet security requirements and mitigate security risks, describing secure-by-design attention during design as key for improving software security and helpful to development efficiency, naming threat modeling, attack modeling and attack surface mapping as risk modeling forms, and expecting security requirements, risks and design decisions to be tracked with recorded risk responses, mitigation rationales and periodically re-evaluated exceptions, with any requirement relaxation justified by risk-based analysis." .

se:CLM-07C-NIST-SSDF-REQUIREMENTS-CONTINUOUS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-NIST-PO1-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:applicability "Organizations adopting SSDF-style secure development; practice selection remains risk-based per the publication." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PO1-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Exception processes exist within the practice itself; requirements may be waived with recorded, periodically reviewed exceptions.", "Notional implementation examples are illustrative; none is required." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 Section 2, Table 1, practice PO.1 and tasks PO.1.1-PO.1.3; a risk-based government recommendation, not a mandatory sequence." ;
     se:statement "NIST SSDF v1.1 practice PO.1 recommends ensuring that security requirements for software development are known at all times so they can be taken into account throughout the SDLC, drawing on internal sources (policies, business objectives, risk management strategy) and external sources (applicable laws and regulations), maintaining them over time, and communicating them to third-party component suppliers including provenance and integrity verification expectations." .

se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-BY-DESIGN a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK grounding for lifecycle-integrated security; usable as the professional counterpart to the SSDF secure-by-design and curriculum security-mindset records in this lane." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "No effectiveness evidence is claimed by this lane.", "The Guide itself states there are no bulletproof ways to achieve secure software development." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 1; a professional-BOK characterization of a generally accepted belief, not an empirical finding." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA13 reports a generally accepted belief that it is much better to design security into software than to patch it in after the software is developed, and that designing security in requires considering every development life cycle stage — requirements, design, construction and testing — with security also considered during maintenance, where security faults and loopholes are often introduced, and with vulnerabilities also arising from third-party components." .

se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-PROPERTIES-LIFECYCLE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-PROPERTIES ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting lifecycle expectations for security properties within SWEBOK-grounded records." ;
     se:claim_kind "NORMATIVE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-PROPERTIES ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The sections name the properties without operationalizing how to measure or check them." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 sections 1.1-1.2; a professional-BOK 'should' statement, not a compliance requirement." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA13 states that software engineers should define the security properties of their software and maintain them throughout the software development life cycle, characterizing security as a product quality characteristic and information security as preserving confidentiality, integrity and availability, with authenticity, accountability, non-repudiation and reliability possibly also involved." .

se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS-SCOPE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK characterization of the security-requirements activity for the requirements-linkage records in this lane." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The subsection does not prescribe one elicitation or prioritization technique." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 4.1; generally accepted KA content, not a mandated process shape." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA13 characterizes security requirements engineering as elicitation, specification and prioritization informed by threats (illustrated by misuse and abuse cases), threat actors and security risk assessments, holds traceability of security requirements throughout the development process important, notes that the selected life cycle model may impact the order of activities, and expects security requirements to be revisited when the software product is revised." .

se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING-PURPOSES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK characterization of security verification; complements the SEK-06C testing-lane records, which cover test-execution practices PW.7/PW.8 rather than the dual-purpose framing." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The subsection does not compare detection effectiveness across methods." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 4.5; generally accepted KA content, not a comparative effectiveness claim." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA13 assigns security testing two purposes — ensuring that the implemented software meets the security requirements, and verifying that the implementation contains none of the known vulnerabilities — with the former addressable by general testing methods and the latter requiring security-specific methods (static analysis of source or binaries, and authorized dynamic testing such as vulnerability assessment or penetration testing) operated with security expertise and within legal boundaries." .

se:CLM-07D-ERROR-BUDGET-COMPARISON a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-ERROR-BUDGET ;
     se:applicability "Reliability strategy discussions that accept curriculum framing; the curriculum itself does not enumerate the domains." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-ERROR-BUDGET ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The topic poses the comparison and domain mapping; no domain mapping is carried by the anchor." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Reliability KA Core topic 6; curriculum-scope comparative topic, not a verdict that either approach is universally superior." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum treats the choice between the \"error budget\" approach to reliability and the \"error-free\" approach as core reliability knowledge, framing it as a comparison in which the domains where each approach is relevant must be identified." .

se:CLM-07D-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT-DEPENDENCE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT ;
     se:applicability "Choosing failure-response strategies where consequence classes differ; the examples are the curriculum's illustrations, not a strategy taxonomy." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Non-core status means the curriculum does not require coverage in every CS program." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Reliability Non-core topic 8 (with subtopic 8a); elective curriculum scope with illustrative, not exhaustive, examples." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum teaches that fault tolerance is context-dependent — its illustrative contrast is that crashing a flight-critical system is strongly avoided while crashing a data-processing system before corrupt data is written to storage is highly valuable — and lists this under Non-core (elective) fault tolerance techniques and models." .

se:CLM-07D-HIGH-RELIABILITY-DEFECT-MINIMIZATION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-DEFECT-INJECTION-REMOVAL ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching the CS2023 reliability curriculum; usable by practitioners only as curriculum guidance." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-DEFECT-INJECTION-REMOVAL ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum names example levers without comparing their effectiveness." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Reliability KA Core topics 3 and 5; a curriculum teaching expectation, not a professional-consensus claim." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum teaches that because software failures are caused by defects, high reliability pursues a minimum of defects through two levers — injecting fewer defects (better training, education and planning) and removing most injected defects (testing, code review and similar) — and devotes a separate core topic to the defect injection and removal cycle." .

se:CLM-07D-MEASUREMENT-BASED-RELIABILITY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-MEASUREMENT-TELEMETRY ;
     se:applicability "Curriculum-grounded framing of operational reliability analysis; the topic names instrument families, not metric definitions." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-MEASUREMENT-TELEMETRY ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Non-core status limits how far the curriculum itself pushes measurement-based practice." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Reliability Non-core topic 11; elective curriculum scope." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum lists measurement-based analysis of software reliability — exemplified by telemetry, monitoring and alerting, dashboards and release qualification metrics — as its topic for analyzing reliability from operational data (Non-core, elective)." .

se:CLM-07D-RELIABILITY-PROBABILITY-DEFINITION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07D-CS2023-SE-RELIABILITY ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-RELIABILITY-CONCEPT ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting reliability discussions within the CS2023 curriculum model; aligns with SWEBOK's failure-rate measurement name but neither source is treated as definitive." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-RELIABILITY-CONCEPT ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The topic names two quantifications without measurement procedure; this claim does not import one." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE knowledge unit SE-Reliability: Software Reliability, KA Core topic 1; a curriculum-scope characterization, not a professional-standard definition." ;
     se:statement "In the CS2023 curriculum, the concept of reliability is characterized as the probability of failure or the mean time between failures, with faults treated as the cause of failures." .

se:CLM-07D-SOFTWARE-VS-SYSTEM-RELIABILITY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-SYSTEM-RELIABILITY ;
     se:applicability "Distinguishing reliability scopes when reading CS2023-grounded records; the extract carries no elaboration of system-level failure analysis." ;
     se:claim_kind "DOMAIN_BOUNDARY" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-SYSTEM-RELIABILITY ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Topic titles only; the anchor carries no analysis method for system reliability." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Reliability KA Core topics 2 and 4; the distinction is curriculum structure, and this claim does not exceed it." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum keeps software reliability, system reliability and failure behavior as a distinct core topic from the reliability concept itself, and separately makes identifying reliability requirements for different kinds of software a core topic; on that structure, software-level reliability and system-level reliability are curriculum-distinct concerns and reliability requirements are not one-size-fits-all." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-MANAGED a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-SLA ;
     se:applicability "Operations-side availability management; complements the curriculum's reliability concept with the managed-availability loop." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-SLA ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Describes generally accepted activities; no availability-target values are prescribed." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 2.3 (printed page 6-8); generally accepted operations content, not a claim every service must instantiate every element." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 frames service availability and continuity as managed obligations: defined early as nonfunctional requirements, supported by planned, designed, implemented and tested infrastructure, measured and recorded, with unplanned nonavailability investigated and acted on, and reported as indicators against service-level targets, with SLAs documenting the agreed obligations." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-FORECASTING a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-MGMT ;
     se:applicability "Capacity and performance engineering for operated services; the annual-plan and costed-options framing applies where service-level targets exist." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-MGMT ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Rooted in ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1 as relayed by SWEBOK; the ISO standard itself remains registry metadata-only." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 2.4 (printed page 6-8); the Guide relays an ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1 need whose own normative text is not admitted." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6, relaying ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1, frames software capacity management as ensuring capacity at all times for current and future agreed demand: translating business predictions and workload estimates into documented requirements, keeping the reaction to workload or environment variation predictable, capturing and analyzing component and resource-utilization data, and producing (at least annually) a capacity plan with actual performance, expected requirements and costed options for meeting SLA service-level targets." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-EVIDENCE-NOT-HOPE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-MONITOR-EVIDENCE ;
     se:applicability "Operational decision-making about capacity, continuity and availability; the KPI list is the Guide's illustration of admissible evidence." ;
     se:claim_kind "NORMATIVE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-MONITOR-EVIDENCE ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The norm is stated for operations control; it does not define how each KPI is computed." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 4.3 (printed page 6-11); a DevOps-mindset norm stated by the Guide, scoped to software engineering operations control." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 holds, as an operations-control norm, that hope should not be a strategy: engineers should be informed about system quality and operational health with evidence — key performance indicators available to stakeholders in real time, exemplified by production monitoring and product telemetry, actionable V&V results before and after release, end-user activity and resource use, impact analysis results, operational dependencies, unapproved configuration changes, and security and resilience performance capability." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-FAILURE-INEVITABLE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE ;
     se:applicability "Resilience engineering for services where recovery time matters; the claim scopes to what the section states and adds no quantitative recovery targets." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section does not rank backup/failover approaches; 'drastically' is the Guide's qualitative wording." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 2.5 (printed pages 6-8 to 6-9); generally accepted operations content for continuity preparedness." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 states that software engineers should understand that failure is inevitable; that automated failover daemons can reduce recovery time drastically but require applications to include failure-handling logic planned during development; and that preparedness and regular testing of backup, disaster recovery and failover must be rehearsed as the production environment changes." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-PLAN-EARLY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-PERF-RELIABILITY-EARLY ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK characterization of early performance/reliability planning; counterpart anchor for the lane's capacity and performance records." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-PERF-RELIABILITY-EARLY ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Short topic statement; no load-balancing or sizing procedure is prescribed." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 1.6 (printed page 6-6); a description of generally accepted operations knowledge, not a mandated procedure." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 presents performance, reliability and load balancing as concerns that operations engineers plan for early in software projects so that project requirements are met, and observes that demand-adjusting infrastructure services plus DevOps practice let those needs be anticipated early and the supporting services be used and tested during development." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-RELIABILITY-MEASURES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-RELIABILITY-MEASURES ;
     se:applicability "Measurement grounding for reliability records; connects the curriculum's mean-time-between-failures framing to named professional measurements." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-RELIABILITY-MEASURES ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Informal definitions only; the Guide's treatment of measurement error and model assumptions is not carried by this anchor." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 section 2.3.1 (printed pages 12-8 to 12-9); informal measurement characterizations as printed in the Guide." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 names error density, defect density and failure rate (mean time to failure) as three widely used software quality measurements, and states that reliability models built from failure data collected during testing or in service can estimate the probability of future failures and assist decisions about when to stop testing." .

se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-RISK-REDUCTION-TRIO a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-RISK-REDUCTION-TRIO ;
     se:applicability "Reliability and dependability engineering where failure consequences justify systematic risk reduction; the claim does not prescribe integrity-level schemes." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-RISK-REDUCTION-TRIO ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Anchored in a safety-critical/dependability context; extension to ordinary services is applicability judgment, not the source's assertion." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 section 1.4 (printed pages 12-5 to 12-6); engineering-technique content only — the quality-model level is owned by SEK-07A and is not claimed here." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 names three complementary techniques for reducing software failure risk — avoidance, detection and removal, and damage limitation — and states that they impact software functional requirements, performance requirements and development processes, with increasing risk implying more rigorous quality assurance and review, and assurance cases structuring claims, arguments and supporting evidence." .

se:CLM-07E-BUILD-REPRODUCIBILITY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-SCM ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:applicability "Build processes whose outputs must be re-creatable for recovery, testing or maintenance." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide says SCM 'must usually' be able to reproduce releases; the inspected text does not enumerate the exceptional cases." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 section 6.1 (printed page 8-13); generally accepted SCM content, scoped to the inspected section." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 6.1 defines software building as constructing the correct versions of SCIs, using the appropriate configuration data, into a software package for delivery, states that SCM must usually be able to reproduce previous releases for recovery, testing, maintenance or additional release purposes, and that rebuilding an exact copy of a previously built SCI requires supporting tools and associated build instructions to be under SCM control to ensure availability of the correct tool versions." .

se:CLM-07E-BUILD-SYSTEMS-VALUE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-KU ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching build-systems content within the CS2023 curriculum." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum names the value attributes without prescribing a specific build technology or ranking approaches." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Tools KA Core topic 4a; curriculum topic scope." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SE-Tools unit presents build systems through the value of fast, hermetic, reproducible builds and asks students to compare and contrast approaches to building a project." .

se:CLM-07E-CI-ALIGNMENT-SYNTHESIS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:CON-07E-CONTINUOUS-INTEGRATION ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SYNTHESIS-CI-ALIGNMENT ;
     se:applicability "Supporting the dual anchoring of the CI concept and pipeline practice in this lane; not usable as evidence about industry CI practice." ;
     se:claim_kind "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Functional alignment is a lane-level reading; neither source was reviewed by a human for this lane." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "Cross-source lane synthesis over two verified evidence records; explicitly not a sentence in either source, and asserted only for the two inspected anchors." ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07E): the CS2023 SE-Tools learning outcome on CI as ground truth for the state of the team's shared code and the SWEBOK KA8 commit-triggered pipeline description align on CI's function — automated build-plus-test verification of the current shared head/trunk — while remaining different authority levels (curriculum outcome vs professional-BOK process description)." ;
     se:uncertainty "Alignment does not establish equivalence of authority: the curriculum outcome states a learning expectation, the BOK a process description." .

se:CLM-07E-CI-GROUND-TRUTH a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-OUTCOMES ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting the epistemic role the curriculum assigns to CI systems for shared-code state." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "An illustrative outcome states what students should understand, not that CI ground truth is achieved in industry practice." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Tools illustrative learning outcome 5; curriculum expectation scope." ;
     se:statement "A CS2023 illustrative learning outcome for the SE-Tools unit asks students to understand the use of CI systems as a ground-truth for the state of the team's shared code (build and test success)." .

se:CLM-07E-CI-PIPELINE-TRIGGER a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:applicability "Describing CI build pipelines at the fidelity of the inspected professional-BOK section." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The pipeline's 'often combined' steps are presented as common combinations, not mandatory stages." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 section 6.1 (printed page 8-13); the Guide's process-level characterization of CI at the build activity." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 6.1 describes continuous integration as software building performed automatically when changes to CIs are committed to a source control repository: tools running on a local or cloud-based server monitor the source control system and initiate a pipeline of steps undertaken on every commit to a particular branch, configured to retrieve a fresh copy of the complete source code, compile and link, often combined with automated static analysis of coding standards, unit tests, code coverage metrics or documentation extraction, with the resulting artifacts deployed through the release management process." .

se:CLM-07E-CI-PRELIMINARY-VALIDATION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07E-SE-TOOLS-KU ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-KU ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching the CS2023 SE tools curriculum; the curriculum word 'preliminary' bounds the validation depth claimed." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum topic sentence does not define 'basic tests' or a required pipeline structure." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE knowledge unit SE-Tools, KA Core topic 4b; a curriculum teaching characterization, not a professional-consensus claim." ;
     se:statement "In the CS2023 SE-Tools unit, continuous integration is taught as the use of automation and automated tests to do preliminary validation that the current head/trunk revision builds and passes basic tests." .

se:CLM-07E-DEP-RELEASE-DISTINCTION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-OPERATIONS ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:applicability "Delivery-process discussions anchored in the inspected SWEBOK KA6 section." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The definitions are quoted sense-by-sense from one section; SWEBOK KA8 uses 'release' with a different emphasis recorded by se:CLM-07E-SWEBOK-RELEASE-TERM-VARIANCE." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 (printed page 6-10); generally accepted KA content, not a mandate on release governance." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 distinguishes deployment (the installation of a specified version of software to a given environment) from release (making a feature or set of features available to all customers or a segment of customers), and states that once the application platform is deployed in the targeted production environment, the decision to make it available to users (release it) becomes a business decision." .

se:CLM-07E-ENV-SINGLE-SOURCE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS ;
     se:applicability "Multi-environment delivery pipelines whose verification environments must track production." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The recommendation is DevOps-scoped within the Guide's presentation; the lane does not claim it as a universal standard." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.2 (printed pages 6-7 to 6-8); the single-source/automation element is presented as a DevOps recommendation, the coherence requirement as an engineering must." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.2 describes development, testing/QA, preproduction and production environments that must be coherent and synchronized with the production environment to build quality in and reduce release risk, and reports the DevOps recommendation that the creation of all the different environments be automated and built from a single code repository — all environments built from the same code source (single source of truth) — which leads to the concept of infrastructure as code." .

se:CLM-07E-MANUAL-DEPLOY-INEFFICIENT a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:applicability "Assessing manual hand-off deployment processes against the inspected professional-BOK characterization." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The efficiency characterization is the Guide's qualitative statement; this lane adds no empirical evidence for it." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 (printed page 6-10); a characterization in the professional BOK of generally accepted content, given without quantitative evidence in the inspected text." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 characterizes traditional delivery processes — an application ready for deployment transferred from a development team to an operations team, with deployment mostly done manually — as inefficient from both time and quality perspectives, and reports the DevOps call to automate the deployment steps (packaging the code, generating configuration files, restarting servers, configuring servers and databases, installing the software, launching execution, and executing smoke testing)." .

se:CLM-07E-RELEASE-MGMT-SCOPE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-RELEASE-MGMT ;
     se:applicability "Scoping release management activities anchored in the inspected SWEBOK KA8 sections." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The named artifacts (VDD, release notes) reflect the section's framing; different organizations may name or merge these artifacts differently." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 sections 6 and 6.2 (printed pages 8-13 to 8-14); generally accepted release management content as presented in the inspected sections." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 sections 6 and 6.2 frame release as distributing software and related artifacts outside the development activity (including internal releases and customer distribution) and release management as encompassing the identification, packaging and delivery of product elements, with the when-to-release decision affected by the severity of problems addressed and measurements of fault densities of prior releases, the physical contents documented in a version description document, release notes and installation/upgrade instructions included in the package, distribution tracking where needed, integrity mechanisms such as digital signatures possible, and — in continuous delivery — build artifacts combined into an installation package and deployed into an environment for verification or production use." .

se:CLM-07E-RELEASE-PLANNING-TASKS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-RELEASE-PLANNING ;
     se:applicability "Release and version planning anchored in the inspected SWEBOK section." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The task list is presented for the operations role; projects without a distinct operations role must adapt the responsibility, not the tasks." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.1.1 (printed page 6-7); the operations-engineer role's release/version planning activity as presented in the inspected section." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.1.1 states that once individual requests are received and validated, the release or version planning activity requires operations engineers to identify the target availability dates of individual requests, agree on the content of subsequent releases or versions, identify potential conflicts and develop alternatives, assess the risk of a given release and develop a rollback and data migration plan in case problems arise, and inform all stakeholders." .

se:CLM-07E-RELEASE-STRATEGY-FAMILIES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:applicability "Classifying release strategies when reading SWEBOK-anchored delivery records." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "A two-category grouping of the section's own examples; it does not claim exhaustiveness over all industry release techniques." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 (printed page 6-10); a two-family grouping plus named techniques as presented in the inspected section." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 groups strategies for reducing software release risk into two main categories — environment-based release strategies (deploying the new version of the application to a staging environment) and application-based release strategies (toggles such as feature toggles that enable or disable code sections via configuration parameters) — and describes canary release testing as a partial and time-limited deployment of a change whose evaluation helps the operations engineer decide whether to proceed with a complete deployment, with tools able to observe newly started software and automatically roll back changes that fail the validation period." .

se:CLM-07E-ROLLBACK-REHEARSED a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ROLLBACK ;
     se:applicability "Production deployment processes anchored in the inspected SWEBOK section." ;
     se:claim_kind "NORMATIVE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section does not specify rehearsal cadence or rollback-time budgets; applicability to systems where rollback is technically impractical is not discussed in the inspected text." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.3 (printed pages 6-10 to 6-11); a generally accepted requirement of the operations delivery activity as presented in the professional BOK." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 3.3 states that software engineers ensure a newly deployed version and its databases can easily and quickly be rolled back in case the new version causes defects or product degradation, and that a planned and rehearsed rollback is done before a new version of the software is deployed in production; both release strategy categories (environment-based and application-based) can be used to support rollback." .

se:CLM-07E-SBOM-LIBRARY-FOUNDATION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-LIBRARIES ;
     se:applicability "Artifact and dependency inventory practices for delivery anchored in the inspected sections." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The sections name the roles; specific SBOM formats or artifact-store products are outside the inspected text." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 sections 2.5 and 2.6 (printed pages 8-8 to 8-9); generally accepted SCM content as presented in the inspected sections." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 defines the SBOM as a formal record of the details and supply chain relationships of the components used in building software, and identifies software libraries — including the definitive media library containing the release baselines deployable to test, stage and production systems — as key elements that the release management and delivery process depends on, with build-derived binary objects stored with cryptographic hashes used to perform the physical configuration audit." .

se:CLM-07E-SCM-PLANNING-CI-AWARE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-PLANNING-CI ;
     se:applicability "SCM and build planning at team/organization level anchored in the inspected sections." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section assigns planning responsibility without prescribing a specific branching model." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 sections 1.3-1.4 (printed pages 8-3 to 8-5); generally accepted SCM planning content." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 1.3 directs that branching and merging strategies be carefully planned and communicated because they affect many SCM activities, notes that many development approaches use continuous integration — characterized by frequent build-test-deploy cycles — so SCM activities must be planned accordingly, and section 1.4 records that the SCM plan's subordinate procedures define which branching strategies are used, how frequently builds occur and how often automated tests of all kinds are run." .

se:CLM-07E-SWEBOK-RELEASE-TERM-VARIANCE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-RELEASE-MGMT ;
     se:applicability "Anyone consuming both SWEBOK-derived deployment and release records from this lane must disambiguate the sense per record." ;
     se:claim_kind "LIMITATION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Only two sections were inspected; SWEBOK's usage of 'release' elsewhere may include further senses not recorded here." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "Lane-level terminological observation grounded in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 and KA8 section 6; a reading of source usage, not a criticism of either chapter." ;
     se:statement "The term 'release' carries different emphases in the two SWEBOK chapters inspected by this lane: KA6 section 3.2 ties release to making features available to users or customers (a business decision following deployment), while KA8 section 6 ties release to distributing software and related artifacts outside the development activity, including internal releases; the variance is recorded as-is rather than averaged into a single definition." .

se:CLM-07F-CS2023-COMPAT-RELATIONSHIP a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:applicability "Reasoning about interface change and evolution wherever this relational framing applies; other frameworks may define compatibility per artifact." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum asserts the relational framing without exemplar evaluation procedures; the topic names no specific compatibility model." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Refactoring KA Core topic 2 with subtopics 2a-2b; a curriculum-scope relational definition of compatibility." ;
     se:statement "CS2023 teaches that backward compatibility is not a property of a single entity but a property of a relationship, and that it needs to be evaluated in terms of provider plus consumer(s), or with a well-specified model of what forms of compatibility a provider aspires to or promises." .

se:CLM-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-DEBT-REMEDY a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07F-CS2023-SE-REFACTORING ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching the CS2023 refactoring curriculum; the topic names the tool families without ranking them." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Curriculum topic statement; it does not evidence real-world remediation effectiveness." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Refactoring KA Core topic 3 with subtopics 3a-3d; a curriculum teaching expectation, not a professional-consensus claim." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum lists the value of refactoring as a remedy for technical debt as a KA Core topic of the SE-Refactoring unit, alongside standard refactoring patterns (rename, inline, outline, etc.), the use of refactoring tools in IDEs and the application of static-analysis tools to identify code in need of refactoring and to generate changes." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-EVOLUTION-LAWS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-EVOLUTION-LAWS ;
     se:applicability "Framing long-term system evolution wherever the BOK-reported laws are cited as reported research, not as lane-verified empiricism." ;
     se:claim_kind "EMPIRICAL_FINDING" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Secondhand summary; the underlying studies, their systems of origin and their applicability conditions are not available in this lane's sources." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 1.5 as a secondhand report of Lehman's research; the primary publications are not registered sources and the finding is not independently verified by this lane." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 reports that research by Lehman and others over twenty years produced eight laws of software evolution (Continuing Change, Increasing Complexity, Self-Regulation, Invariant Work Rate, Conservation of Familiarity, Continuing Growth, Declining Quality, Feedback System) and concludes that maintenance is evolutionary development: large software programs are never complete, continue to evolve, and grow more complex unless action is taken to reduce the complexity." ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether each named law generalizes beyond the large-system context Lehman studied is not assessable from SWEBOK's summary alone." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-IMPACT-ANALYSIS-ROLE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-IMPACT-ANALYSIS ;
     se:applicability "Maintenance change requests routed through SCM-controlled change processes, in plan-based and Agile life cycles alike." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section names the activity and its outputs; it does not prescribe a specific technique or tool." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.1.3; description of generally accepted activity, not a mandate that every project run a formal analysis of this shape." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 presents impact analysis as assessing the detailed effects of proposed changes on existing software: performed after a change request enters the software configuration management process, it identifies the systems and software products that would be affected, estimates the resources needed and determines the risks involved, with problem-report severity guiding how and when to fix." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-KA8-SCR-PROCESS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-KA8-SCR-PROCESS ;
     se:applicability "Controlled change to configuration items during the software life cycle, including maintenance-phase changes." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:exceptions "The section itself notes an emergency path usually also exists, where changes are implemented first and the change process is performed afterward." ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Change-to-CI rules can differ by industrial sector and company; SWEBOK states its description applies where no specific rules exist." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 sections 3-3.2; generally accepted change-control process description, cited here for its maintenance relevance." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 section 3 presents software configuration change control as a process for determining what changes to make, the authority for approving them, and support for implementing them: the SCR process provides formal procedures for submitting and recording change requests, evaluating potential cost and impact, and accepting, modifying, deferring or rejecting them; the technical evaluation is also known as an impact analysis; authority rests with a configuration control board (or a leader or assigned individual on smaller projects); and implementation includes tracking which SCRs enter particular versions and baselines, closing with audits that verify only approved changes were made." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-CATEGORIES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07F-SOFTWARE-MAINTENANCE ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-CATEGORIES ;
     se:applicability "Classifying maintenance requests wherever a SWEBOK-grounded category scheme is appropriate; additive/perfective distinction is this edition's refinement." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Older SWEBOK editions and other taxonomies use a four-category scheme without additive maintenance; this claim is scoped to v4.0a's report." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 1.6; a BOK-reported standard taxonomy of maintenance request categories." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 states that five categories of software maintenance have been standardized — corrective, preventive, adaptive, additive and perfective — that ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 regroups them as corrections or enhancements, and that the standard also defines a sixth category, emergency maintenance (unscheduled modification to temporarily keep a system operational pending corrective maintenance)." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-DEFINITION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-DEFINITION ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK characterization of what software maintenance is; usable wherever the lane's maintenance records need a definitional anchor." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 objective is reported by SWEBOK; the ISO text was not independently inspected." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 Introduction and section 1.1; a professional-BOK definition citing ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764, whose text is metadata-only in this project." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a defines software maintenance as the totality of activities required to provide cost-effective support for software in operation, performed in pre-delivery and post-delivery stages, and reports the ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 objective of software maintenance as modifying existing software while preserving its integrity." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-REFACTORING-DEFINITION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-REFACTORING ;
     se:applicability "Maintenance-time restructuring of existing code and legacy modernization framing; behavior preservation is the technique's aim." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Behavior preservation is stated as the aim of the technique, not a guarantee that every refactoring preserves behavior in practice." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 4.2; professional-BOK definitions of refactoring and reengineering including the modernization purpose." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 defines refactoring as a reengineering technique that aims to reorganize a program without changing its behavior while improving its internal structure and maintainability, and states that refactoring techniques can be used during maintenance to reduce the technical debt of the codebase before and after code changes; it frames reengineering more broadly as reconstituting software in a new form, often to replace aging legacy software." .

se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-DEFINITION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:applicability "Identification and management framing for technical debt in operational codebases; not tied to a specific debt-quantification method." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The definition quantifies debt as remediation effort; other communities use metaphor-forward definitions this claim does not address." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 sections 2.1.4 and 2.3.1; a cost/effort definition plus measurement guidance at BOK fidelity." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 defines technical debt as the effort required to fix problems that remain in the code when an application is initially released, states that debt generally makes code more expensive to maintain than it has to be and that compromised maintainability creates it, names size, complexity, flaw counts and violations of good architectural design and coding practices as measurable indicators, identifies uncertain reward as the common barrier to addressing it, and requires handling it through root causes rather than in isolation." .

se:CLM-07G-CS2023-DISTRIBUTED-TEAM-RISK a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-CS2023-TEAMWORK-PROCESSES ;
     se:applicability "Curriculum-grounded awareness of team-distribution risks; the topic names no risk-management procedure." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "KA Core for the SE knowledge area; programs delivering the SE area are expected to cover it, others need not." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Teamwork KA Core topic 8; curriculum scope; team-structure risk specifically, not general engineering risk management." ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 curriculum places the risks associated with physical, distributed, hybrid and virtual teams — including communication, perception, structure, points of failure, mitigation and recovery — in the Teamwork unit's KA Core (topic 8)." .

se:CLM-07G-CS2023-ESTIMATION-DIFFICULT a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-CS2023-REQ-EFFORT ;
     se:applicability "Curriculum-scope statement about estimation difficulty; not a professional-BOK or empirical accuracy claim." ;
     se:claim_kind "LIMITATION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Non-core (elective) status; a statement about student estimation, not a measurement of industry estimation accuracy." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Requirements Non-core topic 9 (subtopics 9a-9c); curriculum pedagogy scope." ;
     se:statement "CS2023's SE-Requirements unit carries effort estimation as a Non-core (elective) topic and characterizes it as quite difficult — students are likely to be way off in many cases — teaching it through learning estimation techniques and through practicing estimation while comparing the estimates to how long the tasks actually take." .

se:CLM-07G-CS2023-TEAM-ESTIMATION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-CS2023-TEAMWORK-PROCESSES ;
     se:applicability "Reading or teaching the CS2023 SE curriculum; the topic names no estimation technique." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Curriculum topic enumeration only; CS2023 does not prescribe how teams should estimate." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Teamwork CS Core topic 5; a curriculum teaching expectation, not a professional-consensus claim." ;
     se:statement "In the CS2023 curriculum, the Teamwork knowledge unit of the Software Engineering area carries team processes — responsibilities for tasks, effort estimation, meeting structure and work schedule — as a CS Core topic (topic 5)." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-DECISION-PROCESS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS ;
     se:applicability "Engineering trade-off and investment decisions from pre-project through end-of-life; technique families apply per assignability of probabilities." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The process is a structuring aid, not an automatic decision-maker; the section notes steps can overlap, iterate and reorder in practice." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 15 section 2 (printed pages 15-5 to 15-7); generally accepted KA content presented as a process, fluid and iterative in practice by the section's own caveat." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 2 presents engineering decision-making as a process of understanding the real problem, identifying all reasonable technically feasible solutions, defining the selection criteria, evaluating each alternative against those criteria on the same basis for comparison and planning horizon, selecting the preferred alternative (with decision-making-under- risk techniques when probabilities can be assigned and decision-making-under-uncertainty techniques when they cannot), and monitoring the performance of the selected alternative; the time, effort and care spent should scale with the consequences of a wrong decision, and estimate inaccuracy is addressed with ranges of estimates, sensitivity analysis and delaying final decisions." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-ESTIMATES-UNCERTAIN a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION ;
     se:applicability "Any estimate-backed engineering decision; the uncertainty-assessment obligation is quoted professional-ethics guidance." ;
     se:claim_kind "LIMITATION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The clause number and wording are as quoted by SWEBOK; the Code of Ethics text was not separately consulted by this lane." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 15 section 8 (printed pages 15-13 to 15-14); a limitation claim about estimation itself, including the Guide's quotation of the Code of Ethics (not an independent inspection of the Code)." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 8 states that an estimate analytically predicts a quantity; that software engineers estimate in order to make decisions when critical quantities are unknown, so that behind every estimate is one or more decisions; that all estimates are inherently uncertain because there is a nonzero probability the actual outcome will differ; and that estimates need not be perfect, only good enough to lead the decision-maker to the right decision. The section also quotes Software Engineering Code of Ethics clause 3.09 as requiring realistic quantitative estimates of cost, scheduling, personnel, quality and outcomes together with an uncertainty assessment of these estimates." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MEASUREMENT-INFORMED a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-INTRO ;
     se:applicability "Management of software engineering work where decisions can be informed by qualitative or quantitative measurement." ;
     se:claim_kind "NORMATIVE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide asserts rather than evidences the principle in the inspected sections; no empirical support is cited by this lane." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 Introduction (printed page 9-4); the Guide's own normative framing, not an empirical finding." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9's introduction presents measurement-informed management as a basic principle of any true engineering discipline, asserting that management without measurement (qualitative and quantitative) suggests a lack of discipline, that measurement without management suggests a lack of purpose or context, and that software engineers must use both measurement and management to be effective." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MEASUREMENT-PROCESS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-MEASUREMENT ;
     se:applicability "Designing or auditing measurement programs at project, process or work-product level." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 is not a registered source; the claim's authority is SWEBOK's summary, and the standard's own requirements are out of reach for this lane." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 section 6 (printed pages 9-14 to 9-16); the Guide's characterization of the ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 process, not the standard's normative text." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 6, presented as following ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939, describes a software engineering measurement process in four parts: establish and sustain measurement commitment (measurement requirements guided by organizational objectives, defined scope, formal commitment and committed resources); plan the measurement process (characterize the organizational unit, identify information needs derived from its goals, constraints, risks and problems, select measures with clear links to those needs against criteria such as cost of collection and degree of process disruption, define collection/analysis/reporting procedures and evaluation criteria); perform the process (integrate procedures into the measured processes, collect, verify and store data, communicate results); and evaluate the measurement process and its information products against the specified criteria, recording lessons learned." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MULTI-ESTIMATE-RECONCILE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-ESTIMATION ;
     se:applicability "Effort, schedule and cost estimation for software projects; the predictive/adaptive scheduling differences noted by the section apply per SDLC." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section does not rank the named approaches by accuracy or prescribe how to reconcile divergent estimates." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 section 2.3 (printed pages 9-8 to 9-9); generally accepted KA content, not a mandate of specific techniques on every project." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.3 characterizes estimating costs for software projects as error-prone — effort depends almost entirely on human elements and on dynamic factors such as changing and emergent requirements — and advises that software project managers use multiple estimation approaches and reconcile the differences among the estimates, combining (where data is available) calibrated estimation models based on historical size and effort data with bottom-up estimates from those who will accomplish the work, and negotiating estimates iteratively with affected stakeholders until consensus is reached." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-QUALITY-PLANNING a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:applicability "Quality planning for software projects; the quality-attribute enumeration in the section is illustrative, not a taxonomy." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section frames project quality management via the PMBOK Guide's definition, cited here only as SWEBOK's secondary characterization." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 section 2.6 (printed pages 9-9 to 9-10); the planning-side quality-management direction, explicitly deferring process depth to the Software Quality KA (not inspected by this lane)." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.6 directs that a software project's quality requirements, and those of its associated work products, be identified — perhaps both quantitatively and qualitatively — that thresholds for acceptable quality measurements be set for each software quality requirement based on stakeholder needs and expectations, and that procedures concerned with ongoing software quality assurance and quality improvement, with verifying and validating the deliverable product (for example technical reviews, inspections or demonstrations of completed functionality), be specified during quality planning." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-RISK-LIFECYCLE a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:applicability "Software project risk management across SDLCs; named assessment methods (expert judgment, historical data, decision trees, process simulations) are examples, not an exhaustive set." ;
     se:claim_kind "PRACTICE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section names risk-management activities without prescribing a register format or a fixed cadence." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 section 2.5 (printed pages 9-9 to 9-10); generally accepted KA content." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.5 presents risk management as identifying risk factors, analyzing the probability and potential impact of each factor, prioritizing factors, and developing mitigation strategies to reduce the probability of a negative event and minimize its impact, with the resulting data (often a risk register) representing the project risk profile; it further states that risk management should be done not only at the beginning of a project but at periodic intervals throughout the project life cycle, and that particular attention should be paid to managing risks related to software quality requirements such as safety or security." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-DEFINITION a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-INTRO ;
     se:applicability "Interpreting the scope of software engineering management records in this lane; other bodies may delimit the discipline differently." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The definition is the Guide's; adopting it here does not assert industry-wide terminological consensus." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 Introduction (printed pages 9-1 and 9-4); a professional-BOK definition claim, not a regulatory definition." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 defines software engineering management as the collection of work activities involved with planning, estimating, measuring, controlling, coordinating, leading and managing risk factors for a software project so that software products and engineering services are delivered efficiently, effectively and to the stakeholders' benefit, and treats project management and measurement management together in one knowledge area because of their close relationship." .

se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-VALUE-BASED a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-VALUE ;
     se:applicability "Governance framing for engineering trade-off decisions across the software product life cycle." ;
     se:claim_kind "NORMATIVE_CLAIM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "A position statement of the Guide; the lane does not adopt its implied criticism of value-neutral engineering as fact." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 15 Introduction (printed pages 15-1 to 15-2); the KA's stated position, not an empirical finding." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 takes the position that software engineering must be value-based — neutral or negative value from an organization's software investment is not sustainable — framing engineering economics as the science of choice and engineering as balancing what is technically feasible against what is economically acceptable, stating that apparently easy technical decisions such as reusing a preexisting library versus developing one's own can have serious business-viability implications, and requiring that decisions also consider unquantifiable criteria alongside monetary ones." .

