     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Validation KA Core topics 8c/8d and illustrative learning outcomes 14/15 (curriculum authority only)." ;
     se:statement "CS2023 presents pre-commit and post-commit testing as alternatives with distinct roles, pros and cons, and pairs them with an explicit tradeoff between test coverage and the throughput/latency of testing." .

se:CLM-TVV-SWEBOK-FAULT-FAILURE-LIMIT a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-TVV-SWEBOK-FAULTS-ORACLES-LIMITS ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK grounding for the lane's testing-limitation records; complements the curriculum and secure-development sources without replacing them." ;
     se:claim_kind "DEFINITION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-SWEBOK-FAULTS-ORACLES-LIMITS ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The presence-not-absence limitation is a theory-level statement; it does not itself quantify the residual risk of a passing suite." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 5 section 1 (1.1, 1.2.7, 1.2.8; printed pages 5-3 to 5-5); generally accepted KA content, not a claim that any specific test suite attains a measured confidence level." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA5 distinguishes faults (causes) from failures (observed effects), identifies oracles as the agents that decide test verdicts, and reports testing theory's limitation that testing can show the presence of defects but not their absence." .

se:CLM-TVV-SWEBOK-LEVELS-NO-PROCESS a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-TVV-SWEBOK-LEVELS-OBJECTIVES ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK counterpart to the curriculum's test-level and V&V records in this lane." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-SWEBOK-LEVELS-OBJECTIVES ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Target-based classification only; the same chapter's objective-based test types are outside this locator." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 5 section 2 (printed pages 5-6 to 5-7); generally accepted KA content, not a mandated stage-gate process." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA5 distinguishes the unit, integration, system and acceptance test stages by the target of the test, states that the four stages imply no particular development process and none is more important than the others, and recommends stating test objectives in precise quantitative terms to support measurement and control." .

se:CLM-TVV-SWEBOK-TECHNIQUE-CLASSES a se:KnowledgeClaim ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-TVV-SWEBOK-TECHNIQUE-CLASSES ;
     se:applicability "Professional-BOK counterpart to the curriculum's technique-teaching records in this lane." ;
     se:claim_kind "TAXONOMY_STATEMENT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-SWEBOK-TECHNIQUE-CLASSES ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Class names only; the Guide's per-technique treatments beyond the inspected pages are not asserted here." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:review_status "PENDING" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 5 section 3 (intro, 3.1-3.3 heading; printed pages 5-10 to 5-13); generally accepted KA content, not a prescription of which class to use." ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA5 classifies test techniques by the information available about the system under test: specification-based (black-box) techniques from input/output behavior, structure-based (white-box) techniques from design and code information, and experience-based techniques as a further class, with classification overlap and combination explicitly acknowledged." .

se:CON-07A-ABSTRACTION-LEVELS a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "ABSTRACTION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "Per SWEBOK KA18, abstraction reduces information about a concept, problem or observable phenomenon to focus on the big picture; work proceeds at levels of abstraction connected through encapsulated interfaces, the levels may be organized in hierarchies, and multiple alternate abstractions of the same problem (for example a class diagram, a state chart and a sequence diagram) complement rather than supersede one another." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA18-DESIGN-ABSTRACTION ;
     se:label "Levels of abstraction and alternate abstractions" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA18 sections 3.1-3.4 fidelity, applied here to software engineering modeling." .

se:CON-07A-QUALITY-REQUIREMENT a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "QUALITY_ATTRIBUTE" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "An attribute of, or a constraint on, functional requirements — the \"-ilities\" shorthand — distinguished in SWEBOK KA12 from software quality itself; software requirements are expected to define the required quality attributes and to influence the measurement methods and acceptance criteria for assessing quality." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:label "Software quality requirement (the -ilities)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 fidelity for model-level use; engineering detail of specific attributes stays out of this lane." .

se:CON-07A-SLCM a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A model with which a specific software life cycle is created in conformance: a project-specific sequence of activities is produced by mapping the activities of a standard onto the selected model. SWEBOK KA10 names waterfall, V-model, incremental, spiral and Agile as well-known examples and keeps the model concept distinct from the life cycle itself." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS ;
     se:label "Software life cycle model (SLCM)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 sections 2.3-2.5 fidelity; used as the Guide defines it, not as any single method brand." .

se:CON-07A-SOFTWARE-LIFE-CYCLE a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The evolution of a software system from conception through retirement; in SWEBOK KA10's presentation, the life cycle of a software system comprises all processes, activities and tasks from the ideation of the system to its retirement, including production, operation and evolution, as well as acquisition and supply when needed, considering both the business and the technical needs of stakeholders." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-STRUCTURE ;
     se:label "Software life cycle (SWEBOK KA10 sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 section 2.1 fidelity for this lane; aligned with the Guide's cited general definitions, not with any normative ISO clause." ;
     se:synonyms "software development life cycle" .

se:CON-07A-SOFTWARE-QUALITY a se:Concept ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "An overloaded term spanning, per SWEBOK KA12, the desirable characteristics of software products, software product quality (the extent a particular product has those characteristics), and software process quality (the processes, tools and techniques used to achieve those characteristics); the definitions the KA surveys share a conformance-to-requirements premise." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:label "Software quality (SWEBOK KA12 overloaded sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA12 introduction fidelity; explicitly a multi-sense term, not a single normative definition." .

se:CON-07A-WICKED-PROBLEM a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A problem that can be defined only by solving it, or solving part of it: per the account in SWEBOK KA18 (term attributed to Horst Rittel, paradox explained via McConnell), a wicked problem must be solved once to define it clearly and solved again to create a solution that works; design problems are generally considered wicked problems." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA18-DESIGN-ABSTRACTION ;
     se:label "Wicked problem (engineering design sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA18 section 2.2 fidelity; used for engineering-design foundations, not as a general epistemology claim." .

se:CON-07B-CODE-SMELL a se:Concept ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:SUB-07B-SOFTWARE-CONSTRUCTION ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "In the CS2023 SE-Design curriculum classification, an indication of code quality explicitly distinct from correctness; the curriculum names the category (with a cross-reference to SEC-Engineering) without enumerating specific smells." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CODE-SMELLS ;
     se:label "Code smell (CS2023 curriculum category)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design CS Core topic 4 fidelity; a curriculum category name, not a professional definition or a smell catalogue." .

se:CON-07B-CYCLOMATIC-COMPLEXITY a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "METRIC" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A static-analysis measure of how difficult code is to test and understand, computed as the number of linearly independent paths through a program's source code; SWEBOK KA4 1.1 attributes the tool to Thomas J. McCabe, Sr. (1976) and notes that ideally there should be at least that number of test cases." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-MINIMIZE-COMPLEXITY ;
     se:label "Cyclomatic complexity (SWEBOK KA4 sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 1.1 fidelity; the Guide names the measure and its test-case corollary without prescribing thresholds." .

se:CON-07B-DEFENSIVE-PROGRAMMING a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "Protecting a routine from being broken by invalid inputs, commonly by checking the values of input parameters and deciding how to handle bad inputs, with assertions often used for the checks (SWEBOK KA4 4.4); CS2023 lists defensive coding practices among the \"best practices\" for coding in SE-Construction (KA Core topic 4a)." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU, se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-DBC-ERROR ;
     se:label "Defensive programming" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Definition grounded at SWEBOK KA4 4.4 fidelity, with the CS2023 curriculum placement recorded as a corroborating placement, not a definition source." .

se:CON-07B-DESIGN-BY-CONTRACT a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A development approach in which preconditions and postconditions are included for each routine, so that each routine or class forms a contract with the rest of the program that precisely specifies the routine's semantics; SWEBOK KA4 4.4 states it is thought to improve the quality of software construction." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-DBC-ERROR ;
     se:label "Design by contract" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 4.4 fidelity; the quality-effect claim is hedged in the source and stays hedged here." ;
     se:synonyms "contract programming" .

se:CON-07C-SECURITY-PATTERN a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "ABSTRACTION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A description of a particular recurring security problem that arises in a specific context together with a well-proven generic solution to it, per SWEBOK v4.0a KA13 section 4.3." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-PATTERNS ;
     se:label "Security pattern" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Professional-BOK definition at SWEBOK KA13 4.3 fidelity; specific pattern catalogs are outside this lane's admitted content." .

se:CON-07C-SECURITY-REQUIREMENT a se:Concept ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07C-NIST-PO1-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07C-NIST-SSDF-REQUIREMENTS-CONTINUOUS, se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS-SCOPE ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A requirement on software or on its development that originates in security objectives — organization policies, business objectives and risk management strategy, applicable laws and regulations, or threat and risk analysis — and that both SWEBOK KA13 4.1 and SSDF PO.1 expect to be elicited, specified, prioritized, documented, maintained over time and kept traceable rather than assumed." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PO1-REQUIREMENTS, se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:label "Security requirement (SWEBOK/SSDF sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at the intersection of SWEBOK KA13 section 4.1 and SSDF practice PO.1 fidelity; not asserted as a definition from any single authority." .

se:CON-07C-THREAT-MODEL a se:Concept ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW1-SECURE-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A risk-modeling artifact that illustrates how a system is being attacked or otherwise assesses the security risk facing the software; SSDF PW.1.1 treats threat modeling, attack modeling and attack surface mapping as forms of risk modeling whose results feed the design and architecture mitigations, and SWEBOK KA13 4.2 presents threat modeling as the activity by which developers specify a security design for the mitigation." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW1-SECURE-BY-DESIGN, se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-DESIGN ;
     se:label "Threat model" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined from SWEBOK KA13 4.2 and SSDF PW.1.1 as a design-feeding risk model; no specific notation is asserted." ;
     se:synonyms "attack model", "attack surface mapping" .

se:CON-07D-ERROR-BUDGET a se:Concept ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "One of the two reliability approaches CS2023 asks students to compare: the \"error budget\" approach to reliability, contrasted in the curriculum with the \"error-free\" approach, with the relevance of each to be identified per domain." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-ERROR-BUDGET ;
     se:label "Error budget (reliability approach)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined only as far as the CS2023 SE-Reliability KA Core topic 6 sentence goes; the curriculum names the two approaches without defining budget mechanics." .

se:CON-07D-FAULT-TOLERANCE a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The capability of software to keep operating acceptably in the presence of faults, realized through fault tolerance techniques and models; CS2023 teaches that the tolerated response is context-dependent (a crash may be strongly avoided in a flight-critical system yet highly valuable in a data-processing system before corrupt data is written), and SWEBOK v4.0a requires failure-handling logic to be planned during development for automated failover to work." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT, se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE ;
     se:label "Fault tolerance (context-dependent response)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 Non-core topic 8 (with 8a) plus SWEBOK KA6 section 2.5 scope; not a survey of fault-tolerance technique families." .

se:CON-07D-SERVICE-LEVEL-AGREEMENT a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The agreed, documented statement of the level of service an operations provider owes its customers; per SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 it is the instrument that clarifies the full range of operations service obligations, while the service-level management process monitors the agreed level of service including workload characteristics, performance and availability trend information." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-SLA ;
     se:label "Service-Level Agreement (SLA)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.3 fidelity (operations-services context); other communities may define SLA variants this anchor does not cover." ;
     se:synonyms "SLA" .

se:CON-07D-SOFTWARE-RELIABILITY a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "QUALITY_ATTRIBUTE" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The reliability of software characterized quantitatively: CS2023's reliability topic frames it as the probability of failure or the mean time between failures, with faults as the cause of failures; SWEBOK v4.0a's quality-measurement section names failure rate (mean time to failure) among the widely used quality measurements." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-RELIABILITY-CONCEPT, se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-RELIABILITY-MEASURES ;
     se:label "Software Reliability (failure-quantified)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Anchored to the CS2023 SE-Reliability topic 1 quantifications and SWEBOK KA12 2.3.1 measurement names; not asserted as a complete professional definition of reliability." ;
     se:synonyms "failure rate" .

se:CON-07E-CONTINUOUS-INTEGRATION a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Teams with a shared source-control repository and automated build capability." ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A working practice in which changes committed to a shared source-control repository automatically trigger building the software and running automated checks: SWEBOK KA8 6.1 describes tools monitoring the repository that initiate a pipeline retrieving a fresh copy of the complete source, compiling and linking, often combined with automated static analysis, unit tests and coverage metrics; CS2023 SE-Tools characterizes CI as the use of automation and automated tests to do preliminary validation that the current head/trunk revision builds and passes basic tests." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-KU, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:label "Continuous Integration (SWEBOK KA8 / CS2023 dual-anchored sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at the fidelity of the two inspected anchors (SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 6.1 and CS2023 SE-Tools KA Core topic 4b); not asserted as a single canonical industry definition." ;
     se:synonyms "CI" .

se:CON-07E-DEPLOYMENT a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Environment-oriented delivery discussions anchored in the inspected SWEBOK sections." ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The installation of a specified version of software to a given environment, such as deploying code into an integration test environment or deploying code in production, as defined in SWEBOK KA6 section 3.2." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:label "Deployment (SWEBOK KA6 sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 terminology scope; distinct from the release concept recorded separately." .

se:CON-07E-RELEASE a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Reading delivery-related SWEBOK-derived records; sense must be disambiguated per context." ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The term release carries two recorded senses in the SWEBOK sections this lane inspected: KA6 section 3.2 — making a feature or set of features available to all customers or a segment of customers, which becomes a business decision once the platform is deployed in the targeted production environment; and KA8 section 6 — distributing software and related artifacts outside the development activity, including internal releases and distribution to customers. Both senses are recorded side by side; this lane does not merge them into one definition." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-RELEASE-MGMT ;
     se:label "Release (two recorded SWEBOK senses)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Terminological variance preserved across SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 and KA8 section 6; the variance itself is asserted by se:CLM-07E-SWEBOK-RELEASE-TERM-VARIANCE." .

se:CON-07E-REPRODUCIBLE-BUILD a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Build and release processes that must re-create specific past versions." ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A build outcome in which the correct versions of the software configuration items are constructed with the appropriate configuration data such that a specific version can be re-created: SWEBOK KA8 6.1 requires the ability to reproduce previous releases and places supporting tools and build instructions under SCM control so an exact copy of a previously built item can be rebuilt; CS2023 SE-Tools names reproducible builds/configuration as a core SCM-and-version-control topic." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-KU, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:label "Reproducible build" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 section 6.1 and CS2023 SE-Tools CS Core topic 1a; describes the SCM-side re-creation capability, not bit-level build determinism standards." .

se:CON-07E-SBOM a se:Concept ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:CON-07E-REPRODUCIBLE-BUILD ;
     se:applicability "Build-output inventory and supply-chain traceability discussions anchored in the inspected sections." ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A formal record containing the details and supply chain relationships of the configuration items (components: source code, libraries, modules and other artifacts, open source or proprietary) used in building software, as defined in SWEBOK KA8 section 2.5; KA8 section 6.1 names the SBOM listing the artifacts included in the build as an important configuration management output." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-LIBRARIES ;
     se:label "Software Bill of Materials (SBOM)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 sections 2.5 and 6.1 terminology scope; format standards for SBOMs are outside this lane's inspected sections." ;
     se:synonyms "SBOM" .

se:CON-07F-HYRUMS-LAW a se:Concept ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:PAT-07F-DUAL-API-LARGE-SCALE-REFACTORING ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A named law (the Law of Implicit Interfaces) that the CS2023 SE-Refactoring unit lists as its first KA Core topic; the unit's illustrative learning outcomes operationalize it as identifying both explicit and implicit behavior of an interface and identifying potential risks from the law. The lane records the curriculum topic and outcome, not an independent statement of the law's content." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:label "Hyrum's Law (Law of Implicit Interfaces, CS2023 curriculum sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 curriculum-scope entry point; the full statement of the law is not contained in the inspected topic text and is not asserted by this lane." .

se:CON-07F-MAINTAINABILITY a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT ;
     se:concept_kind "QUALITY_ATTRIBUTE" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The capability of the software product to be modified, per ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 as reported by SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.1.4, where modifications include corrections, improvements, adaptation to environment changes, and changes in requirements and functional specifications; SWEBOK adds that it should be specified, reviewed and controlled during development to reduce maintenance costs." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT ;
     se:label "Maintainability (ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 via SWEBOK)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Quality-attribute sense at SWEBOK KA7 fidelity; the ISO standard text is metadata-only and the definition is cited as SWEBOK reports it." .

se:CON-07F-REFACTORING a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-REFACTORING ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A software reengineering technique that aims to reorganize a program without changing its behavior, per SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 4.2, seeking to improve the internal structure and maintainability of software; CS2023 additionally names standard refactoring patterns, IDE refactoring tools and static-analysis support among its curriculum topics and describes refactoring as a remedy for technical debt." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-REFACTORING ;
     se:label "Refactoring" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Behavior-preserving restructuring at SWEBOK KA7 fidelity with CS2023 curriculum complements; the definition claims behavior preservation as the technique's aim, not that every real refactoring achieves it." .

se:CON-07F-SOFTWARE-MAINTENANCE a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-DEFINITION ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The totality of activities required to provide cost-effective support for software in operation, per SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7, spanning pre-delivery preparation (planning for post-delivery operations, maintainability and transition logistics) and post-delivery activities (surveillance, modification, training, help-desk operation), with the reported ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 objective of modifying existing software while preserving its integrity." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-DEFINITION ;
     se:label "Software maintenance (SWEBOK KA7 sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at SWEBOK v4.0a KA7 fidelity as directly inspected; the ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 attribution is reported, not ingested." .

se:CON-07F-TECHNICAL-DEBT a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The effort required to fix problems that remain in the code when an application is initially released, per SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.3.1, which also states debt generally makes code more expensive to maintain than it has to be; KA7 section 2.1.4 adds that compromised maintainability creates technical debt when quick, under-considered and often unreviewed maintenance solutions accumulate." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:label "Technical debt (SWEBOK KA7 sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK v4.0a KA7 fidelity; a cost/effort framing of debt, not a particular commercial debt-tracking methodology." .

se:CON-07G-EFFORT-ESTIMATE a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "An analytical prediction of some quantity — such as a project's size, cost or schedule — made because the actual value cannot be precisely known until the work is done; inherently uncertain and existing to support decisions rather than to be right in itself." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION ;
     se:label "Estimate (SWEBOK KA15 section 8 sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at the fidelity of SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 8; not asserted as a universal metrological definition." ;
     se:synonyms "cost estimate", "effort estimate" .

se:CON-07G-INFORMATION-NEED a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-MEASUREMENT ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "In the SWEBOK KA9 measurement process (following the Guide's characterization of ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939), the needs, derived from an organizational unit's goals, constraints, risks and problems, that measures are selected to address; candidate measures must keep clear links to the identified, prioritized and communicated needs." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-MEASUREMENT ;
     se:label "Information need (measurement process)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Scoped to SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 6.2 as the Guide's characterization of the ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 concept; the standard's own definitions are not cited." .

se:CON-07G-RISK-REGISTER a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "A document used as a risk-management tool: a repository for all identified risks plus additional information about each, usable among other things to fulfill regulatory compliance, whose data represents the project risk profile." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:label "Risk register" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "As characterized in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.5; template and field structure are context-dependent and not specified by the inspected text." .

se:CON-07G-RISK-VS-UNCERTAINTY a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:concept_kind "BOUNDARY_CONCEPT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:definition "The SWEBOK KA9 distinction that uncertainty results from a lack of information, while risk is the effect of uncertainty on objectives and can carry negative (threats) or positive (opportunities) consequences; risk management operates on this effect, not on ignorance in general." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:label "Risk versus uncertainty (SWEBOK KA9 section 2.5 distinction)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Boundary concept as stated in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.5; other frameworks may draw the boundary differently." .

se:CON-ARC-COUPLING-COHESION a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Software design education context within the CS2023 curriculum model." ;
     se:concept_kind "PROPERTY" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "A paired design-property topic named in CS2023 SE-Design CS Core topic 1d: coupling concerns the interdependence between software components and cohesion concerns how strongly the elements of one component belong together. The extracted curriculum text lists the topic by name among system design principles without elaborating the definitions, so the property gloss here is a conventional reading, not extracted source text." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0002 ;
     se:label "Coupling and cohesion" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope; name-level curriculum anchor with lane-provided property gloss." .

se:CON-ARC-DESIGN-ABSTRACTION-LEVELS a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Use to keep architecture-level and detailed-design statements at distinct abstraction levels in curriculum-scoped records." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-ARC-0001 ;
     se:concept_kind "BOUNDARY_CONCEPT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "The levels-of-abstraction structure of software design presented in CS2023 SE-Design CS Core topic 1a, exemplified by architectural design versus detailed design. The SE Overview anchors the same span from large design concerns such as software architecture to small-scale design choices like API design. This is a curriculum boundary concept; architecture description as an ISO/IEC/IEEE 42010 concept is not represented and remains a coverage gap." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0001, se:EVD-ARC-0009 ;
     se:label "Design abstraction levels" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope; distinguishes abstraction levels, not standardized architecture-description concepts." .

se:CON-ARC-SEPARATION-OF-CONCERNS a se:Concept ;
     se:applicability "Software design education context within the CS2023 curriculum model." ;
     se:concept_kind "ABSTRACTION" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "A system design concept taught in CS2023 SE-Design: a software system is divided so that distinct parts address distinct concerns. The curriculum lists it among system design principles (CS Core topic 1b) and expects students to adapt a flawed design to better follow it." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0002 ;
     se:label "Separation of concerns" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope; not asserted as professional consensus." .

se:CON-REQ-QUALITY-ATTRIBUTE a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "QUALITY_ATTRIBUTE" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "A requirement concerning a non-functional property of the software being specified; CS2023 calls non-functional requirements Quality Attributes and names security, usability and performance as examples." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-QUALITY-ATTRIBUTES ;
     se:label "Quality Attribute (CS2023 non-functional requirement)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Requirements KA Core topic 4 terminology scope; the term equivalence holds within this curriculum." ;
     se:synonyms "non-functional requirement" .

se:CON-REQ-REQUIREMENTS-ELICITATION a se:Concept ;
     se:concept_kind "TERM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "The activity of obtaining requirements for a product or feature; the CS2023 SE-Requirements unit characterizes it by its sources (users, administrators and support personnel as examples) and its gathering methods (surveys, interviews and behavioral analysis as examples)." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-ELICITATION-SOURCES-METHODS ;
     se:label "Requirements Elicitation (CS2023 curriculum sense)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Defined at CS2023 SE curriculum fidelity for this lane; not asserted as a professional-consensus definition." ;
     se:synonyms "requirements gathering" .

se:CON-TVV-TEST-COVERAGE a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-TVV-0007 ;
     se:applicability "Use when planning test suites or judging coverage claims; a coverage figure must identify which dimension it measures." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-TVV-0006 ;
     se:concept_kind "MECHANISM" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "The extent to which a test suite exercises the subject software. CS2023 SE-Validation topics decompose it into test matrices, code coverage and environment coverage rather than expressing it as a single percentage; the same curriculum pairs coverage with testing throughput/latency as an explicit engineering tradeoff." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0007, se:EVD-TVV-0009 ;
     se:label "Test coverage" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Framing from CS2023 KA Core topics 6b and 8d. Code coverage is one dimension of three, and rising coverage is not treated as a correctness guarantee." .

se:CON-TVV-VERIFICATION-VALIDATION a se:Concept ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-TVV-0003 ;
     se:applicability "Use whenever testing, review or analysis records are classified, to keep conformance-checking separate from fitness-for-purpose." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-TVV-0001 ;
     se:concept_kind "BOUNDARY_CONCEPT" ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "A two-question boundary from the CS2023 SE-Validation unit: verification asks whether the software is being built right, and validation asks whether the right software was built. The two questions target different failure modes (disagreement with a specification versus unsuitability of what was specified) and are answered by different kinds of evidence." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0003, se:EVD-TVV-0004 ;
     se:label "Verification and validation distinction" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Curriculum boundary concept anchored in CS2023 CS Core topic 1 and learning outcome 2. Two conflations are explicitly out of scope of this concept: executing tests, even passing ones, is not treated as establishing correctness; and validation is not equated with user acceptance or sign-off — the curriculum's own test-kind list names \"validation\" as a test kind while its learning-outcome list names \"acceptance\" separately, so the two are not merged here." ;
     se:synonyms "V&V", "verification versus validation" .

se:DEC-07D-CAPACITY-INVESTMENT a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-MGMT ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-FORECASTING ;
     se:constraints "Capacity must exist at all times for current and future agreed demand; reaction to workload variation must be predictable.", "The capacity plan is produced at a suitable frequency (at least annually per the SWEBOK-relayed requirement) and must document costed options." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Costed options for meeting the business requirements (the capacity plan's own currency).", "Current and forecast workload against the agreed service-level targets in the SLA.", "Observed resource-utilization data at an appropriate level." ;
     se:decision_question "When and how should capacity be added or reshaped so the service keeps meeting agreed service-level targets?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-MGMT ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "Add capacity ahead of forecast demand.", "Reshape demand or architecture (sizing and modeling of new/changed services) instead of adding raw capacity.", "Ride current capacity and accept the SLA risk." ;
     se:procedure "Assemble utilization data and workload forecasts; size and model the candidate changes; build the costed options against the SLA targets; recommend the option that achieves the agreed targets at acceptable cost, and record the decision in the recurring capacity plan." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.4 fidelity (relaying ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1); professional-BOK decision aid for services with SLA targets." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Early capacity addition costs money before demand materializes; late addition risks SLA breach when variation arrives." .

se:DEC-07D-ERROR-BUDGET-OR-ERROR-FREE a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-CS2023-ERROR-BUDGET ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07D-ERROR-BUDGET-COMPARISON, se:CLM-07D-HIGH-RELIABILITY-DEFECT-MINIMIZATION ;
     se:constraints "High reliability is pursued by minimizing defects — inject fewer, remove more — wherever the error-free side is chosen.", "The CS2023 curriculum requires the comparison and domain mapping to be made explicitly, not a default to one approach (KA Core topic 6)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Consequence class of failure in the domain (the fault-tolerance context contrast: crash-avoided versus crash-valuable).", "Cost of the defect-minimization levers (training/education/planning for injection; testing/code review for removal).", "Whether a quantified reliability target (probability of failure / mean time between failures) is definable for the service." ;
     se:decision_question "Which reliability approach — error budget or error-free — fits a given software domain, and how much defect-minimization effort should follow from that choice?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-DEFECT-INJECTION-REMOVAL, se:EVD-07D-CS2023-ERROR-BUDGET, se:EVD-07D-CS2023-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "A domain-partitioned combination, where different service classes follow different approaches.", "Error budget approach: tolerate a quantified amount of unreliability and govern against the budget.", "Error-free approach: pursue minimum defects without a tolerated-error allowance." ;
     se:procedure "Classify the domain's failure consequences; determine whether failures can be quantified against a budget or must be driven toward zero; map the domain to the approach whose assumption about tolerable failure matches that classification, following the comparison the CS2023 topic prescribes; then size the two defect-minimization levers accordingly." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured around CS2023 SE-Reliability KA Core topic 6 (comparison framing) with topics 3/5 (defect-minimization levers) and Non-core topic 8a (consequence context); curriculum-scope decision aid." ;
     se:tradeoffs "An error budget makes residual unreliability explicit and governable but requires quantified targets; an error-free stance avoids budgeting failure but concentrates cost in defect minimization." .

se:DEC-07E-RELEASE-STRATEGY a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CLM-07E-RELEASE-STRATEGY-FAMILIES ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07E-RELEASE-PLANNING-TASKS, se:CLM-07E-RELEASE-STRATEGY-FAMILIES, se:CLM-07E-ROLLBACK-REHEARSED ;
     se:constraints "A planned and rehearsed rollback must exist before production deployment (SWEBOK KA6 3.3).", "Once deployed to the targeted production environment, making the feature available to users is a business decision (SWEBOK KA6 3.2).", "Release risk is assessed during release/version planning, together with the rollback and data migration plan (SWEBOK KA6 2.1.1)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Ability to evaluate the change before complete exposure.", "Blast radius if the change misbehaves.", "Infrastructure and environment cost of maintaining the strategy.", "Rollback support (both strategy categories can support rollback per SWEBOK KA6 3.3).", "Speed of feedback to the team." ;
     se:decision_question "Which release strategy should a team use to reduce the risk of deploying a change to production?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-RELEASE-PLANNING, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ROLLBACK ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "Application-based release: ship with feature toggles that enable or disable code sections via configuration", "Canary release: partial, time-limited deployment of the change with an evaluation deciding whether to proceed", "Direct full deployment without a staged strategy", "Environment-based release: deploy the new version to a staging environment before wider release" ;
     se:procedure "Assess the risk of the given release in release/version planning; classify the candidate approaches into the environment-based and application-based families and weigh them against the criteria (evaluation before full exposure, rollback support, blast radius, cost, feedback speed); select the strategy (or a combination); define the evaluation window and observation for staged exposure; define and rehearse the rollback and data migration plan; obtain the business release decision before making features available to users." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 sections 3.2, 3.3 and 2.1.1; a curriculum-free professional-BOK decision aid that structures judgment rather than deciding automatically." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Feature toggles decouple deployment from release but add configuration management burden of their own.", "Staged strategies reduce risk but add environment or configuration complexity and delay full availability." .

se:DEC-07F-DEBT-VERSUS-FEATURES a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07F-SYNTHESIS-DEBT-PRIORITIZATION ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-ORG-PRIORITIES ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-DEFINITION ;
     se:constraints "Debt is only one of several factors contributing to excess unplanned work; team and process issues may dominate.", "Organizational objectives and constraints must be balanced with maintainability and engineering standards to avoid code decay and technical debt." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Alignment of the software architecture with organizational objectives.", "Extent of technical debt (measured with size, complexity, flaw and practice-violation indicators).", "Impact of current quality issues on the business.", "Potential savings from investing in quality enhancement.", "Quality of the current software (measured).", "Urgency triage: not all technical debt is urgent (code quality versus relevance)." ;
     se:decision_question "When should a maintenance organization invest capacity in reducing technical debt (for example refactoring) instead of delivering new features?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SYNTHESIS-DEBT-PRIORITIZATION ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "Invest in debt reduction (refactoring, quality enhancement) now", "Mixed allocation: debt work sized against measured urgency and business impact", "Prioritize new features and defer debt reduction" ;
     se:procedure "Measure the debt indicators; record current quality, debt extent, potential savings and business impact; triage debt items by urgency; compare the measured case for debt reduction against the feature roadmap's business objectives; choose an allocation (debt now, features now, or mixed) and re-measure after the next interval, examining root causes wherever unplanned work stays high. This procedure structures the SWEBOK KA7 2.1.4/2.2.1/2.3.1 guidance; the single-procedure form is a lane synthesis (see se:EVD-07F-SYNTHESIS-DEBT-PRIORITIZATION)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SWEBOK v4.0a KA7 sections 2.1.4, 2.2.1 and 2.3.1; a lane-structured decision aid, not a professional-standard decision procedure." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Debt-first allocation improves future velocity but delays user-visible value; feature-first allocation delivers value now at the risk of code decay.", "Mixed allocation needs measurement discipline to avoid becoming an unprincipled split." .

se:DM-07A-LIFECYCLE-MODEL-SELECTION a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07A-SLCM ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07A-LIFECYCLE-VS-MODEL, se:CLM-07A-NO-UNIVERSAL-PROCESS ;
     se:constraints "Adaptation does not require keeping the standard names or including all processes; decisions and rationale must be documented (KA10 section 2.8).", "No ideal process exists: the choice must fit the project and organizational context (SWEBOK KA10 section 1).", "The tailored life cycle needs metrics/measure definitions yielding realistic estimations and measurements (KA10 section 2.9)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Ability to deliver usable increments within agreed time frames.", "Need for early risk reduction (risk-driven versus document- or code-driven).", "Product size and complexity; organizational context.", "Requirements stability (closed set versus open to change).", "Stakeholder communication cadence and trust." ;
     se:decision_question "Which development life cycle model should a software project conform to, and how should it be tailored?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS, se:EVD-07A-KA10-PROCESS-DEFINITION, se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "A hybrid: a selected model deliberately tailored to the system's stages and processes", "A predictive model (e.g., waterfall or V-model)", "An Agile model", "An evolutionary, risk-driven model (e.g., spiral)", "An incremental or iterative model" ;
     se:procedure "Identify the differential characteristics of the software system and its stakeholders; assess requirements stability and expected change (predictive life cycles assume a closed requirement set; Agile-family models claim openness to change); evaluate the candidate models against the criteria, using the Guide's paradigm characterizations (predictive, iterative, evolutionary, incremental) as comparison axes; select and tailor the stages and processes; record the selection and adaptation rationale; monitor with realistic estimations and measurements and revisit the choice if measurement accuracy proves uncertain." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 sections 1.1 and 2.3-2.9; a professional-BOK decision aid, not a normative selection procedure." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Predictive models fix scope, time and cost early but resist change; incremental and Agile models absorb change but shift effort into iteration coordination and integration; the spiral model adds risk-analysis overhead." .

se:DM-07B-INTEGRATION-STRATEGY a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU, se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION-APPROACHES ;
     se:constraints "Continuous integration requires an automated build-and-test pipeline (SWEBOK 3.7).", "Incremental integration usually requires additional test infrastructure (SWEBOK 3.7)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Availability and cost of test scaffolding (stubs, drivers, mock objects) and automation.", "Earliness of product delivery and customer feedback.", "Error locatability when an integration fails.", "Progress monitoring and feedback timeliness during construction." ;
     se:decision_question "Which integration approach should a construction team use to combine individually constructed components into a system?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU, se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "Continuous integration: frequent integrations per day, automated by a build-and-test pipeline.", "Incremental integration: write and test small pieces, combine one at a time (CS2023 additionally names top-down, bottom-up and sandwich variants for comparison).", "Phased (big bang) integration: defer integration until all parts intended for release are complete." ;
     se:procedure "Inventory the components and their testability; assess what scaffolding and automation exist or can be built; compare the candidate approaches against the criteria for the project context using SWEBOK 3.7's contrast axes and CS2023's compare-and-contrast exercise (top-down, bottom-up, sandwich); then record the chosen strategy together with its scaffolding and interim-version test points." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 3.7 and the CS2023 SE-Construction learning outcome 7 naming top-down/bottom-up/sandwich; a decision aid, not a mandate of one strategy." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Phased integration needs no interim scaffolding but concentrates risk in one late event; incremental and continuous integration distribute risk and feedback but require infrastructure." .

se:DM-07C-DESIGN-REVIEW-DEPTH a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07C-SECURE-DESIGN ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW2-DESIGN-REVIEW ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07C-CS2023-SECURITY-TRADEOFFS, se:CLM-07C-NIST-INDEPENDENT-DESIGN-REVIEW ;
     se:constraints "Any exception to security requirements must be justified and periodically re-evaluated (SSDF PW.1.2).", "Where review is performed, it must confirm meeting all security requirements and satisfactorily addressing identified risks (SSDF PW.2.1)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Cost and availability of qualified reviewers.", "Feasibility of automating requirement and risk checks in the toolchain.", "Security risk level of the software and the data it handles.", "Sensitivity of data and security functionality involved (identification, authentication, access control, credential management).", "Time constraints, risk tolerance, risk acceptance and return on investment (explicit tradeoff analysis)." ;
     se:decision_question "How should a software design be reviewed for security, and how deeply, given its risk context?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-CS2023-SEC-ENGINEERING, se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW2-DESIGN-REVIEW ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "Automated toolchain-enforced design checks", "Combined independent review plus automated enforcement", "Independent qualified-person design review (reviewers not involved with the design)", "No formal design review, as a documented and periodically re-evaluated exception" ;
     se:procedure "Assess the software's security risk profile (data classification and high-risk areas per the PW.1.1 examples), weigh the review options against the criteria using explicit engineering tradeoff analysis (time, cost, risk tolerance, risk acceptance, return on investment, per the CS2023 SEC-Engineering tradeoff topic), select the review depth, record the decision and its rationale, and record the review findings as artifacts." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SSDF practice PW.2 and the CS2023 SEC-Engineering tradeoff topics at their respective authority levels; a decision aid, not an automatic decision." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Independent review brings judgment but costs scarce expert time; automated enforcement scales but only checks what it is configured to check; the combined option costs more upfront and covers both gaps." .

se:DM-07G-ESTIMATION-TECHNIQUE-SELECTION a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07G-EFFORT-ESTIMATE ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-ESTIMATES-UNCERTAIN ;
     se:constraints "Decomposition requires explicit allowances for cross-cutting factors (requirements, integration, testing, documentation work).", "Every estimate is inherently uncertain and must carry an uncertainty assessment (Code of Ethics clause 3.09 as quoted in SWEBOK KA15 section 8).", "Parametric estimation presumes a developed and validated equation over an adequate historical data base." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:criteria "Availability of a suitable analogy with known actual results.", "Availability of accurate historical data (enables calibrated/parametric estimation).", "Availability of estimators or experts.", "Consequences of a wrong decision (higher consequences justify more techniques and effort).", "Need for defensibility of the estimate to third parties.", "Time and effort budget for the estimation activity itself." ;
     se:decision_question "Which estimation technique, or combination of techniques, should produce the estimate for a given quantity and the decision it serves?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION, se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-ESTIMATION ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "Decomposition (bottom-up) over a work breakdown", "Estimation by analogy against a known similar outcome", "Expert judgment alone", "Expert judgment via a group process (for example Wideband Delphi or Planning Poker)", "Multiple techniques with convergence/divergence reconciliation", "Parametric estimation with a calibrated statistical equation" ;
     se:procedure "State the quantity and the decision it serves; inventory the available inputs (experts, analogies with known results, historical data, work breakdown); shortlist the feasible techniques from SWEBOK KA15 section 8.1-8.4; select a single technique only when the consequences of a wrong decision are small and inputs are rich, otherwise estimate with multiple techniques and examine convergence (agreement suggests accuracy; divergence suggests overlooked factors — investigate and reestimate, per section 8.5 and KA9 section 2.3's reconciliation advice); express the result as a range with an uncertainty assessment; record the technique choice and compare against actuals afterward." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 8 (8.1-8.5) plus the multiple-approach reconciliation of KA9 section 2.3; the accuracy ordering among techniques is the Guide's overview characterization, not an independent empirical ranking." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Expert judgment is always available but least accurate; analogy is quicker and more accurate but needs a known analogue; decomposition is thorough but laborious and fails under correlated bottom-level bias; parametric is most accurate and defendable when validated but expensive to develop." .

se:DM-ARC-DESIGN-TRADEOFF-EVALUATION a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-ARC-0005 ;
     se:constraints "Design-tradeoff evaluation is Non-core curriculum content in CS2023.", "The example tradeoff pairs and quality goals are limited to those named in CS2023 SE-Design Non-core topic 13." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:criteria "Efficiency", "Reliability", "Security", "Usability" ;
     se:decision_question "When two design goals conflict (for example efficiency vs reliability or security vs usability), how should a design decision be evaluated and recorded?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0006, se:EVD-ARC-0007 ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:options "Adopt an intermediate design that partially satisfies both goals.", "Prioritize the first quality goal of the conflicting pair.", "Prioritize the second quality goal of the conflicting pair." ;
     se:procedure "Identify the conflicting design goals; state each option's effect on the named criteria; record the chosen tradeoff and its rationale; and record which requirements remain satisfied by the chosen design, per the requirement-traceability competency (KA Core topic 9)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope (Non-core topic 13 with KA Core topic 9); option scaffolding is lane-level structure, not curriculum content." ;
     se:tradeoffs "The curriculum's example pairs are efficiency vs reliability and security vs usability; other pairs are outside the extracted scope." .

se:DM-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-DOC-METHOD a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-REQ-METHODS ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-DOC-METHODS ;
     se:constraints "At least one documentation and structuring method must actually be used (CS2023 topic 1a).", "The chosen method applies uniformly across the requirement set for consistency." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:criteria "How well the method supports design and implementation.", "Stakeholder readability of the resulting specification.", "Strengths and weaknesses of the specific approach.", "Team familiarity with the method." ;
     se:decision_question "Which method should a team use to document and structure the functional requirements of a product or feature?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-REQ-METHODS ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:options "A defined combination of both, or another structured method, applied uniformly", "Use cases", "User stories" ;
     se:procedure "For each candidate method, assess it against the agreed criteria for the product context — the CS2023 curriculum frames the comparison axes as the method's support for design and implementation and its approach-specific strengths and weaknesses — then select one method (or a defined combination) and record the selection rationale with the requirement set." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured around CS2023 SE-Requirements KA Core topic 1 (subtopics 1a-1c); curriculum-scope decision aid, not a professional-standard decision procedure." ;
     se:tradeoffs "A lighter method such as user stories is faster and more readable but less detailed; a heavier method such as full use cases captures interaction detail at higher documentation cost." .

se:DM-TVV-PRE-POST-COMMIT-TESTING a se:DecisionModel ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-TVV-0007 ;
     se:constraints "Available test automation and infrastructure.", "Commit cadence and team workflow." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:criteria "Change risk and blast radius of the affected component.", "Feedback speed for developers.", "Test coverage achieved at each gate.", "Throughput and latency of testing at each gate." ;
     se:decision_question "Should a given automated test run pre-commit, post-commit, or should the suite split across both gates?" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0009 ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:options "Run the full suite post-commit (for example in continuous integration).", "Run the full suite pre-commit as a blocking gate.", "Split: fast, high-value checks pre-commit and broader suites post-commit." ;
     se:procedure "Enumerate the suite's tests and their runtimes; classify tests by coverage contribution and risk sensitivity; compare candidate gate placements against the coverage versus throughput/latency tradeoff; assign gates; revisit the assignment when suite runtime or the risk profile changes." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Decision framing from CS2023 SE-Validation KA Core topics 8c/8d and illustrative learning outcomes 14/15 (curriculum authority only)." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Higher pre-commit coverage slows every change; lower pre-commit coverage delays defect discovery.", "Post-commit breadth preserves commit throughput but postpones feedback." .

se:DOMAIN-SOFTWARE-ENGINEERING a se:Domain ;
     se:boundary_notes "No single source is treated as a complete ontology of the discipline.", "SWEBOK v4.0a identity and registry reconciliation is complete (SEK-02R #325 merged via PR #326); SWEBOK content is cited only where a lane has directly reinspected the located sections, and such records stay PROPOSED/PENDING_REVIEW until human review." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "A bounded engineering discipline concerned with the multi-person, multi-version construction and evolution of software, represented here through curriculum, secure-development and related classification views pending professional-BOK reconciliation." ;
     se:label "Software Engineering" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_exclusions "the entirety of computer science", "unreviewed professional body-of-knowledge claims pending lane-level content admission and review" ;
     se:scope_inclusions "curriculum knowledge-unit classification for software engineering", "secure-development cross-cutting guidance" .

se:EVD-07A-KA10-ASSESSMENT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 section 3 (3.1-3.4), printed pages 10-9 to 10-10; process assessment and improvement approaches." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section surveys the approaches; it does not compare their effectiveness empirically within the Guide." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 228-229; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The PDCA, GQM, framework-based and retrospective approaches and their shared empirical-evidence framing were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA10-ASSESSMENT ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA10 section 3 presents software process assessment and improvement approaches grounded in empirical evidence: the classic Shewhart-Deming plan-do-check-act (PDCA) paradigm, already discussed and applied in the 1950s, described as an opportunity to meet the widely recognized need for empirical evidence to make decisions such as choosing, assessing or improving a process; the Goal Question Metric (GQM) approach, based on Basili's Quality Improvement Paradigm, which sets goals that can be measured, changes something and evaluates the effect of the change; framework-based methods that use a process reference model and a process assessment model — the Guide names CMM, CMMI and the ISO/IEC 33000 series (also known as SPICE); and, for Agile life cycles, iteration retrospectives that analyze what went well and what did not, understand why, and set improvement actions, forming a continuous learning loop." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 2.3-2.5, printed pages 10-5 to 10-7; life cycle model concepts, paradigms and named model families." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide surveys generally accepted model families; it does not rank their effectiveness, and its topic citations are not comprehensive." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 224-226; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The model-versus-life-cycle distinction, the named model families, the paradigm definitions and the Agile misconceptions were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA10 sections 2.3-2.5 distinguish a life cycle from a life cycle model: a project-specific sequence of activities is created by mapping the activities of a standard onto a selected software life cycle model (SLCM), so a life cycle is created in conformance with the model. Examples of well-known life cycle models named for product development are the waterfall model, the V-model, the incremental model, the spiral model and the Agile model. The sections characterize paradigms: predictive life cycles (scope, time and cost determined early; requirements treated as a closed set), iterative life cycles (scope generally determined early while time and cost estimates are routinely modified as understanding increases), evolutionary life cycles (requirements introduced in successive steps), incremental life cycles (deliverable produced through iterations that successively add functionality within a predetermined time frame) and continuous development (frequent releases through automated tools). Waterfall is described as predictive and document-driven with a strict phase order; the spiral model as evolutionary and risk-driven; and Agile as claiming openness to change, with the Guide naming several misconceptions — that Agile is a method in itself, that it is faster because no documents are produced, and that it has a limited or unstructured set of methods and practices." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-STRUCTURE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 2.1-2.2, printed pages 10-3 to 10-4; life cycle definition, process categories and rationale." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The category enumeration follows the reference the Guide cites (ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207:2017); the normative standard text itself was not admitted in this corpus." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 222-224; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The life cycle definition, the four-category process taxonomy and the interrelated-processes rationale were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA10-LIFECYCLE-DEFINITION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA10 defines a life cycle as the evolution of a system, product, service, project or other human-made entity from conception through retirement, and presents the life cycle of a software system as comprising all processes, activities and tasks from the ideation of the system to its retirement, including production, operation and evolution, as well as acquisition and supply when needed. Section 2.1 enumerates four process categories: technical processes; technical management processes; organizational project-enabling processes; and agreement processes. Section 2.2 adds that life cycle processes are interrelated — each process's inputs and outputs may depend on other processes — and that the specification of life cycles is a powerful tool for implementing an engineering approach to the creation, operation and retirement of software systems." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA10-PROCESS-DEFINITION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 1.1-1.2, printed pages 10-1 to 10-3; process fundamentals before the life cycle sections." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide's definitions are surveyed generally accepted knowledge; the section does not prescribe which process any project must use." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF (SHA-256 0c9db4da0b2425c58cf778b99131eb31bb6c188e98b67163a549a1366d96e511) on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 220-222; text extracted with pypdf and paraphrased originally." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The process-definition family, the no-ideal-process statement and the empirical-measurement requirement were confirmed for the lane's process-scope claims." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA10-FUNDAMENTALS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA10 frames an engineering process as a set of interrelated activities that transform one or more inputs into outputs while consuming resources, and defines a process description as covering required inputs, transforming activities and the generated outputs; the output of one process can be an input to another process, and processes may include controls (directives, constraints) and enabling mechanisms (tools, technologies, workforce, infrastructure). The KA states that no ideal process, or set of processes, exists — software processes must be selected, adapted and applied as appropriate for each project and each organizational context — and that software engineering process management should be supported by empirical measurement." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 10 sections 2.6-2.9, printed pages 10-7 to 10-9; stage model, process management levels, adaptation and practical considerations." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The stages and adaptation steps are presented as generally accepted guidance, not as a mandated tailoring procedure." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 226-228; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The six-stage model with non-sequential transitions, the adaptation activity with documented rationale, and the estimation/measurement lessons were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA10-MANAGEMENT-ADAPTATION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA10 section 2.6 lists six general stages of a software system's life cycle — concept, development, production, utilization, support and retirement — and states they are not supposed to be sequential: the specification of the life cycle includes the transitions between stages, and specific life cycles have their own stages, meaningful to the project's stakeholders, that fit into the general ones. Section 2.8 presents software life cycle adaptation as identifying the relevant characteristics of the software system and the stakeholders' needs, selecting appropriate standards or documents internal to the organization, selecting a development strategy/life cycle model, stages and processes, and documenting the decisions and rationale; the adaptation does not require keeping the provided names or including all of the processes. Section 2.9 records practical considerations: estimations and measurements are essential, wrong or uncertain estimations in the context of a life cycle will lead to failure, and working with large processes without producing deliverables along the way increases uncertainty; discussions of predictive versus Agile life cycles should be supported by realistic process and product estimations and measurements." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA11-MODEL-ANALYSIS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 11 section 3 (3.1-3.5), printed pages 11-5 to 11-7; model analysis techniques." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The techniques are named with brief characterizations; the Guide does not prescribe which technique applies to which modeling language." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 236-238; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The five analysis techniques and the stated benefits of traceability were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA11-ANALYSIS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA11 section 3 presents the analysis techniques used so that constructed models are complete, consistent and correct enough to serve their intended purpose for stakeholders: analyzing for completeness (the degree to which all specified requirements have been implemented and verified, checked with tooling, state-space reachability or review); for consistency (the model contains no conflicting requirements, assertions, constraints, functions or component descriptions); for correctness (syntactic correctness in the modeling language and semantic correctness of what is represented); for traceability (mapping and controlling the dependency relationships among work products; the Guide states use of traceability typically improves the management of software work products and software process quality and enables change-impact analysis); and for interaction (examining communications or control-flow relations among model parts, including via simulation)." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA11-MODELING-PRINCIPLES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 11 introduction and sections 1.1-1.2, printed pages 11-1 to 11-3; the modeling foundation for the lane's model-level records." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The KA's scope statement is a characterization of the knowledge area, not evidence that any particular modeling method is effective." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 232-234; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The structure-imposing purpose, the scope range, the three modeling principles and the model-property definitions were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA11-MODELING ;
     se:statement "The SWEBOK v4.0a KA11 introduction states that software engineering models and methods impose structure on software engineering to make it systematic, repeatable and ultimately more success-oriented: models provide an approach to problem-solving, a notation and procedures for model construction and analysis, while methods provide an approach to the systematic specification, design, construction, testing and verification of the end-item software and associated work products. Models and methods vary widely in scope — from addressing a single software life cycle phase to covering the complete software life cycle — and the KA focuses on those that encompass multiple phases regardless of the type of life cycle process model, such as iterative and Agile ones. Section 1.1 gives three general modeling principles: model the essentials (abstract away nonessential information to keep models manageable and useful); provide perspective (views such as structural, behavioral, temporal or organizational, each with its own notation, vocabulary, methods and tools); and enable effective communications. Section 1.2 defines completeness, consistency and correctness as properties of models within the chosen notation and tooling, and describes a model as an abstraction or simplification of a system comprising an aggregation of abstractions that together describe only the selected aspects, perspectives or views needed to make informed decisions." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA12-DEPENDABILITY a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 section 1.4 (incl. 1.4.1-1.4.2), printed pages 12-5 to 12-6; model-level composition of quality characteristics." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Model-level claim only; the engineering of individual characteristics (reliability, performance) is out of this lane's scope (SEK-07 lane 07D)." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 249-250; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The dependability composition, the integrity-level definition and the assurance-case structure were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA12-DEPENDABILITY ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA12 section 1.4 presents software dependability and integrity levels: system and software dependability regroups several related quality characteristics — availability, reliability, maintainability and supportability, safety, and security — as the main quality requirement, aside from basic functionality, where system failure may have severe consequences. An integrity level is defined as a value representing project-unique characteristics (for example complexity, criticality, risk, safety level, security level, desired performance and reliability) that define the importance of the system, software or hardware to the user, and defining integrity levels is presented as a method of risk management. An assurance case is described as a reasoned, auditable artifact created to support the contention that its claims are satisfied, containing claims about properties, arguments that logically link the evidence and assumptions to the claims, and a body of evidence and assumptions." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 12 introduction and section 1 (incl. 1.1-1.2), printed pages 12-1 to 12-4; quality concepts at model level." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Model-level scope only: quality assurance and V&V process detail is out of this lane's scope.", "The definitions are attributed to their authors/organizations within the Guide; recording them is not an endorsement of any single definition." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 245-248; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The three-way term overload, the surveyed definition family, the quality-versus-quality-requirements boundary, the process-quality characteristics and the life-cycle-linked quality planning statements were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA12-QUALITY-CONCEPTS ;
     se:statement "The SWEBOK v4.0a KA12 introduction states the term software quality is overloaded: it may refer to the desirable characteristics of software products, to the extent to which a particular software product has those characteristics (software product quality), or to the processes, tools and techniques used to achieve those characteristics (software process quality). The KA surveys definitions — Phil Crosby's conformance to requirements, Watts Humphrey's fitness for use, IBM's market-driven quality, and definitions under which quality is the capability of a software product to satisfy stated and implied needs under specified conditions, or the degree to which a software product meets established requirements while depending on how accurately those requirements represent stakeholder needs, wants and expectations — noting both share the premise of conformance to requirements. The KA distinguishes software quality from software quality requirements (\"the -ilities\"): quality requirements are attributes of, or constraints on, functional requirements. Software process quality is observable in process characteristics such as efficiency, effectiveness, usability and learnability, and defects in the process will likely show up as defects in the resulting software product. Section 1 adds that software requirements are expected to define the required software quality attributes and influence the measurement methods and acceptance criteria for assessing quality, that software quality should be planned early and assessed at many milestones during the software life cycle, that software quality assurance activities must be adapted to accommodate different life cycles such as Agile software development, and it defines error, defect (fault) and failure as distinct terms per the cited standards; the value-and-costs topic frames cost of software quality as conformance cost (appraisal plus prevention) and nonconformance cost." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA18-DESIGN-ABSTRACTION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 18 sections 2-3 (incl. 2.2 and 3.4), printed pages 18-2 to 18-4; design-as-wicked-problem and alternate-abstraction foundations." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The KA notes engineering design's scope is generally viewed as broader than software design; the lane uses these foundations only for its process/modeling scope." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 367-369; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The life-cycle-cost effect of design, the quality-attribute guidance for design, the wicked-problem characterization and the alternate-abstraction concept were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA18-DESIGN-ABSTRACTION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA18 sections 2-3 present engineering design and abstraction foundations: a product's design will affect or even determine its life cycle costs, and software design is guided by the features to be implemented and the quality attributes to be achieved; in engineering, most problems have many solutions and the focus is finding a feasible solution that best meets the needs presented, economically; and design problems are generally considered wicked problems — per the KA's account, the term coined by Horst Rittel, with the McConnell-explained paradox that a wicked problem can be defined only by solving it, or solving part of it, so it must be solved once to define it clearly and solved again to create a solution that works. Abstraction is described as an indispensable problem-solving technique — the process and result of generalization that reduces information to focus on the big picture; engineers work at levels of abstraction connected through encapsulated interfaces, levels may be organized into hierarchies, and multiple alternate abstractions of the same problem (for example a class diagram, a state chart and a sequence diagram at the same level) complement rather than supersede one another, though keeping alternate abstractions in sync is sometimes difficult." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-KA18-ENG-PROCESS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 18 introduction and section 1, printed pages 18-1 to 18-2; engineering foundations for the lane's process and systems-thinking scope." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section is an explicit high-level summary; the KA refers the fuller discussion to the Software Engineering Economics KA." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 366-367; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The IEEE engineering definition, the common-foundations statement and the six-step iterative engineering process were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA18-ENG-PROCESS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA18 opens with the IEEE definition of engineering as the application of a systematic, disciplined, quantifiable approach to structures, machines, products, systems or processes, and states that as the theory and practice of software engineering mature, it is increasingly apparent the discipline is based on skills and knowledge common to all engineering disciplines. Section 1 summarizes the engineering process, common to all engineering disciplines, as necessarily iterative — knowledge gained at any point may be relevant to earlier steps, triggering iteration — across the steps: understand the real problem (using root cause analysis techniques to discover the underlying problem needing a solution, because the problem that needs to be solved is not always the problem engineers are asked to solve); define the selection criteria; identify all reasonable, technically feasible solutions (the best solution is rarely the first that comes to mind); evaluate each solution against the selection criteria; select the preferred option; and monitor the performance of the selected solution, because the process necessarily depends on estimates and those estimates can be wrong." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07A-SYNTHESIS-UNIVERSAL-LIFECYCLE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "Cross-section inference combining verified KA10 statements (sections 1.1, 2.3-2.5 and 2.9); lane-marked synthesis supporting the anti-pattern record." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical case evidence in this lane tests the anti-pattern's consequences.", "Synthesis inference, not a direct SWEBOK statement." ;
     se:method "Lane-level combination of three verified evidence records from the same knowledge area (se:EVD-07A-KA10-PROCESS-DEFINITION, se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS, se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Provides the inference trail motivating se:ANT-07A-UNIVERSAL-LIFECYCLE-MANDATE." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07A-SWEBOK-KA10-FUNDAMENTALS ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07A): combining KA10's statement that no ideal process or set of processes exists (processes must be selected, adapted and applied per project and organizational context), KA10's presentation of multiple model families and paradigms (predictive, iterative, evolutionary, incremental; waterfall through Agile) with named misconceptions about Agile, and KA10's practical-consideration lesson that lifecycle discussions should be supported by realistic estimations and measurements, mandating one life cycle model universally contradicts the inspected sections themselves: model fit is presented as contextual and adaptation as a required, documented activity. This is not a sentence that appears in SWEBOK." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether real organizations that mandate a single model always incur the listed consequences is untested in this lane." .

