se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "CS2023 SE-Construction: Software Construction, CS Core topics 1-2, KA Core topics 3-7, KA Core Illustrative Learning Outcomes 3-5 and 7-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Curriculum topic placement, not professional-consensus endorsement; secure-coding content detail belongs to SEC-Coding (lane SEK-07C scope).", "Non-core topics (larger-scale testing, work sequencing, security problems, autogenerated documentation, green-field vs existing code base, release management, DevOps) are elective and not admitted as lane records beyond the locator summary." ;
     se:method "Read the SE-Construction topic and outcome lists in the 2026-11-07 full-text extraction of the official Body of Knowledge PDF; heading-anchored KU locator." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The CS Core testing/documentation topics with the TDD rationale, the KA Core practice topics and the named learning outcomes were confirmed for curriculum-scope records." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-CS2023-SE-CONSTRUCTION-KU ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SE-Construction unit places practical small-scale testing (CS Core: unit testing; test-driven development, motivated as psychologically valuable because a test-first approach gives students the intellectual satisfaction of spotting problem cases a priori instead of the implement-first instinct of avoiding trying to crash their new creation) and documentation (CS Core: interface documentation describing interface requirements, potentially including formal or informal contracts, pre and post conditions and invariants; implementation documentation focused on tricky and non-obvious code, explicitly not restating common or obvious operations, clarifying dataflow and computation, and refactoring toward self-explanatory code where possible) at CS Core level. KA Core topics are coding style (style guides, commenting, naming), \"best practices\" for coding (defensive coding practices; secure coding practices and principles; using exception handling mechanisms for robustness and fault tolerance), debugging, logging, and use of libraries and frameworks developed by others. Learning outcomes ask students to describe techniques, coding idioms and mechanisms for achieving reliability, efficiency and robustness, to write robust code using exception handling, to compare and contrast integration strategies including top-down, bottom-up and sandwich integration, and to describe analyzing and implementing changes to a project's own and to a large existing code base." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "The KU subsection's printed page number was not independently confirmed; heading-anchored citation is used." .

