se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-API-DESIGN a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 4.1 API Design and Use, printed page 4-10." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The design goals are stated as guidance without effectiveness evidence in the inspected text." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The signature-plus-semantics definition, the six design goals, the outlasts-implementations rationale and the API-first characterization were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-SWEBOK-KA4-TECHNOLOGIES ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA4 section 4.1 defines an API as a set of signatures exported and available to users of a library or framework, states that besides signatures an API should always include statements about the program's effects and/or behaviors (its semantics), and lists the design goals that an API be easy to learn and memorize, lead to readable code, be difficult to misuse, be easy to extend, be complete, and maintain backward compatibility; because APIs usually outlast their implementations for a widely used library or framework, an API should be straightforward and stable. It describes API use as selecting, learning, testing, integrating and possibly extending APIs, notes the OpenAPI standard for HTTP APIs with code generation, and reports the API-first approach of designing and building an application's APIs first, usually with an API description language establishing a contract for expected behavior." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

