se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-DBC-ERROR a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 sections 4.4 and 4.5, printed pages 4-10 to 4-11." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The quality improvement of design by contract is hedged in the source ('is thought to improve') and is not an empirical finding in the inspected text." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The assertion, design-by-contract and defensive-programming definitions and the exception-policy principles were confirmed, with the Guide's own hedge on the quality effect preserved." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-SWEBOK-KA4-TECHNOLOGIES ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA4 section 4.4 defines an assertion as an executable predicate placed in a program that performs runtime checks, notes assertions are especially useful in high-reliability programs for flushing out mismatched interface assumptions and errors introduced by modifications, and that they are typically compiled into the code at development time and later compiled out so they do not degrade performance. It defines design by contract as a development approach in which preconditions and postconditions are included for each routine so that each routine or class forms a contract with the rest of the program, precisely specifying the routine's semantics; design by contract is said to be thought to improve the quality of software construction. Defensive programming means protecting a routine from being broken by invalid inputs, commonly by checking input parameter values and deciding how to handle bad inputs, with assertions often used for the checks. Section 4.5 adds that how errors are handled affects correctness, robustness and other nonfunctional attributes, and lists exception-handling policy principles such as including in the exception message all information that led to the exception, avoiding empty catch blocks, knowing the exceptions library code throws, possibly building a centralized exception reporter, and standardizing the program's use of exceptions." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 3.7 Integration, printed pages 4-9 to 4-10." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "CI delivery-pipeline mechanics beyond the construction-side characterization belong to lane SEK-07E scope.", "The advantage list is presented as 'thought to offer', not as a measured empirical result." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The integration-concern list, the phased/incremental contrast with its hedged advantage list, the scaffolding requirement and the CI characterization were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-SWEBOK-KA4-INTEGRATION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA4 section 3.7 describes integrating individually constructed routines, classes, components and subsystems into a single system as a key construction activity, with concerns including planning the integration sequence, identifying needed hardware, creating scaffolding for interim versions, and determining the degree of pre-integration testing. It presents phased integration (also called big bang integration) as delaying integration of component parts until all parts intended for release in a version are complete, and incremental integration as writing and testing the program in small pieces and combining the pieces one at a time; incremental integration is thought to offer many advantages over phased integration (easier error location, improved progress monitoring, earlier product delivery, improved customer relations) but usually needs additional test infrastructure including stubs, drivers and mock objects. It closes by noting continuous integration has been widely adopted in practice: a team integrates its work frequently, leading to multiple integrations per day, usually automated by a pipeline that builds and tests each integration." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-MINIMIZE-COMPLEXITY a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 1.1 Minimizing Complexity, printed page 4-2." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide asserts the working-memory rationale and the technique supports; it cites no empirical study for them in the inspected text." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The working-memory rationale, the everywhere-and-testing scope claim, the cyclomatic-complexity example with its test-case corollary and the simple-over-clever rule were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-SWEBOK-KA4-INTRO-FUNDAMENTALS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA4 section 1.1 presents minimizing complexity as one of the key goals of software construction, grounded in all people having limited ability to hold complex structures and information in working memory especially over long periods; states the need applies to essentially every aspect of construction and is particularly critical to testing; gives cyclomatic complexity as an example static-analysis measure of how difficult code is to test and understand (tool developed by Thomas J. McCabe, Sr. in 1976, counting linearly independent paths through source code, with ideally at least that number of test cases); and states reduced complexity is achieved by creating simple and readable code rather than clever code, using standards, modular design and other specific techniques, with construction-focused quality techniques also supporting it." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-QUALITY-TECHNIQUES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 3.6 Construction Quality, printed pages 4-8 to 4-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section enumerates generally accepted techniques; it does not compare their effectiveness." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The primary-technique enumeration, the selection dependency and the code-adjacent focus were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-SWEBOK-KA4-CODING-QUALITY ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA4 section 3.6 states that faults introduced during construction can cause serious quality problems including security vulnerabilities, and lists the primary techniques used to ensure the quality of code as it is constructed: unit testing and integration testing, test-first development, use of assertions and defensive programming, debugging, inspections, technical reviews including security-oriented reviews, and static analysis. It adds that the technique selection depends on the software constructed and on the skill set of the software engineers, and that construction quality activities are differentiated from other quality activities by their focus on artifacts closely related to code, such as detailed design, rather than requirements, high-level designs or plans." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07B-SYNTHESIS-BIG-BANG a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "Inference inverting SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 4 section 3.7's stated incremental-integration advantage list onto its phased-integration description; anchored to the same verified locator." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical case evidence in this lane supports the severity of the characterized failure modes.", "Synthesis inference, not a statement made by SWEBOK." ;
     se:method "Lane-level inversion of one verified section's own contrast; no additional source consulted." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Provides the inference trail motivating the se:ANT-07B-BIG-BANG-INTEGRATION record." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07B-SWEBOK-KA4-INTEGRATION ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07B): reading SWEBOK 3.7's description of phased integration (deferring integration of component parts until all parts intended for release in a version are complete, \"also called big bang integration\") against its named advantages of incremental integration (easier error location, improved progress monitoring, earlier product delivery, improved customer relations) yields a failure characterization of big bang integration: faults and interface mismatches surface together in one late event, error location is harder, progress between code-complete and integrated is less observable, and feedback to developers and customers arrives later. The Guide presents the comparison but does not itself label big bang integration an anti-pattern; this inversion is a lane inference, not a sentence that appears in the Guide." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether real teams using phased integration always incur the characterized failure modes is untested in this lane." .

