     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-CS2023-SEC-FOUNDATIONS ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SEC-Foundations unit teaches, as CS Core, developing a security mindset incorporating crosscutting concepts — confidentiality, integrity, availability, risk assessment, systems thinking, adversarial thinking and human-centered thinking — principles and practices of protection such as least privilege, open design, fail-safe defaults, defense in depth and zero trust and how they can be implemented, and optimization considerations between security, privacy, performance and other design goals." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Printed page numbers of the SEC-Foundations topics were not independently confirmed; heading-anchored citation is used." .

se:EVD-07C-NIST-PO1-REQUIREMENTS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 Section 2, Table 1, practice PO.1 and tasks PO.1.1-PO.1.3, printed pages 5-6." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "A government recommendation addressed to software producers and acquirers; adoption is risk-based, not mandatory.", "Notional implementation examples are illustrative; none is required by the publication." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official NIST PDF (SHA-256 617746e553a9e2da49bfbd4eef0dfc3094758a39b869314e4173ac36605cde22) on 2026-11-07; text extracted at the verified page indices and paraphrased originally." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The continuous-availability intent, the internal/external source split, the maintenance tasks and the third-party communication task were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-NIST218-PO1 ;
     se:statement "NIST SSDF v1.1 practice PO.1 recommends ensuring that security requirements for software development are known at all times so they can be taken into account throughout the SDLC and so duplication of effort is minimized, drawing on requirements from internal sources (the organization's policies, business objectives and risk management strategy) and external sources (applicable laws and regulations). Its tasks include identifying and documenting security requirements for development infrastructure and processes and for organization-developed software and maintaining them over time (with examples such as risk-based architecture and design requirements, compliance verification at key SDLC points, and at least annual reviews), and communicating requirements to third parties providing commercial software components, including requiring provenance data and integrity verification mechanisms." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW1-SECURE-BY-DESIGN a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 Section 2, Table 1, practice PW.1 and tasks PW.1.1-PW.1.3, printed page 11." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "A recommendation framed for voluntary adoption; the publication's own ordering note states table order does not imply implementation sequence or relative importance." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official NIST PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The secure-by-design intent, the named risk-modeling forms, and the requirements/risks/design-decision tracking with re-evaluated exceptions were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-NIST218-PW1 ;
     se:statement "NIST SSDF v1.1 practice PW.1 recommends designing software to meet security requirements and mitigate security risks: identify and evaluate the security requirements for the software, determine what security risks the software is likely to face during operation and how its design and architecture should mitigate those risks, and justify any cases where risk-based analysis indicates that security requirements should be relaxed or waived; the practice states that addressing security requirements and risks during software design (secure by design) is key for improving software security and also helps improve development efficiency. Task PW.1.1 names threat modeling, attack modeling and attack surface mapping as forms of risk modeling; task PW.1.2 tracks and maintains the software's security requirements, risks and design decisions, recording the response to each risk and the rationales for approved exceptions and periodically re-evaluating them." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW2-DESIGN-REVIEW a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 Section 2, Table 1, practice PW.2 and task PW.2.1, printed page 12." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "A recommendation; organizations determine review depth by their own risk context." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official NIST PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The independence condition (not involved with the design), the automated-enforcement alternative and the artifact-recording expectations were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-NIST218-PW2 ;
     se:statement "NIST SSDF v1.1 practice PW.2 recommends reviewing the software design to verify compliance with security requirements and risk information so the software will meet the security requirements and satisfactorily address the identified risks. Task PW.2.1 assigns this review to 1) a qualified person (or people) who were not involved with the design and/or 2) automated processes instantiated in the toolchain, to confirm and enforce that the design meets all the security requirements and satisfactorily addresses the identified risk information; the notional examples include having the designer correct failures to meet the requirements, changing the design or the risk response strategy when security requirements cannot be met, and recording design-review findings to serve as artifacts." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-CONSTRUCTION-RULES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 4.4, printed pages 13-4 to 13-5." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The rules are presented as generally helpful guidance, not as guarantees; the Guide states there are no bulletproof ways to achieve secure software development." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The coding-rules enumeration and the CERT top-ten practice list were confirmed for the implementation-practice records." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-SWEBOK-KA13-CONSTRUCTION-TESTING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA13 section 4.4 states that, in its current state, software construction security mostly refers to the coding of security into software, achieved by following recommended rules: structure the process so that sections requiring extra privileges are modules as small as possible performing only the tasks that require those privileges; ensure assumptions in the program are validated, or document them for installers and maintainers so they know the assumptions attackers will try to invalidate; ensure the program does not share objects in memory with other programs; and check every function's error status, recovering only when neither the error's cause nor its effects affect security considerations. It also lists the CERT top ten software security practices, which include validating input, heeding compiler warnings, architecting and designing for security policies, keeping it simple, default deny, adhering to least privilege, sanitizing data sent to other software, practicing defense in depth, using effective quality assurance techniques, and adopting a software construction security standard." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-BY-DESIGN a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 1 Software Security Fundamentals (with 1.1/1.2), printed page 13-1." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide reports this as a generally accepted belief, not as an empirical finding; no effectiveness evidence is cited by this lane." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF (SHA-256 0c9db4da0b2425c58cf778b99131eb31bb6c188e98b67163a549a1366d96e511) on 2026-11-07; text extracted at the verified page index and paraphrased originally." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The design-in preference, the every-stage scope and the third-party introduction of vulnerabilities were confirmed for the professional-BOK scoped claim." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-SWEBOK-KA13-FUNDAMENTALS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA13 reports a generally accepted belief that it is much better to design security into software than to patch it in after the software is developed, and that designing security in requires considering every development life cycle stage — software requirements, design, construction and testing security — with security also considered during maintenance, where security faults and loopholes can be and often are introduced; the same introduction adds that security vulnerabilities are not only introduced during development but also by third-party components such as libraries, COTS and operating systems." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-DESIGN a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 4.2, printed page 13-4." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
