     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The activity triple, the threat/risk inputs, the lifecycle-order and revisiting qualifications, and the traceability expectation were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-SWEBOK-KA13-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA13 section 4.1 characterizes security requirements engineering as including elicitation, specification and prioritization; it considers threats, illustrated by misuse and abuse cases, threat actors, and security risk assessments, and notes that the selection of life cycle models may impact the order of activities and that software product revision implies a need to revisit security requirements. It states that traceability of security requirements throughout the development process is important and that security teams may include specialists in security requirements." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 section 4.5, printed page 13-5." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The subsection names method families; it does not compare their detection effectiveness." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The dual purpose (requirements compliance and known-vulnerability absence), the static/dynamic method split, the expertise requirement and the authorization boundary were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-SWEBOK-KA13-CONSTRUCTION-TESTING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA13 section 4.5 gives security testing two purposes: ensuring that the implemented software meets the security requirements, and verifying that the software implementation contains none of the known vulnerabilities. Whereas general software testing methods can handle the former, the latter requires security-specific methods: static analysis, conducted on source code or compiled binaries, where automation plays a significant role but the expertise of security professionals is required to properly operate and configure the tools and verify the results; and dynamic testing, typically vulnerability assessment or penetration testing (also called ethical hacking), which should always be conducted within legal boundaries and with proper authorization so as to remain distinguishable from illegal hacking." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-VULNERABILITY-DATABASES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 13 sections 4.6 and 5.1, printed pages 13-5 to 13-6." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The sources describe the databases' existence and purpose, not their quality or coverage." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The knowledge-base names, the disclosure-process pairing and the explicit tool-limitation statement were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-SWEBOK-KA13-VULNERABILITY-MANAGEMENT ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA13 section 4.6 names the Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures (CVE), Common Weakness Enumeration (CWE) and Common Attack Pattern Enumeration and Classification (CAPEC) databases as the shared categorization of common security defects, with the Common Vulnerability Scoring System (CVSS) expressing characteristics and severity of software vulnerabilities; security maintenance encompasses mitigating the effects of vulnerabilities in a system and in the third-party components it uses, often accompanied by a vulnerability disclosure process allowing vulnerabilities to be reported. Section 5.1 adds that security vulnerability checking tools — source code analyzers and binary analysis tools — significantly aid detection but cannot find all vulnerabilities, for example those that manifest only in hard-to-produce software states or unusual circumstances." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07C-SYNTHESIS-AFTERTHOUGHT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "Cross-source inference combining SWEBOK KA13 section 1 with SSDF practices PO.1 and PW.1; lane-marked synthesis." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical case evidence in this lane supports the severity of the anti-pattern's consequences.", "Synthesis inference, not a direct statement of any source." ;
     se:method "Lane-level comparison of three verified evidence records from two directly inspected sources." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Provides the inference trail motivating the se:ANT-07C-SECURITY-AFTERTHOUGHT record." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07C-SWEBOK-KA13-FUNDAMENTALS ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07C): SWEBOK KA13 section 1 reports designing security in as much better than patching after development, SSDF PW.1 describes secure-by-design attention during design as key for improving software security, and SSDF PO.1 expects security requirements to be known at all times throughout the SDLC; on that combined reading, treating security as a post-development afterthought inverts all three authorities at once — the security-requirements baseline, the threat-informed design record and the requirements-compliance anchor for verification are all missing when security is deferred, which is the defining characteristic of the anti-pattern this lane records. This is not a sentence that appears in any of the three sources." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether real teams that defer security always incur the listed consequences is untested in this lane." .

