se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-SLA a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 2.3, printed page 6-8." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section describes generally accepted operations activities, not a mandated availability-management process for every service." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The manage-measure-investigate loop and the SLA clarification role were confirmed for the availability claim and the SLA-anchored pattern." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07D-SWEBOK-KA6-AVAILABILITY-SLA ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.3 presents service availability and continuity as managed properties: they must be managed so customer commitments are met; they are defined as nonfunctional requirements early in a project (the section points to the Software Quality KA); operations engineers plan, design, implement and test the proper infrastructure; software availability is measured and recorded; unplanned nonavailability is investigated and acted on; and service reports produce availability and continuity indicators against service-level targets. The service-level management process monitors the agreed level of service, including workload characteristics, performance and availability trend information and customer satisfaction, and defining, agreeing and documenting service-level agreements (SLAs) helps clarify the full range of operations service obligations." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-MGMT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 2.4, printed page 6-8." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section relays an ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1 need; the underlying standard's normative text is not itself admitted (registry ISO sources are metadata-only)." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The forecast-translate-analyze-plan loop, the annual capacity plan and the costed-options framing were confirmed for the capacity claim and decision model." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07D-SWEBOK-KA6-CAPACITY ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.4, relaying ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1, describes software capacity management as ensuring the software product has the capacity, at all times, to meet current and future agreed-upon demands created by the customer's business needs: current and expected business requirements should be understood and translated into specific, documented requirements; the reaction to workload or environment variations should be predictable; and data on current and previous components and on resource utilization at an appropriate level should be captured and analyzed to support the process. Capacity management is called the focal point for all performance and capacity issues, should support development of new and changed services by sizing and modeling them, and produces a capacity plan (at a suitable frequency, at least annually) documenting actual infrastructure performance and expected requirements, with costed options and recommended solutions for achieving the agreed service-level targets in the SLA." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 2.5, printed pages 6-8 to 6-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section names approaches (automated failover daemons, full/incremental backups) without comparative reliability data." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The failure-is-inevitable stance, the planned-during-development failure-handling logic and the rehearsal requirement were confirmed for the plan-for-failure principle and the failover practice." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07D-SWEBOK-KA6-DR-FAILOVER ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.5 states that after a major service failure or disaster, backups of data, documents and software plus any equipment or staff necessary for service restoration should be quickly available; that the need for successful recovery should influence the choice of backup and recovery methods (full or incremental), how frequently restore points are established, where they are stored and how long they are retained; and that preparedness and regular testing of backup, disaster recovery and failover should be constantly rehearsed as the production environment changes (testing disaster recovery requires stopping the service, identifying the checkpoint state and triggering the failover process). It further states that software engineers should understand that failure is inevitable, that automated failover daemons can reduce recovery time drastically, and that to achieve this software applications should include failure-handling logic, which must be planned during development." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-MONITOR-EVIDENCE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 4.3, printed page 6-11." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The KPI list is illustrative of what evidence should inform operations; the section does not define each indicator's computation." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The evidence-over-hope norm and the real-time KPI inventory were confirmed for the observability claim, principle and practice." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07D-SWEBOK-KA6-MONITOR ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 4.3 states that software engineering operations activities monitor capacity, continuity and availability, and that in a DevOps mindset hope should not be a strategy: engineers should instead be informed about system quality and operational health with evidence, in the form of key performance indicators available to stakeholders in real time. The named evidence sources are production system monitoring and product telemetry; actionable verification and validation results before and after release to production; end-user activity and resource use; impact analysis results; inter- and intra-related dependencies required for system operation; configuration changes unrelated to approved deployment tasks; and security and resilience performance capability." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-PERF-RELIABILITY-EARLY a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 section 1.6, printed page 6-6." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
