se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 subsection 3.2, printed page 6-10." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section characterizes traditional manual hand-off processes as inefficient without citing quantitative evidence in the inspected text." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The deployment/release definitions, release control activities, the manual-vs-automated deployment contrast, the two release strategy families, the canary technique and the business-decision framing were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-DEPLOY-RELEASE ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 3.2 defines deployment as the installation of a specified version of software to a given environment (for example an integration test environment or production) and release as making a feature or set of features available to all customers or a segment of customers. It lists release control activities from ISO/IEC/IEEE 12207 (identify and record release requests, identify the software system elements in a release, approval, and tracking releases in their specified environments). It contrasts traditional processes — an application ready for deployment is transferred from a development team to an operations team responsible for deployment that is mostly done manually, which the Guide characterizes as inefficient from both time and quality perspectives — with the DevOps call to automate the deployment steps: packaging the code, generating configuration files, restarting servers, configuring servers and databases, installing the software on the servers, launching execution, and smoke testing. It groups risk-reducing release engineering strategies into environment-based strategies (deploying the new version to a staging environment) and application-based strategies (toggles such as feature toggles enabling or disabling code sections via configuration parameters). It describes the canary release testing technique as a partial, time-limited deployment of a change with an evaluation that helps decide whether to proceed with complete deployment, notes tools that observe newly started software and automatically roll back changes that do not pass the validation period, and states that once the application platform is deployed in the targeted production environment, the decision to make it available to users (release it) becomes a business decision." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 subsection 2.2, printed pages 6-7 to 6-8." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The single-repository and automation recommendation is presented by the Guide as a DevOps recommendation, not as a universally mandated practice." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The environment typology, the coherence/synchronization requirement and the single-source automation recommendation were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.2 describes the overall software process as requiring different environments at different stages — typically the development environment, the testing or quality assurance environment, the preproduction environment and the production environment — and states that to build quality into the product and reduce the risks of releasing software in production, engineers must ensure the different environments are coherent and synchronized with the production environment. It reports that DevOps recommends the creation of all the different environments be automated and built from a single code repository, that in mature DevOps organizations environment creation is completely automated and available as a service, and that all environments need to be built from the same code source (single source of truth), which leads to the concept of infrastructure as code." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-RELEASE-PLANNING a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 subsection 2.1.1, printed page 6-7." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section describes the planning activity from the operations engineer's role; it does not prescribe a universal release-planning procedure for all contexts." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF (SHA-256 0c9db4da0b2425c58cf778b99131eb31bb6c188e98b67163a549a1366d96e511) on 2026-11-07; text extracted at the verified page index and paraphrased originally." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The release/version planning task list was confirmed for the professional-BOK scoped claim." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-RELEASE-PLANNING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 2.1.1 states that once individual requests are received and validated, the release or version planning activity requires operations engineers to identify the target availability dates of individual requests, agree on the content of subsequent releases or versions, identify potential conflicts and develop alternatives, assess the risk of a given release and develop a rollback and data migration plan in case problems arise, and inform all stakeholders." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ROLLBACK a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 6 subsection 3.3, printed pages 6-10 to 6-11." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section describes the generally accepted activity; it does not quantify rollback time budgets or prescribe rehearse frequency." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The rollback definition, the rehearse-before-deploy requirement, the automation/surveillance note and the strategy-category linkage were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ROLLBACK ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA6 section 3.3 describes rollback and data migration as the terms used for the process of returning software and its database to a state where they work properly, and states that software engineers ensure that when a new version of the software and its databases have been modified and deployed to production, they can easily and quickly be rolled back in case the new version causes defects or product degradation; this means a planned and rehearsed rollback is done before a new version is deployed in production. It adds that DevOps processes automate the process, that automated surveillance can trigger rollback and data migration to a previous state so quickly the end user does not notice the problem, and that both release strategy categories (environment-based and application-based, from section 3.2) can be used to support rollback." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

