     se:result "The building definition, the reproduction and tool-control requirements, the SBOM-as-build-output note and the commit-triggered pipeline description were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 6 frames release as distributing software and related artifacts outside the development activity, including internal releases and distribution to customers, and states that when different versions of a software item are available for delivery, re-creating specific versions and packaging the correct materials for version delivery are frequently necessary, with the software library a key element in release and delivery tasks. Section 6.1 defines software building as constructing the correct versions of SCIs, using the appropriate configuration data, into a software package for delivery to a customer or other recipient such as a testing team; build instructions help ensure the proper build steps are taken in the correct sequence; in addition to building for new releases, SCM must usually be able to reproduce previous releases for recovery, testing, maintenance or additional release purposes; and to rebuild an exact copy of a previously built SCI, supporting tools and associated build instructions must be under SCM control to ensure availability of the correct tool versions. It notes that the build process and products are often subject to software quality verification, that build outputs may become records of quality, security or regulatory compliance, and that the SBOM listing the artifacts included in the build is an important CM output. On continuous integration it states that software building is performed automatically when changes to CIs are committed to a source control repository: tools running on a local or cloud-based server monitor the project's source control system and initiate a pipeline of steps undertaken every time a change is committed to a particular branch, configured to retrieve a fresh copy of the complete source code and execute the necessary commands to compile and link the code, often combined with steps to validate coding standards via automated static analysis, execute unit tests, determine code coverage metrics, or extract documentation from the source code, with the resulting artifacts then deployed through the release management process." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-LIBRARIES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 8 subsections 2.5 and 2.6, printed pages 8-8 to 8-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section names library concepts and roles; it does not prescribe a specific repository or artifact-store architecture." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The SBOM definition, the software library/definitive media library roles and the release-management dependency on libraries were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-LIBRARIES ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 2.5 defines a Software Bill of Materials (SBOM) as a formal record containing the details and supply chain relationships of the CIs used in building software, with SBOM CIs frequently called components — source code, libraries, modules and other artifacts that can be open source or proprietary, free or paid — and notes that relationships between CIs can be tracked in an SBOM. Section 2.6 defines a software library as a controlled collection of source code, scripts, object code, documentation and related artifacts, states that source code is stored in a version control system providing traceability and security for the baselines developed and that multiple development streams are supported in version control systems linked to the binary objects derived during the build process, which are typically stored in a repository that should contain cryptographic hashes used to perform the physical configuration audit. It also states that the definitive media library contains the release baseline(s) of the artifacts that can be deployed to the test, stage and production systems, and that the release management process depends on these software libraries to manage the artifacts deployed." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-PLANNING-CI a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 8 subsections 1.3 and 1.4, printed pages 8-3 to 8-5." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The build-test-deploy cycle phrase is the Guide's characterization of CI at planning level; the section does not prescribe specific tooling or pipeline structure." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The branching/merging planning rule, the continuous-integration characterization and the SCMP subordinate-procedure examples were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-PLANNING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 1.3 directs that branching and merging strategies be carefully planned and communicated because they affect many SCM activities, defining a branch as a set of evolving source file versions and merging as combining different changes to the same file. It notes that the chosen software development life cycle model affects SCM activities — for instance, many software development approaches use continuous integration, which is characterized by frequent build-test-deploy cycles, so SCM activities must be planned accordingly — and section 1.4 records that the SCM plan is a living document whose subordinate procedures define, among day-to-day matters, which branching strategies will be used, how frequently builds will occur, and how often automated tests of all kinds will be run." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-RELEASE-MGMT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 8 subsection 6.2, printed pages 8-13 to 8-14." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section describes generally accepted release management activities; fault-density-driven release timing is presented as an influence on the decision, not a formula." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The release management scope, the when-to-release criteria, the VDD/release-notes/packaging elements, the integrity mechanism and the continuous-delivery pipeline were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-RELEASE-MGMT ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA8 section 6.2 states that software release management encompasses the identification, packaging and delivery of the elements of a product (for example an executable program, documentation, release notes or configuration data), and that given product changes can occur continually, one concern is determining when to issue a release, with the severity of the problems addressed by the release and measurements of the fault densities of prior releases affecting this decision. The packaging task identifies which product items are to be delivered and selects the correct variants for the product's intended application; the information documenting the physical contents of a release is the version description document (VDD); release notes describe new capabilities, known problems and platform requirements; the package contains installation or upgrade instructions, complicated by users holding versions several releases old; release management may need to track the product's distribution to customers or target systems, and a mechanism to help ensure the released item's integrity (for example a digital signature) can be implemented. On continuous delivery it states that a pipeline is established to build software continuously as described in 6.1, and the resulting build artifacts — executable code and libraries — can be combined into an installation package and deployed into an environment for verification or production use." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07E-SYNTHESIS-CI-ALIGNMENT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "Cross-source inference combining one CS2023 learning outcome (outcome 5 of SE-Tools) with the SWEBOK KA8 6.1 continuous-integration description; explicitly a lane-level synthesis." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Alignment of function does not establish that either source endorses the other's authority level.", "Synthesis inference, not a statement in either source." ;
     se:method "Lane-level comparison of two verified evidence records (se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-OUTCOMES and se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING) from two different permitted sources." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Provides the inference trail for the alignment claim se:CLM-07E-CI-ALIGNMENT-SYNTHESIS." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07E-CS2023-SE-TOOLS-KU ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07E): the CS2023 SE-Tools learning outcome that positions CI systems as a ground-truth for the state of the team's shared code (build and test success) and the SWEBOK KA8 description of CI as commit-triggered automated building with compile, link and automated checks align on the same function — automated build-plus-test verification of the current shared head/trunk — even though the two sources express it at different authority levels (curriculum outcome vs professional-BOK process description). This combined reading is not a sentence that appears in either source." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether every practice described as CI in industry matches this aligned function is untested in this lane." .

