se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "CS2023 Software Engineering knowledge unit SE-Refactoring: Refactoring and Code Evolution, KA Core topics 1-4 and Non-core topic 5 with illustrative learning outcomes." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Curriculum topic titles and outcomes only; the unit does not rank methods or prescribe professional procedure.", "Semantic Versioning and trunk-based development are named as versioning topics without the curriculum defining them here." ;
     se:method "Read the verbatim topic extraction of the official CS2023 Body of Knowledge PDF produced on 2026-11-07 (full text via extraction service; binary download unavailable from this network); heading-anchored KU locator, printed page not independently confirmed." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Topics 1-5 with their subtopics and the selected learning outcomes were confirmed against the verbatim extraction." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:statement "The CS2023 SE knowledge unit 'SE-Refactoring: Refactoring and Code Evolution' lists as KA Core topics: Hyrum's Law (the Law of Implicit Interfaces); backward compatibility, including that compatibility is not a property of a single entity but a property of a relationship, and that it needs to be evaluated in terms of provider plus consumer(s) or with a well-specified model of what forms of compatibility a provider aspires to or promises; refactoring, covering standard refactoring patterns (rename, inline, outline, etc.), use of refactoring tools in IDEs, application of static-analysis tools to identify code in need of refactoring and to generate changes, and the value of refactoring as a remedy for technical debt; and versioning, covering Semantic Versioning and trunk-based development. Its Non-core topic addresses large-scale refactoring — techniques for when a refactoring change is too large to commit safely or when change cannot be synchronized between a provider and all consumers (multiple repositories, consumers with private code) — naming the techniques of expressing both old and new APIs so that they can co-exist and minimizing the size of behavior changes, and asking why the techniques are required ('API consumers I can see' versus 'consumers I can't see'). Its illustrative learning outcomes include identifying both explicit and implicit behavior of an interface and the potential risks from Hyrum's Law, identifying changes that can be broadly considered backward compatible, and evaluating whether a proposed change is sufficiently safe given the versioning methodology in use." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "The knowledge unit's printed page number was not independently confirmed; heading-anchored citation within the registry-recorded SE section span is used." .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-EVOLUTION-LAWS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 1.5 Evolution of Software, printed pages 7-3 to 7-4." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The laws are reported secondhand by SWEBOK as a summary of Lehman's research; the primary Lehman publications are not registered sources in this project.", "The paraphrase names all eight laws but restates in full only the two laws the lane's principle uses (Continuing Change, Increasing Complexity)." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase of the eight named laws and the key-findings sentences." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The eight law names and the evolutionary-development interpretation were confirmed for the lane's evolution claim and principle." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-EVOLUTION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 1.5 reports that research by Lehman and others over a period of twenty years led to eight laws of software evolution, which it names Continuing Change (software must be continually adapted or it becomes progressively less satisfactory), Increasing Complexity (complexity increases unless work is done to maintain or reduce it), Self-Regulation, Invariant Work Rate, Conservation of Familiarity, Continuing Growth, Declining Quality and Feedback System. It records key findings that maintenance is evolutionary development, that maintenance decisions are aided by understanding what happens to software over time, and that large software programs are never complete, continuing to evolve and growing more complex unless action is taken to reduce that complexity." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-IMPACT-ANALYSIS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 2.1.3 Impact Analysis, printed page 7-6." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section describes generally accepted activity; it does not prescribe a specific impact-analysis technique or tool." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The impact-analysis purpose, its SCM entry point, its identified outputs (affected items, resources, risks) and the severity guidance were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-IMPACT ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.1.3 describes impact analysis as assessing the detailed effects of proposed changes on existing software: it is performed after a change request (originating from a modification request or problem report) enters the software configuration management process; it identifies all systems and software products that would be affected by the change request, develops an estimate of the resources needed to accomplish the change, and determines the risks involved in making it. Maintainers often use the severity of a problem report as a guide when deciding how and when to fix, conduct the analysis to identify affected components, develop potential solutions and recommend a course of action; designing software with maintainability in mind greatly facilitates impact analysis." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-KA8-SCR-PROCESS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 8 sections 3-3.2 Software Configuration Change Control, printed pages 8-9 to 8-10." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Only the change-control sections of KA8 were inspected; the rest of the SCM knowledge area belongs to other lanes' scope." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase. Cited for maintenance/change-control relevance only; CI/CD delivery mechanics remain outside this lane." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The SCR process steps, the impact-analysis technical evaluation, the CCB authority and the implementation tracking and audit close-out were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA8-CHANGE-CONTROL ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA8 section 3.1 describes the software change request (SCR) process as providing formal procedures for submitting and recording change requests, evaluating the potential cost and impact of a proposed change, and accepting, modifying, deferring or rejecting it; a change request may be originated by anyone at any point in the life cycle, with the type of change (defect or enhancement) usually recorded, and an emergency path usually also exists where changes are implemented with the change process performed afterward. Once an SCR is received, a technical evaluation — also known as an impact analysis — determines the extent of the modifications necessary should the request be accepted. The authority for accepting or rejecting proposed changes rests with a configuration control board (on smaller projects possibly the leader or an assigned individual, with an SCM representative always present), whose composition can vary by criticality, nature of the change and life-cycle position. Section 3.2 adds that approved SCRs are implemented using defined procedures, that a means for tracking which SCRs are incorporated into particular software versions and baselines must be provided, and that completed changes may undergo configuration audits and quality verification including ensuring that only approved changes have been made." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-CATEGORIES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 1.6 Categories of Software Maintenance, printed pages 7-4 to 7-5." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Category definitions are attributed by SWEBOK to ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764; the ISO text itself was not ingested (registry metadata-only)." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase of the category definitions." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The five standardized categories, the corrections/enhancements regrouping, the emergency sixth category and the additive-versus-perfective distinction were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-CATEGORIES ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 1.6 states that five categories of software maintenance have been standardized to classify a maintenance request — corrective (reactive repair of discovered problems after delivery), preventive (correcting latent faults before they occur in the live system), adaptive (keeping the product usable in an evolving environment), additive (adding functionality or features with relatively large additions) and perfective (enhancements for users, documentation improvement and recoding to improve performance, maintainability or other attributes) — that ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 regroups these as either corrections or enhancements, and that the standard also defines a sixth category, emergency maintenance: unscheduled modification performed to temporarily keep a system operational pending corrective maintenance." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-DEFINITION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 Introduction and section 1.1 Definitions and Terminology, printed pages 7-1 to 7-2." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 standard itself is registry metadata-only in this project; the objective is cited as SWEBOK reports it, not from the ISO text." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF (SHA-256 0c9db4da0b2425c58cf778b99131eb31bb6c188e98b67163a549a1366d96e511) on 2026-11-07; text extracted at the verified page indices with pypdf 7.0.0 and paraphrased originally." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The maintenance definition, the pre-/post-delivery stage split and the ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 objective were confirmed for the lane's foundational definition claim and concept." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-MAINT-DEF ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 defines software maintenance as the totality of activities required to provide cost-effective support for software in operation, with activities performed during a pre-delivery stage (planning for post-delivery operations, maintainability, and logistics support for the transition from development to maintenance) and a post-delivery stage (software surveillance, modification, training, and operating or interfacing with a help desk). Citing ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764, it states the objective of software maintenance as modifying existing software while preserving its integrity, and notes the maintenance phase begins after a warranty or post-implementation support period while maintenance activities occur much earlier." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 2.1.4 Maintainability, printed pages 7-6 to 7-7." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The maintainability definition is attributed to ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764, whose text remains metadata-only in this project." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The maintainability definition, the maintainability-to-technical-debt linkage, the accumulation mechanism and the three investigation areas were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.1.4, citing ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764, defines maintainability as the capability of the software product to be modified (corrections, improvements, adaptation to environment changes, and changes in requirements and functional specifications), states it should be specified, reviewed and controlled during development to reduce maintenance costs, and states that compromised maintainability typically increases the burden on the engineers who maintain the software in the future — in other words, it creates technical debt. It describes debt accumulating when the need to quickly address corrective, emergency and additive maintenance tasks, constrained by limited time and understanding of the software, leads to immediate but potentially under-considered solutions that are often not peer-reviewed, and names three areas to investigate in depth when addressing technical debt: code quality versus relevance (not all technical debt is urgent), alignment with organizational objectives, and process loss (complementary skills of the engineers involved)." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-ORG-PRIORITIES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 2.2.1 Alignment with Organizational Objectives, printed pages 7-7 to 7-8." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section characterizes common organizational behavior; it does not quantify the priority skew." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The feature-priority pressure, the balance-against-maintainability requirement and the develop-new versus enhance-existing comparison were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-ORG-OBJECTIVES ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.2.1 observes that, as a consequence of maintenance economics being less visible than development economics, adding new features is often given higher priority than other maintenance activities such as refactoring, security or performance improvement, and that such organizational objectives and constraints must be balanced with software maintainability and engineering standards to avoid code decay and technical debt. It also notes that applying product management approaches to development and maintenance can help organizations understand the total cost of operational software over its full life cycle and compare the costs and benefits of developing new software versus enhancing existing software." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-REFACTORING a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 4.2 Software Reengineering, printed pages 7-13 to 7-14." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The continuous-integration remark is cited here only as refactoring context; CI/CD delivery mechanics belong to the 07E lane boundary and are not recorded by this lane." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page indices; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The reengineering definition including the legacy-replacement purpose, the behavior-preserving refactoring definition, the debt-reduction role before and after changes, and the continuous-refactoring observation were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-REENGINEERING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 4.2 defines software reengineering as the examination and alteration of software to reconstitute it in a new form, including the subsequent implementation of the new form, noting it is often undertaken not to improve maintainability but to replace aging legacy software. It defines refactoring as a reengineering technique that aims to reorganize a program without changing its behavior, seeking to improve the internal structure and maintainability of software, and states refactoring techniques can be used during maintenance activities to reduce the technical debt of the codebase before and after code changes. It further notes that in Agile development the incremental nature of continuous integration often requires the code to be continuously refactored, supporting volatile life cycles by reducing and managing growing complexity while improving developer productivity." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-REVERSE-ENGINEERING a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 4.3 Reverse Engineering, printed page 7-14." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section names tool categories without evaluating specific tools." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The passive-analysis definition, the three named types and the tooling features including debt estimation and code-smell detection were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-REVERSE-ENGINEERING ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 4.3 defines reverse engineering as the process of analyzing software to identify its components and their interrelationships and to create representations of the software in another form or at higher levels of abstraction; it is passive, changing neither the software nor producing new software. Its named types are re-documentation, design recovery and data reverse engineering (recovering logical schemata from physical databases), and software visualization tools commonly accompany quality assurance features such as quality metrics calculation, technical debt estimation and detection of bad design and coding practices (code smells)." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-COST a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 7 section 2.3.1 Technical Debt Cost Estimation, printed page 7-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The listed maintainability improvements are SWEBOK's report of ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 suggestions; the ISO text was not ingested." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at the verified page index; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The debt definition, the measurement indicators, the uncertain-reward barrier, the four organizational determinations and the root-cause requirement were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 2.3.1 states that technical debt generally makes code more expensive to maintain than it has to be and represents the effort required to fix problems that remain in the code when an application is initially released by the development team. It names size, complexity, the number of engineering flaws, and violations of good architectural design and coding practices in the source code as indicators and techniques that can help engineers measure technical debt, and attributes to ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 a list of maintainability improvements (legibility, structured code, reduced complexity, accurate comments, error tracing, code-to-design traceability, inspections and code reviews). It identifies the uncertain reward for addressing debt — or for implementing any potential enhancement — as a common barrier, and lists what organizations need to determine: the quality of their current software, the extent of their technical debt, the potential savings from investing in quality enhancement, and the impact of current quality issues on their business. It closes that technical debt is only one of several factors contributing to excess unplanned work, and should not be handled in isolation but through examination of its root causes." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07F-SYNTHESIS-DEBT-PRIORITIZATION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "Cross-section inference combining SWEBOK KA7 sections 2.1.4, 2.2.1 and 2.3.1; synthesis evidence anchored at the section 2.3.1 locator." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical case evidence in this lane tests the procedure's effectiveness.", "Synthesis inference, not a direct SWEBOK statement." ;
     se:method "Lane-level comparison of three verified evidence records from the same knowledge area." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Provides the inference trail motivating the lane's debt-versus-features decision model (se:DEC-07F-DEBT-VERSUS-FEATURES)." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07F-SWEBOK-KA7-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07F): SWEBOK KA7 section 2.2.1 reports that organizations commonly give new features higher priority than debt work such as refactoring, while section 2.3.1 reports that debt decisions should rest on measured determinations of current quality, debt extent, potential savings and business impact, and section 2.1.4 adds that not all technical debt is urgent. Read together, the inspected sections imply a repeatable decision procedure — quantify debt against business objectives before choosing between feature work and debt reduction — that no single inspected section states as one procedure. This is a lane inference, not a sentence in SWEBOK." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether real organizations applying the quantification step make better feature-versus-debt choices is untested in this lane." .

