se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 15 section 2 (2.1-2.7 The Engineering Decision-Making Process), printed pages 15-5 to 15-7." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The named ISO 12207/15288 recommendations are the Guide's secondary characterization; those standards are metadata-only in the registry and their normative text is not cited." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 291-293; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The six-step process, the consequence-scaled effort rule, the risk/uncertainty technique families and the close-the-loop requirement were confirmed for the decision-process claim, the trade-off pattern, and the close-the-loop principle." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 2 presents the engineering decision-making process as: understand the real problem (e.g., via interrogative techniques and broader context); identify all reasonable technically feasible solutions (the best solution must be in the candidate set before it can be selected; prototyping and peer review help verify feasibility); define the selection criteria (expressed as objectively as possible, accepting unquantifiable criteria; more criteria when the consequences of a wrong decision are greater); evaluate each alternative against the criteria (same basis for comparison, same planning horizon, same kinds of costs and incomes); select the preferred alternative (decision-making-under- risk techniques when probabilities can be assigned — expected value, Monte Carlo, decision trees, value of perfect information — and decision-making-under-uncertainty techniques when they cannot — Laplace, Maximin, Maximax, Hurwicz, Minimax Regret; ranges of estimates, sensitivity analysis and delaying final decisions address estimate inaccuracy); and monitor the performance of the selected alternative, closing the loop on estimates by comparing them to actual outcomes so that estimation improves over time. The time, effort and care spent should scale with the consequences of a wrong decision, and ISO 12207 and ISO 15288 are cited as recommending a decision-management strategy and stakeholder identification for high-consequence decisions." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 15 section 8 (8.1-8.5 Estimation), printed pages 15-13 to 15-16." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Code of Ethics clause is quoted by SWEBOK; the Code text itself was not independently inspected by this lane.", "The technique accuracy ordering is the Guide's overview characterization, not an empirical ranking cited from studies." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 299-302; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The estimate definition, the decisions-not-certainty framing, the inherent-uncertainty statement, the quoted ethics clause and the five techniques with their trade-offs were confirmed for the uncertainty claim, the estimate concept, the decision model and the anti-pattern synthesis." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 8 defines an estimate as an analytical prediction of a quantity (a project's size, cost or schedule, among others) and states that software engineers estimate in order to make decisions when critical quantities are unknown — behind every estimate is one or more decisions. Because estimates are predictions, there is a nonzero probability that the actual outcome will differ; all estimates are inherently uncertain, and estimates need not be perfect but only good enough to lead the decision-maker to the right decision. The section quotes the Software Engineering Code of Ethics and Professional Practice clause 3.09 as requiring realistic quantitative estimates of cost, scheduling, personnel, quality and outcomes, with an uncertainty assessment of these estimates. The general techniques are: expert judgment (simplest, always available, least accurate; multiple expert estimates can feed group processes such as Wideband Delphi and Planning Poker); analogy (base the estimate on a similar thing's known actual result adjusted for listed differences; more accurate than expert judgment; requires a suitable analogy with known results); decomposition (bottom-up: break into pieces small enough to estimate, then sum, assuming over- and under-estimates cancel; fails if bottom-level estimates are biased, and cross-cutting factors such as integration and testing must be added); parametric (statistical relationship between the quantity and observable factors; typically most accurate, most defendable and easiest to use once the equation is developed and validated, which requires an adequate base of accurate historical data); and multiple estimates (estimate the same thing with different techniques and possibly different estimators; convergence suggests accuracy, divergence suggests overlooked factors and reestimation)." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-VALUE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 15 Introduction and Breakdown of Topics, printed pages 15-1 to 15-2." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The introduction's position statement is professional-BOK framing, not an empirical result." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 287-288; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The value-based position, the technical-decision/business-implication framing and the unquantifiable-criteria rule were confirmed for the value-based claim and the trade-off pattern's context." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-INTRO ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15's introduction takes the position that software engineering must be value-based, because neutral or negative value from an organization's investment in software is not sustainable. It frames economics as the science of choice rather than the science of money, quotes engineering as finding the balance between what is technically feasible and what is economically acceptable, and states that software technical decisions — such as an organization's decision to use a preexisting library or develop its own — may appear easy from a purely technical perspective yet can have serious implications for business viability. The economic perspective applies to decisions across the entire software product life cycle (from the pre-project decision to develop through end-of-life decisions) and at all levels of technical detail (buy-or-build, requirements scope, architecture and design, risk-based testing sufficiency, refactor-versus-redevelop, focus of maintenance). The KA also broadens the traditional financial view: value can derive from unquantifiables like corporate citizenship, employee well-being, environmental friendliness and customer loyalty, so software engineering decisions must also consider relevant unquantifiable criteria." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-ESTIMATION a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 section 2.3 (Effort, Schedule, and Cost Estimation), printed pages 9-8 to 9-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section surveys generally accepted management practice; it does not rank the named approaches' accuracy." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 208-209; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The error-prone characterization, the multiple-approach-and-reconcile advice and the calibrated-model/bottom-up combination were confirmed for the estimation claim, practice and decision model." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-ESTIMATION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.3 characterizes estimating costs for software projects as an error-prone process: the effort required depends almost entirely on human elements (individuals' experience and capabilities, team member interactions, development environment culture) and on dynamic environmental factors such as rapid technology evolution, changing and emergent requirements, and the intangible nature of the product. Software project managers should use multiple estimation approaches and reconcile the differences among the estimates. Where data is available, a calibrated estimation model based on historical size and effort data can determine an estimated effort range, and it is best to also use bottom-up estimation based on estimates from those who will accomplish the work. Estimation of effort, schedule and cost is an iterative activity negotiated and revised among affected stakeholders until consensus is reached; predictive SDLC projects typically schedule tasks during planning while adaptive SDLC projects derive overall effort/schedule estimates or constraints differently." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-MEASUREMENT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 section 6 (6.1-6.4 Software Engineering Measurement), printed pages 9-14 to 9-16." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section characterizes ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939; the standard itself is not a registered source, so its normative text is not cited." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 214-216; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The four-part measurement process, the information-need-driven measure selection with its judging criteria and the ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 attribution were confirmed for the measurement claim, concept, node and practice." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-MEASUREMENT ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 6 states that the section follows the ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 standard, which describes a process to define the activities and tasks necessary to implement a software measurement process. Establishing and sustaining measurement commitment requires measurement requirements guided by organizational objectives, an established scope (organizational unit and temporal scope, e.g. time series to calibrate estimation models), formally communicated team commitment and committed resources (responsibility allocation, funds, training, tools). Planning the measurement process requires characterizing the organizational unit, identifying information needs based on the unit's goals, constraints, risks and problems, selecting measures with clear links to those needs judged against criteria such as cost of collection, degree of process disruption, ease of obtaining accurate consistent data and ease of analysis, and defining data collection, analysis and reporting procedures plus evaluation criteria for information products. Performing the process integrates measurement procedures into the software processes they measure, collects, verifies and stores data, and communicates information products to users and stakeholders; evaluating the measurement process assesses information products and the process against the specified criteria and records lessons learned." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 sections 2.5 (Risk Management), 2.6 (Quality Management) and 2.7 (Plan Management), printed pages 9-9 to 9-10." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Quality-attribute enumeration in the section is illustrative ('not limited to'); no attribute taxonomy is adopted by this lane.", "Section 2.6 frames project quality management by reference to the PMBOK Guide's definition, which is itself not a registered source; the characterization cited here is SWEBOK's secondary statement." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-07 at PDF page indices 209-210; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The uncertainty/risk distinction, the risk-management cycle with register and periodic revisits, the quality-planning directions and the plan-magnitude risk rule were confirmed for the risk and quality claims, concepts, nodes and the risk practice." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.5 distinguishes uncertainty (a lack of information) from risk (the effect of uncertainty on objectives, with negative threats or positive opportunities), and presents risk management as identifying risk factors, analyzing the probability and potential impact of each factor, prioritizing factors and developing mitigation strategies that reduce the probability of a negative event and minimize its impact if it becomes a problem. Risk-management data, often kept in a risk register (a document serving as a repository for identified risks and additional information about each, usable for regulatory compliance), represents the project risk profile; assessment methods named include expert judgment, historical data, decision trees and process simulations; project abandonment conditions can be determined with stakeholders. Particular attention should be paid to risks related to software quality requirements such as safety or security, and risk management should be done not only at the beginning of a project but at periodic intervals throughout the project life cycle. Section 2.6 adds that software quality requirements for the project and its work products should be identified perhaps both quantitatively and qualitatively, that thresholds for acceptable quality measurements should be set for each quality requirement based on stakeholder needs and expectations, and that procedures for ongoing software quality assurance and quality improvement, verification and validation, reviews and audits should be specified during quality planning. Section 2.7 notes the magnitude of the planning effort and a plan's content should be determined partly by the risk of not developing the plan." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-INTRO a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 9 Introduction and Breakdown of Topics, printed pages 9-1 to 9-6." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide's authority limitations apply: generally accepted knowledge, not a uniform prescription.", "The introduction cites an external perception that software is often delivered late, over budget and of poor quality; this lane does not adopt that perception as an empirical finding." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF (SHA-256 0c9db4da0b2425c58cf778b99131eb31bb6c188e98b67163a549a1366d96e511) on 2026-11-07; text extracted at PDF page indices 201-206 and paraphrased originally." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The SEM definition, the measurement-informed management framing, the two working definitions and the seven-topic activity-based breakdown were confirmed for the professional-BOK scoped claims, principle and lane view." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-INTRO ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 defines software engineering management as the collection of work activities involved with planning, estimating, measuring, controlling, coordinating, leading and managing risk factors for a software project so that software products and engineering services are delivered efficiently, effectively and to the stakeholders' benefit, and treats project management and measurement management together because of their close relationship. The introduction asserts that measurement-informed management is a basic principle of any true engineering discipline — that management without measurement (qualitative and quantitative) suggests a lack of discipline and measurement without management suggests a lack of purpose or context — and adopts working definitions of management (a system of processes and controls to achieve the organization's strategic objectives) and measurement (assigning values and labels to work products, processes, resources and models derived from them). The chapter's breakdown is activity-based, listing seven topics (initiation and scope definition, project planning, project enactment, review and evaluation, closure, measurement, management tools) regardless of SDLC model." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-07G-SYNTHESIS-POINT-ESTIMATE a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "Cross-section inference combining directly inspected SWEBOK KA15 section 8 and KA9 section 2.3; this is not a sentence that appears in either section." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_type "SYNTHESIS" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical case evidence in this lane supports the severity of the anti-pattern's consequences.", "Synthesis inference, not a statement made by SWEBOK." ;
     se:method "Lane-level comparison of two verified evidence records (se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION, se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-ESTIMATION); the locator anchors the primary (KA15 section 8) leg of the synthesis." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "Provides the inference trail motivating the se:ANT-07G-POINT-ESTIMATE-COMMITMENT record." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION ;
     se:statement "Lane synthesis (SEK-07G): SWEBOK KA15 section 8 establishes that every estimate is an inherently uncertain prediction made to support a decision and that the quoted ethics clause requires an uncertainty assessment alongside quantitative estimates, while SWEBOK KA9 section 2.3 establishes that software effort/cost estimation is error-prone and advises multiple approaches with reconciliation. On that combined reading, presenting a single-point early estimate as a firm commitment — no range, no uncertainty assessment, no reconciliation, no later comparison against actuals — discards the uncertainty information both sections treat as constitutive of estimation and invites decisions to be made on precision that does not exist." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
     se:uncertainty "Whether real teams that commit to point estimates always incur the listed consequences is untested in this lane." .

