se:EVD-ARC-SWEBOK-DECISIONS-DEBT a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 2 sections 2.4 and 3 (3.1, 3.1.1, 3.2), printed pages 2-7 to 2-9." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The contexts are described qualitatively; the Guide asserts no quantitative measure of architectural debt here." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-06; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The decision/rationale/technical-debt framing and the architecture-versus-design contrast were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-SWEBOK-KA2-ARCH-DECISIONS-PROCESS ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA2 frames architectural design as a creative process whose outcome is a network of significant decisions: decisions affect quality attributes and trade-offs, their rationale (assumptions, alternatives considered, selection and rejection criteria) can be documented, and decisions deferred today can accumulate architectural technical debt that later work must pay. The chapter's process model situates architectural design across life-cycle contexts — traditional stages, product lines, Agile settings where architecture may only \"emerge\", and enterprise/system-of-systems baselines — and separates architecture's concern (shaping requirements and wide-ranging concerns) from design's focus on an established requirement set." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-ARC-SWEBOK-DESCRIPTION-VIEWS a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 2 section 2 (2.1, 2.2), printed pages 2-4 to 2-7 (the reference-architecture passage spans the 2-6/2-7 page break)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The section surveys generally accepted description practice; it does not prescribe a mandatory viewpoint set." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF (SHA-256 0c9db4da0b2425c58cf778b99131eb31bb6c188e98b67163a549a1366d96e511) on 2026-11-06; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The description/views/viewpoints vocabulary and the style/pattern/reference-architecture definitions were confirmed for the professional-BOK scoped claim, including the reference-architecture definition completing on printed page 2-7." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-SWEBOK-KA2-ARCH-DESCRIPTION ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA2 distinguishes a software system's architecture from its architecture description: for large, team-developed systems a tangible representation is needed, and architecture descriptions are the work products that document the architecture for stakeholders whose concerns the architecture answers. Descriptions commonly use multiple architecture views, each addressing specific concerns, with the conventions for each view documented as an architecture viewpoint; architectural styles and patterns are documented solution forms (an architectural style expresses a system's global organization and can be expressed as an architectural pattern; pattern catalogs coordinate them), and a reference architecture is an architecture constraining or guiding other architectures that provides a common basis for developing architectures for individual systems, product lines, families of systems and application domains, capturing commonalities to promote ease of development, integration, interoperability and other kinds of standardization." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

se:EVD-ARC-SWEBOK-DESIGN-STAGES a se:Evidence ;
     se:context "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a Chapter 3 sections 1 and 2 (1.1, 1.3, 1.4, 2.1, 2.2), printed pages 3-2 to 3-6." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_type "DIRECT_SOURCE_PARAPHRASE" ;
     se:extraction_status "EXTRACTED_PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The stage decomposition is a conceptual model; the Guide does not mandate stage-gate sequencing for every project." ;
     se:method "Direct inspection of the official SWEBOK v4.0a PDF on 2026-11-06; original paraphrase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:result "The three-stage decomposition and the high-level versus detailed design content split were confirmed." ;
     se:source_locator_ref se:LOC-SWEBOK-KA3-DESIGN-PROCESSES ;
     se:statement "SWEBOK v4.0a KA3 presents software design as a life-cycle activity with three stages — architectural design (treated in the Software Architecture KA), high-level or external-facing design, and detailed or internal-facing design. High-level design specifies the interaction of major components with one another and the environment (external events and messages, data formats and protocols, ordering and timing, end-to-end transactions, data persistence) within the envelope established by the architecture; detailed design proceeds within the high-level design's constraints and specifies components' internal modules, responsibilities, states, algorithms and data structures. Key issues and design principles apply across the stages." ;
     se:synthetic "false"^^xsd:boolean .

