     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:definition "The \"Respond to Vulnerabilities\" (RV) practice group of NIST SP 800-218 v1.1, comprising three practices: RV.1 identify and confirm vulnerabilities on an ongoing basis, RV.2 assess, prioritize and remediate vulnerabilities, and RV.3 analyze vulnerabilities to identify their root causes." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0011 ;
     se:label "SSDF Respond to Vulnerabilities group" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:node_kind "PRACTICE_GROUP" ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:view_ref se:VIEW-SSDF-SECURITY .

se:PAT-07A-INCREMENTAL-DELIVERY a se:Pattern ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:CON-07A-SLCM ;
     se:consequences "Earlier stakeholder feedback and reduced uncertainty compared with working long stretches without deliverables.", "The deliverable contains the necessary and sufficient capability to be considered complete only after the final iteration (per the Guide's incremental definition)." ;
     se:context "A software product whose requirements understanding increases as the team works, developed under a life cycle that permits repeated cycles; SWEBOK KA10 presents incremental and iterative life cycles and continuous development as established paradigms for exactly this situation." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-LIFECYCLE-MODELS, se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION ;
     se:forces "Early stakeholder feedback versus iteration coordination overhead.", "Per-iteration deliverable completeness versus overall completeness only after the final iteration.", "Predictive scope stability versus changing understanding." ;
     se:label "Incremental delivery within an iterative life cycle" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Long-running large processes without intermediate deliverables increase uncertainty, and product understanding grows while the project runs, so a single late delivery compounds estimation error and feedback latency." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 sections 2.4, 2.5 and 2.9 (paradigm definitions and the no-deliverables lesson); one paradigm among several the Guide presents, not a universal prescription." ;
     se:solution "Structure the life cycle as a series of iterations that successively add functionality within agreed time frames (an incremental life cycle, per the Guide's paradigm definitions), keeping metrics and measure definitions realistic so process and product estimations track status; in Agile-family models the same structure appears as small incremental deliveries with requirements open to change." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Incremental development requires requirements definition, design, implementation and testing to occur in an overlapping, iterative manner rather than a strict sequence; integration discipline grows with iteration count." .

se:PAT-07B-DESIGN-BY-CONTRACT a se:Pattern ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07B-DESIGN-BY-CONTRACT ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:PRA-07B-INTERFACE-DOCUMENTATION ;
     se:consequences "Clarified routine behavior and faster flushing out of mismatched interface assumptions and modification-introduced errors (stated for assertions in SWEBOK 4.4).", "SWEBOK hedges that design by contract is thought to improve the quality of software construction." ;
     se:context "Construction of routines and modules whose interface expectations can be stated precisely, in an environment where runtime checks can be compiled in during development and compiled out afterwards, as described in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 4.4." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-DBC-ERROR ;
     se:forces "Precision of stated semantics versus contract authoring effort.", "Runtime checking cost versus earlier fault exposure." ;
     se:label "Design by contract at routine level" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Routine-level semantics left implicit allow mismatched interface assumptions between callers and callees that surface late, and errors that creep in when code is modified go unnoticed at the boundary." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 4.4 at professional-BOK fidelity; the quality-effect claim remains hedged as in the source." ;
     se:solution "Include preconditions and postconditions for each routine so each routine or class forms a contract with the rest of the program that precisely specifies its semantics; implement the checks as assertions that are compiled into the code at development time and compiled out later so they do not degrade performance." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Contract authoring and maintenance effort; production runs without the development-time checks once assertions are compiled out." .

se:PAT-07B-INCREMENTAL-INTEGRATION a se:Pattern ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION ;
     se:consequences "Additional test infrastructure (stubs, drivers, mocks) must be built and maintained.", "The advantages SWEBOK names for incremental integration: easier error location, improved progress monitoring, earlier product delivery and improved customer relations." ;
     se:context "A construction effort in which parts are built and tested separately and the team can choose the integration sequence and interim scaffolding, as described in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 3.7." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-INTEGRATION ;
     se:forces "Early usable feedback versus degree of pre-integration testing.", "Error-location ease versus scaffolding effort.", "Progress monitoring versus integration ceremony." ;
     se:label "Incremental integration" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Individually constructed routines, classes, components and subsystems must be combined into a single system while keeping errors locatable and progress visible during construction." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 3.7 at professional-BOK fidelity; the advantage list is hedged in the source ('thought to offer')." ;
     se:solution "Write and test the program in small pieces and combine the pieces one at a time, building and integrating a unit (for example a class or component) at a time, supported by additional test infrastructure such as stubs, drivers and mock objects, so the construction process provides early feedback to developers and customers." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Scaffolding and interim-version testing cost is traded against late-event risk concentration." .

se:PAT-07C-SECURITY-PATTERN-REUSE a se:Pattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-PATTERNS ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:CON-07C-THREAT-MODEL ;
     se:consequences "Design decisions are anchored to documented problem/solution pairs, easing independent design review (SSDF PW.2-style).", "Selected patterns must still be validated against the software's actual threats; a pattern is not a guarantee." ;
     se:context "Secure design work where prevention and tolerance concerns (unauthorized disclosure or access, damage limitation, failing and recovering securely, access control, cryptographic material handling) must be specified, per the SWEBOK KA13 4.2 design concerns." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-DESIGN, se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-PATTERNS ;
     se:forces "Adoption cost of a pattern catalog versus ad hoc design.", "Provenness of a documented solution versus fit to the specific context.", "Reviewability: a named pattern gives reviewers a shared reference.", "Threat specificity: a pattern mitigates the threats it was proven against, not all threats." ;
     se:label "Security-pattern-based design" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Recurring security problems in software design need proven, reviewable solutions rather than ad hoc re-derivation for every project." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in the SWEBOK KA13 4.3 security-pattern definition at professional-BOK fidelity; the selection workflow is lane operationalization, not a source-prescribed procedure." ;
     se:solution "Express recurring security problems and their well-proven generic solutions as security patterns (recurring problem in a specific context plus well-proven generic solution, per SWEBOK KA13 4.3), and select and adapt documented patterns when specifying the security design, recording the fit decision with the threat model so reviewers can check pattern-to-threat alignment." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Pattern generality trades against exact context fit; adaptation must be recorded or the pattern's provenness claim breaks." .

se:PAT-07D-CONTEXT-DEPENDENT-FAULT-RESPONSE a se:Pattern ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:CON-07D-FAULT-TOLERANCE ;
     se:consequences "Failover automation has deterministic logic to execute because the response was designed in.", "The failure response matches what each failure actually costs, rather than a uniform policy." ;
     se:context "A service whose failures have domain-specific consequences — the CS2023 curriculum's contrasting cases are a flight-critical system, where crashing is strongly avoided, and a data-processing system, where crashing before corrupt data reaches storage is highly valuable — being designed with SWEBOK's requirement that failure-handling logic be planned during development." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-FAULT-TOLERANCE-CONTEXT, se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE ;
     se:forces "Automated failover presupposes failure-handling logic built into the application.", "Consequences of continued operation versus consequences of stopping differ per domain.", "The failure response must be chosen before the failure occurs, during development." ;
     se:label "Consequence-matched failure response" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "A software system needs a failure response, but the right response differs by what the failure costs in that domain: the same crash that is unacceptable in one context is the safest available action in another." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Pattern shaped from CS2023 SE-Reliability Non-core topic 8a's contextual contrast plus SWEBOK KA6 section 2.5's planned failure-handling logic; the two consequence cases are the curriculum's illustrations." ;
     se:solution "Classify the service's failure modes by consequence (corruption risk, safety risk, availability loss), then select and build the matched response for each class — fail-safe stop where corruption or safety dominates, failover-continued operation where availability dominates — implementing the chosen responses as planned failure-handling logic so automated failover daemons can execute them." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Consequence classification takes design effort up front; a uniform response is cheaper to build but misfits at least one consequence class." .

se:PAT-07D-SLA-ANCHORED-AVAILABILITY a se:Pattern ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-SLA ;
     se:consequences "Availability becomes a managed, evidenced property with a defined target and an investigation path.", "The SLA turns diffuse customer expectations into checkable operations obligations." ;
     se:context "A software service with customers and agreed commitments, entering a project where SWEBOK KA6 expects availability and continuity to be defined as nonfunctional requirements early, with infrastructure planned, designed, implemented and tested for them." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-AVAILABILITY-SLA ;
     se:forces "Availability commitments exist (customer expectations) but are ambiguous until documented in an SLA.", "Stakeholders need indicators they can read against the agreed targets.", "Unplanned nonavailability is only actionable if it is detected and investigated." ;
     se:label "SLA-anchored availability management loop" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "A operated service must meet customer availability commitments, but availability is invisible unless it is defined early, measured, and compared against an agreed target." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.3 fidelity; a professional-BOK pattern for operated services, not a universal mandate." ;
     se:solution "Define availability and continuity early as nonfunctional requirements; agree and document the SLA that clarifies the full range of operations obligations; build the supporting infrastructure; then run the loop — measure and record availability, investigate unplanned nonavailability and act, monitor workload/performance/availability trends via the service-level management process, and report availability and continuity indicators against the service-level targets." ;
     se:tradeoffs "SLA definition and indicator reporting add coordination overhead; without them, availability disputes have no shared reference." .

se:PAT-07E-PIPELINE-GATED-DELIVERY a se:Pattern ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07E-CONTINUOUS-INTEGRATION ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:consequences "Delivered artifacts carry documented contents and reproducible provenance.", "Promotion decisions and verification results are tied to specific built versions.", "The current head/trunk build-and-test state is continuously available as ground truth for the shared code." ;
     se:context "A multi-developer project with a shared source-control repository and a delivery path of environments (verification and production), as described in the SWEBOK KA8 6.1/6.2 continuous-integration and continuous-delivery pipeline descriptions and the CS2023 SE-Tools build-systems/CI topics." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-RELEASE-MGMT ;
     se:forces "Artifact provenance: packaged contents must be known and traceable (SBOM, version description documentation).", "Recovery safety: promotion should preserve a way back (rollback support).", "Reproducibility: the build must construct correct CI versions with appropriate configuration data so artifacts can be re-created.", "Verification depth versus speed: more checks slow the pipeline while faster feedback favors fewer.", "Verification freshness: every commit should be checked, not only scheduled integration points." ;
     se:label "Commit-triggered pipeline with gated artifact promotion" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Changes integrated into a shared source need automated build-and-test verification before their artifacts flow toward delivery, and verified artifacts need to be packaged with their contents documented and deployed into environments consistently." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Pattern packaging is lane-level structure over the directly inspected SWEBOK KA8 6.1/6.2 pipeline descriptions and the CS2023 SE-Tools CI/build topics; it is not a named pattern in either source." ;
     se:solution "Establish a pipeline that is initiated automatically on every commit to the target branch: retrieve a fresh copy of the complete source, build with the appropriate configuration data, run the configured automated checks (static analysis, unit tests, coverage), produce versioned artifacts with their contents documented (for example an SBOM and release documentation), and promote the packaged artifacts into an environment for verification or production use only through the pipeline's gates." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Heavier gate checks increase confidence but delay delivery feedback.", "Pipeline construction and maintenance cost is traded for manual-verification reduction." .

se:PAT-07F-DUAL-API-LARGE-SCALE-REFACTORING a se:Pattern ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:consequences "Consumers migrate incrementally instead of in one forced cutover.", "Smaller behavior changes are easier to evaluate as backward compatible, consistent with the unit's relational compatibility topic.", "The dual-API period itself carries maintenance cost until the old API is retired." ;
     se:context "An evolving interface or subsystem whose consumers are not all visible or not all controllable at once, per the CS2023 SE-Refactoring Non-core large-scale refactoring topic, whose rationale contrasts 'API consumers I can see' with 'consumers I can't see'." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU ;
     se:forces "Behavior changes should stay small enough to evaluate for compatibility.", "Migration coordination across repositories costs time and organization.", "Old and new forms must both remain usable while consumers migrate.", "Unseen consumers may depend on implicit interface behavior (Hyrum's Law topic of the same unit)." ;
     se:label "Dual-API coexistence for large-scale refactoring" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "A needed refactoring is too large to commit safely, or the change cannot be synchronized between the provider and all its consumers (multiple repositories, consumers with private code), so a single atomic breaking change is not feasible." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Refactoring Non-core topic 5 (elective curriculum scope); a curriculum-named technique pair, not a lane-invented procedure." ;
     se:solution "Express both the old and the new APIs so that they can co-exist during migration, and minimize the size of behavior changes, per the CS2023 Non-core techniques for large-scale refactoring." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Coexistence plumbing adds temporary complexity that must eventually be removed; the curriculum topic names the techniques without prescribing the retirement timeline." .

se:PAT-07G-MULTI-ATTRIBUTE-TRADEOFF a se:Pattern ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS ;
     se:consequences "Alternatives are compared on a declared, common basis, making the decision auditable.", "High-consequence decisions leave a recorded justification trail.", "Unquantifiable criteria stay in the decision instead of being silently monetized or dropped." ;
     se:context "An engineering decision, at any level of technical detail, with more than one reasonable technically feasible alternative and selection criteria that cannot all be expressed in money (SWEBOK KA15 sections 2.3-2.6, with the decision examples from the KA15 introduction)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS ;
     se:forces "Some criteria (unquantifiables such as well-being or loyalty) resist objective, let alone monetary, expression.", "The best solution must be among the candidates before it can be selected; adding candidates raises the chance but also the cost.", "The greater the consequences of a wrong decision, the more criteria and care are warranted.", "Too many criteria make the analysis unwieldy; too few fail to differentiate alternatives and can force a suboptimal choice." ;
     se:label "Common-basis multi-attribute trade-off evaluation" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Technical alternatives — buy or build, architecture options, refactor versus redevelop, how much risk-based testing is enough — must be compared on money and on non-monetary criteria without smuggling in inconsistent comparisons that predetermine the winner." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Structured from SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 sections 2.3-2.6 and 6 (printed pages 15-5 to 15-7, 15-9 to 15-10) at BOK fidelity; the specific technique lists are the Guide's enumeration, not a comparative effectiveness study." ;
     se:solution "Follow the KA15 section 2 structure: enumerate all reasonable technically feasible solutions (using prototyping and peer review to verify feasibility and to surface more candidates); define the selection criteria as objectively as possible, explicitly carrying unquantifiable criteria; evaluate every alternative against every criterion from the same viewpoint — the same basis for comparison, the same planning horizon, the same kinds of costs and incomes; and select using compensatory techniques that collapse criteria into a single figure of merit (for example additive weighting or the analytic hierarchy process) or non-compensatory techniques that refuse trade-offs (dominance, satisficing, lexicography), recording the selected alternative and its justification when consequences are high, and monitoring the selected alternative's performance afterward." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Common planning horizons can require arbitrary cut-offs for proposals with different natural lifespans.", "Compensatory techniques let a low score on one criterion be offset by high scores elsewhere, which can mask a fatal weakness; non-compensatory techniques avoid that masking but discard aggregate ordering information." .

se:PAT-ARC-LAYERED-ARCHITECTURE a se:Pattern ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-ARC-SEPARATION-OF-CONCERNS ;
     se:consequences "The curriculum lists layered design as one paradigm among four and n-layer as one standard architecture; it does not elaborate consequences, so none are asserted beyond that framing." ;
     se:context "Software architecture design as framed by CS2023 SE-Design: top-down functional decomposition/layered design is listed as a design paradigm (CS Core topic 2a.i) and n-layer is listed among the standard architectures (CS Core topic 2b). The curriculum anchors the pattern at the architecture abstraction level, distinct from detailed design." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0003, se:EVD-ARC-0004 ;
     se:forces "Component boundaries and dependencies must be identifiable (CS Core topic 2c).", "Separation of concerns across levels of abstraction (CS Core topic 1b)." ;
     se:label "Layered architecture" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:problem "A system must be organized so that responsibilities at different levels of abstraction can be described and reasoned about separately." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope; pattern shape assembled from curriculum topic names plus boundary-concept reasoning." ;
     se:solution "Organize the system as an ordered set of layers in which each layer addresses a level of abstraction and depends on the layers below it, following the layered design paradigm named by the curriculum." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Design tradeoffs such as efficiency vs reliability are evaluated separately (Non-core topic 13); the curriculum attributes no specific tradeoffs to layered design." .

se:PAT-REQ-USER-STORY-SPECIFICATION a se:Pattern ;
     se:RELATED_TO se:CON-REQ-QUALITY-ATTRIBUTE ;
     se:consequences "A uniform, stakeholder-readable functional requirement set whose entries can be traced into design and implementation work.", "The choice of method explicitly shapes how well the specification supports later design and implementation." ;
     se:context "A team specifying the functional scope of a new product or feature chooses among documented structuring methods such as use cases and user stories, per the CS2023 SE-Requirements instruction to use at least one such method." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-FUNCTIONAL-REQ-METHODS ;
     se:forces "Each specific approach has its own strengths and weaknesses.", "Stakeholder readability versus implementation traceability.", "Team familiarity differs across methods.", "The chosen method must support design and implementation, not only description." ;
     se:label "User-story-structured functional specification" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:problem "Functional requirements need a documentation and structuring method that stakeholders can read and that designers and implementers can trace to their work." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Requirements KA Core topic 1 curriculum scope (use cases / user stories as the named example methods); not a claim that user stories dominate other methods." ;
     se:solution "Express each functional requirement as a short user story (actor, capability, purpose) — or a comparable structured unit — and keep the chosen structure uniform across the requirement set, following the curriculum's example methods for documenting and structuring functional requirements." ;
     se:tradeoffs "User stories are lightweight and readable but carry less structured interaction detail than full use cases; use cases capture more detail at higher documentation cost." .

se:PAT-TVV-REGRESSION-SAFETY-NET a se:Pattern ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-TVV-TEST-COVERAGE ;
     se:consequences "Effectiveness depends on reproducible defect records and on the tests actually encoding the original failure.", "Reintroduction of previously fixed defects is caught automatically by the suite.", "The suite grows with every recorded defect, raising maintenance and runtime cost." ;
     se:context "Evolving software where components must remain functional as the code changes (CS2023 SE-Validation CS Core topic 2), with discovered vulnerabilities and defects recorded and triaged in an issue tracking system (NIST PW.8.2)." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0006, se:EVD-TVV-0010 ;
     se:forces "Evolution pressure versus functional stability: testing matters precisely because code keeps changing.", "Issue records hold defect knowledge but cannot fail a build.", "Testing effort is finite and, in the SSDF context, risk-governed." ;
     se:label "Regression safety net from reported defects" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:problem "Defects that have been fixed can be silently reintroduced as the code evolves, because the knowledge that a specific failure existed is recorded in prose but is not executable." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Cross-source pattern record: NIST notional example plus CS2023 curriculum framing; the NIST example is explicitly neither required nor exhaustive, so this is a candidate pattern pending real-case evidence." ;
     se:solution "Turn recorded defect knowledge into executable checks by incorporating tests for previously reported vulnerabilities or defects into the project's test suite, so that the same errors are not reintroduced (grounded in NIST PW.8.2's notional Example 3, supported by the CS2023 evolution rationale for testing)." ;
     se:tradeoffs "Growing suite runtime versus protection against reintroduction; sits inside CS2023's coverage versus throughput/latency tradeoff (KA Core topic 8d)." .

se:PRA-07A-GQM-ASSESSMENT a se:Practice ;
     se:applicability "Teams assessing or improving software engineering processes, including retrospective-driven improvement in Agile life cycles." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07A-ASSESSMENT-EMPIRICAL ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Following SWEBOK KA10 section 3: set goals that can be measured, change something, and then evaluate the effect of the change — an improvement has occurred when the evaluation is positive; framework-based assessments (process reference model plus process assessment model) and Agile retrospectives instantiate the same empirical loop." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-ASSESSMENT ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Process decisions (choosing, assessing, improving) supported by empirical evidence." ;
     se:intent "Ground process-assessment and improvement decisions in empirical evidence rather than opinion." ;
     se:label "Goal-question-metric style process assessment" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A running process whose goals can be stated measurably.", "Willingness to introduce a change and re-measure." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Unrealistic or imprecise measurement definitions undermine the evaluation." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 section 3 (3.1-3.4) at professional-BOK fidelity." ;
     se:steps "Define the questions and metrics that will indicate whether the goal is met.", "Evaluate the effect using the defined metrics; retain the change as an improvement only when the evaluation is positive.", "Introduce the planned change.", "State the improvement goal in measurable terms." .

se:PRA-07A-LIFECYCLE-ADAPTATION a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07A-KA10-SWE-PROCESS ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:PRI-07A-TAILOR-DONT-ADOPT ;
     se:applicability "Any project defining or tailoring a software life cycle; normative constraints may bound the tailoring space." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07A-ADAPTATION-DOCUMENTED ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Following SWEBOK KA10's adaptation topic: identify the differential characteristics of the software system and the stakeholders' needs, select the appropriate standards or organization-internal documents, select a development strategy/life cycle model, stages and processes, and document the decisions and rationale; the adaptation need not keep the standard names or include every process." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A project life cycle in conformance with a selected model, adapted to the system's characteristics, with documented rationale.", "Measurement definitions able to report process and product status during execution." ;
     se:intent "Fit the project life cycle to the software system and organization instead of adopting a model wholesale, and leave an auditable record of the fit decisions." ;
     se:label "Life cycle tailoring with documented rationale" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "Access to candidate standards or internal process documents.", "Knowledge of the product characteristics (e.g., stakeholders' needs, product size or complexity) and of the organizational context." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Working with large processes without producing deliverables along the way increases uncertainty.", "Wrong or uncertain estimations in the context of the life cycle will lead to failure (KA10 practical considerations)." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 sections 2.8-2.9 at professional-BOK fidelity." ;
     se:steps "Identify the relevant characteristics of the software system and its stakeholders.", "Record the adaptation decisions and their rationale; drop or rename elements deliberately rather than silently.", "Select the appropriate standards or organization-internal documents.", "Select the development strategy and life cycle model, the stages and the processes.", "Support the tailored life cycle with metrics/measure definitions that yield realistic process and product estimations and measurements." .

se:PRA-07A-MULTI-VIEW-MODELING a se:Practice ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07A-ABSTRACTION-LEVELS ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:PRI-07A-KEEP-VIEWS-DISTINCT ;
     se:applicability "Modeling software at any life cycle phase; the practice is independent of which life cycle model the project uses." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07A-MODELS-STRUCTURE-SE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Following SWEBOK KA11's modeling principles (model the essentials; provide perspective; enable effective communications) and KA18's alternate abstractions: for each concern choose a view with its own notation and vocabulary, abstract away nonessential information, and keep the resulting alternate abstractions complementary and synchronized." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA11-MODELING-PRINCIPLES, se:EVD-07A-KA18-DESIGN-ABSTRACTION ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A set of complementary views whose union describes exactly the selected aspects needed to make the motivating decisions." ;
     se:intent "Keep different engineering concerns visible in their own views instead of overloading a single model." ;
     se:label "Multi-perspective modeling with synchronized views" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A chosen modeling notation per view.", "Identified stakeholders and a decision about which aspects or features pose the questions driving the modeling effort." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "A single model can give the illusion of complete understanding (KA11 cautions models are abstractions with missing information).", "Keeping alternate abstractions in sync is sometimes difficult (KA18)." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA11 sections 1.1-1.2 plus KA18 sections 2-3 (alternate abstractions) at inspected-section fidelity." ;
     se:steps "Choose the views (e.g., structural, behavioral) appropriate to those concerns.", "Determine which aspects or features pose the questions that motivate the model.", "Keep alternate abstractions consistent; review synchronization whenever any view changes.", "Model only the essentials in each view, abstracting nonessential information.", "Record the assumptions each view makes about its context so reuse can validate them." .

se:PRA-07B-CONSTRUCT-FOR-VERIFICATION a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:SUB-07B-SOFTWARE-CONSTRUCTION ;
     se:applicability "Construction work whose output will be reviewed, unit-tested or operated by others." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Apply the four technique families SWEBOK KA4 1.3 names for constructing for verification: follow coding standards that support code reviews and unit testing; organize code so it supports automated testing; restrict the use of complex or difficult-to-understand language structures; and record software behaviors with logs." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-CONSTRUCT-FOR-VERIFICATION ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Faults findable by the code's authors during construction as well as by independent testers and users." ;
     se:intent "Build code so that faults can be readily found by the engineers writing it and by testers and users during independent testing and operational activities." ;
     se:label "Construct for verification" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A coding standard is adopted or adaptable to review and unit-testing needs.", "A logging facility is available whose output can be inspected during testing and operations.", "Automated testing is available or planned for the constructed units." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Logging adds noise and runtime cost if levels are not chosen deliberately.", "Restricting language constructs can exclude justified advanced uses if applied without review.", "Standards and structure work add upfront effort that may be misallocated on trivial code." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 1.3 (printed page 4-4) at professional-BOK fidelity; the Guide names the technique families without thresholds." ;
     se:steps "Align the coding standard with what code reviews and unit tests need to consume (naming, structure, size).", "Constrain the use of complex or difficult-to-understand language constructs to cases where they are justified and documented.", "Organize the code into units and seams that automated tests can exercise in isolation.", "Record software behaviors with logs at a level usable for fault finding during testing and operations." .

se:PRA-07B-INTERFACE-DOCUMENTATION a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07B-CS2023-SE-CONSTRUCTION ;
     se:applicability "Constructed modules whose interfaces are consumed by other developers or reviewers." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "For each constructed module interface, document the interface requirements, potentially including formal or informal contracts, pre and post conditions and invariants, following the CS2023 SE-Construction documentation topic; keep implementation documentation focused on tricky and non-obvious code, clarifying dataflow and computation, and prefer refactoring toward self-explanatory code over comments that restate obvious operations." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-CS2023-CONSTRUCTION-KU ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Implementation documentation concentrated on genuinely tricky and non-obvious code.", "Interface expectations inspectable by consumers and reviewers independently of the implementation." ;
     se:intent "Make a module's interface expectations explicit enough that consumers and reviewers can check use against stated behavior." ;
     se:label "Contract-style interface documentation" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A module interface whose behavior can be stated as expectations on inputs, outputs and state.", "An agreed notation for contracts, pre/post conditions and invariants (formal or informal)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Contracts drift from the implementation when either changes without the other.", "Full formal contracts may exceed the value on small or stable interfaces." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes CS2023 SE-Construction CS Core topic 2 (subtopics 2a and 2b) at curriculum fidelity; a teaching-scope practice, not a professional documentation standard." ;
     se:steps "Enumerate the interface's operations with their inputs, outputs and observable effects.", "Review implementation comments against the non-obvious-only rule, deleting restatements of common operations.", "State contracts, pre and post conditions and invariants for those operations where applicable.", "Where a tricky piece can be refactored to be self-explanatory, refactor before documenting it." .

se:PRA-07B-STATIC-ANALYSIS-INTEGRATION a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07B-CS2023-TOOLS-ANALYSIS-INTEGRATION ;
     se:applicability "Teams constructing code in languages with usable analysis tooling." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07B-CS2023-TOOLS-ANALYSIS-INTEGRATION ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Select static and dynamic analysis tools appropriate to the language and platform, wire them into the editor/IDE and build workflow, and triage their findings in the team's review process; CS2023 SE-Tools frames the curriculum expectation as describing how such tools can be integrated into the software development environment, and SWEBOK KA4 3.6 lists static analysis among the primary construction-quality techniques." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07B-CS2023-SE-TOOLS-ANALYSIS, se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-QUALITY-TECHNIQUES ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Code-level faults and quality indications visible during construction rather than after release." ;
     se:intent "Surface code-level faults and code-quality indications early by making analysis tooling part of the environment where code is written and built." ;
     se:label "Integrate static and dynamic analysis into the development environment" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A place in the developer workflow (editor, build, or both) where tool output is actually seen.", "Analysis tooling available for the project's language(s) and platform(s)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "High false-positive rates can erode trust and bury important findings.", "Tool sprawl adds maintenance cost without process ownership." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes the CS2023 SE-Tools tool-integration expectation with SWEBOK KA4 3.6's static-analysis placement as the professional-BOK counterpart; selection and triage detail is lane-added procedure, not source text." ;
     se:steps "Integrate the tools into the development environment so findings appear where code is written or built.", "Select static and dynamic analysis tools matching the language, platform and risk profile.", "Triage findings with the review workflow, distinguishing blocking from advisory results." .

se:PRA-07C-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS-BASELINE a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07C-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07C-SECURITY-REQUIREMENT ;
     se:applicability "Organizations and projects able to maintain a requirements record; practice depth remains risk-based." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07C-NIST-SSDF-REQUIREMENTS-CONTINUOUS, se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS-SCOPE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Identify and document security requirements for the software and its development from internal sources (policies, business objectives, risk management strategy) and external sources (laws, regulations), informed by threat analysis (misuse and abuse cases), maintain them over time, verify compliance at key SDLC points, and communicate them to third-party component suppliers including provenance and integrity expectations — per SSDF PO.1 with the SWEBOK 4.1 activity framing." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PO1-REQUIREMENTS, se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A living security-requirements baseline traceable into design, implementation and verification.", "Reduced duplicated requirements effort through a shared, collected-once requirements record." ;
     se:intent "Keep a documented, current baseline of security requirements that informs every SDLC stage and links requirements to design, implementation and verification." ;
     se:label "Maintained security-requirements baseline" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A named owner responsible for maintaining the baseline.", "Access to internal policy and external regulatory requirement sources.", "Threat and risk inputs available for elicitation (misuse/abuse cases, risk assessments)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Approved exceptions accumulate without periodic re-evaluation.", "Over-broad baselines are ignored or waived informally.", "The baseline drifts without an owner or periodic review." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SSDF practice PO.1 (tasks PO.1.1-PO.1.3) and the SWEBOK KA13 4.1 activity framing at recommendation fidelity; not a mandated process." ;
     se:steps "Communicate the requirements to third-party component suppliers, including provenance and integrity verification expectations.", "Document and prioritize the requirements with source provenance.", "Elicit security requirements from internal and external sources, including misuse- and abuse-case threat analysis.", "Review the baseline at least periodically and on major vulnerability or incident events; revisit on software product revision.", "Verify compliance with the baseline at key SDLC checkpoints and record results." .

se:PRA-07C-SECURITY-TESTING-DUAL-CHECK a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07C-SECURITY-VERIFICATION ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING-PURPOSES ;
     se:applicability "Verification stages of software with a maintained security-requirements baseline." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING-PURPOSES ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Derive security checks from the security-requirements baseline (general testing methods) and complement them with security-specific methods: static analysis of source code and compiled binaries, and authorized dynamic testing such as vulnerability assessment or penetration testing — operated and configured by security-skilled people, with results verified rather than trusted, and with dynamic testing kept within legal boundaries and proper authorization — per SWEBOK KA13 4.5, triaging findings through the vulnerability knowledge bases named in 4.6." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-TESTING, se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-VULNERABILITY-DATABASES ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A recorded trail from requirements to verification results.", "Evidence at release that security requirements are met and known vulnerabilities were checked for." ;
     se:intent "Verify both that implemented software meets its security requirements and that it contains none of the known vulnerabilities, using expertise-governed static and authorized dynamic methods." ;
     se:label "Dual-purpose security verification" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A security-requirements baseline providing the compliance target.", "Access to static analysis tooling (source and binary) and security expertise to operate it.", "Explicit authorization and legal clearance for dynamic testing." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Checking tools cannot find all vulnerabilities (for example those in hard-to-produce states); automation alone yields false confidence.", "Requirements compliance and vulnerability absence are different checks; doing one does not cover the other.", "Unauthorized dynamic testing is unlawful; authorization boundaries must be explicit." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes the SWEBOK KA13 4.5 dual-purpose framing and 4.6 knowledge bases at professional-BOK fidelity; test-execution mechanics are SEK-06C territory." ;
     se:steps "Conduct authorized dynamic testing (vulnerability assessment, penetration testing) where the risk context warrants.", "Derive requirements-compliance security checks from the baseline.", "Feed confirmed findings into vulnerability handling and, where requirements fail, back into the baseline and design record.", "Run static analysis on source and compiled binaries; configure tools with security expertise and verify results.", "Triage findings against known-vulnerability knowledge bases (CVE, CWE, CAPEC) and record severity (e.g., CVSS-style scoring)." .

se:PRA-07C-THREAT-MODELING-AT-DESIGN a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07C-SECURE-DESIGN ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07C-NIST-SECURE-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:applicability "Design-stage security work where risks can be modeled before implementation; depth of modeling remains risk-based." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07C-NIST-SECURE-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "During design, model how the software is likely to be attacked — threat modeling, attack modeling or attack surface mapping — determine how the design and architecture should mitigate the identified risks (including access control and cryptographic material handling), justify and record any relaxation or waiver of security requirements, and periodically re-evaluate approved exceptions — per SSDF PW.1 with the SWEBOK 4.2 design concerns and threat-modeling purpose." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07C-NIST-PW1-SECURE-BY-DESIGN, se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-DESIGN ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A threat-informed security design whose risk responses and design decisions are recorded for later review and maintenance." ;
     se:intent "Surface likely operational security risks early and drive the design and architecture to mitigate them, with an auditable record of risks and design decisions." ;
     se:label "Design-time threat modeling with recorded risk responses" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A security-requirements baseline to design against.", "Knowledge of the data types the software will interact with (data classification).", "Stakeholder capacity to act on identified risks during design." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Modeling effort can exceed the value of the decisions it informs.", "Models go stale after design changes unless re-evaluation is scheduled.", "Unrecorded threat assumptions create false confidence." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SSDF practice PW.1 (tasks PW.1.1-PW.1.2) and the SWEBOK KA13 4.2 threat-modeling-to-design path at recommendation fidelity." ;
     se:steps "Build the threat/attack-surface model of how the system is attacked.", "Classify the data the software interacts with and identify high-risk areas (sensitive data, identification, authentication, access control, credential management).", "Determine design and architecture mitigations for the identified risks.", "Re-evaluate all approved exceptions periodically and after design changes.", "Record the response to each risk, mitigation rationales and any approved requirement exceptions with their justifications." .

se:PRA-07D-CAPACITY-PLANNING a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-FORECASTING ;
     se:applicability "Operated services with agreed service-level targets and observable workload." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-FORECASTING ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Translate business predictions and workload estimates into specific documented capacity requirements; capture and analyze data on current and previous components and on resource utilization; size and model new and changed services; and produce a recurring capacity plan (SWEBOK relays at least annually) documenting actual infrastructure performance, expected requirements and costed options for achieving the service-level targets in the SLA." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-CAPACITY-MGMT ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A documented, periodically refreshed capacity plan tied to SLA targets.", "Predictable reaction to workload or environment variation." ;
     se:intent "Ensure the service has capacity, at all times, to meet current and future agreed demand, and make capacity reactions predictable." ;
     se:label "Forecast-driven capacity planning with costed options" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "Access to workload/business forecasts and to component and resource-utilization data.", "Agreed service-level targets (SLA) against which capacity options are judged." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Costed options can favor short-term savings over the agreed targets.", "Forecasts can be wrong; a plan without utilization data drifts into guesswork." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.4 (relaying ISO/IEC/IEEE 20000-1) at professional-BOK fidelity; the ISO standard's own text is not admitted." ;
     se:steps "Capture and analyze component and resource-utilization data at an appropriate level.", "Produce the capacity plan with actual performance, expected requirements and costed options; recommend the option that meets the agreed targets.", "Size and model new and changed services against the data.", "Translate business predictions and workload estimates into specific, documented requirements.", "Understand current and expected business requirements for the service in terms of what the business must deliver." .

se:PRA-07D-DEFECT-INJECTION-REMOVAL a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07D-HIGH-RELIABILITY-DEFECT-MINIMIZATION ;
     se:applicability "Development contexts pursuing high reliability through defect minimization; the curriculum does not rank the levers." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07D-HIGH-RELIABILITY-DEFECT-MINIMIZATION ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Pursue high reliability through a minimum-defect goal: reduce defect injection through better training, education and planning, and remove most injected defects through testing, code review and comparable removal approaches, treating injection and removal as a cycle rather than a one-time phase, as presented in the CS2023 SE-Reliability unit." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-DEFECT-INJECTION-REMOVAL ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A declining share of injected defects surviving to operation.", "Recorded linkage between prevention/removal activities and observed defect classes." ;
     se:intent "Raise software reliability by minimizing the defects that cause failures, working both levers the curriculum names: preventing defect injection and removing injected defects." ;
     se:label "Two-lever defect minimization (inject fewer, remove more)" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A development process in which both prevention activities (training, education, planning) and removal activities (testing, code review) can be resourced." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Prevention without removal leaves early-injected defects in place; removal without prevention keeps the cycle expensive.", "Removal effort can exceed the reliability actually required (see the error-budget comparison for requirement-setting)." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes CS2023 SE-Reliability KA Core topics 3 and 5 at curriculum fidelity; the example levers are the curriculum's, not an exhaustive program." ;
     se:steps "Apply injection-prevention measures (training, education, planning) targeted at those classes and stages.", "Apply removal measures (testing, code review and similar approaches) sized to catch the remaining injected defects.", "Identify the defect classes historically injected by the team and the lifecycle stages where they enter.", "Track the injection/removal cycle over releases so prevention reduces what removal must catch." .

se:PRA-07D-FAILOVER-REHEARSAL a se:Practice ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07D-FAULT-TOLERANCE ;
     se:applicability "Services with recovery-time concerns; the disruptive test path applies where stopping the service is acceptable." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-FAILURE-INEVITABLE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Plan failure-handling logic during development so automated failover can operate; choose backup and recovery methods (full or incremental), restore-point frequency, storage location and retention under the influence of the need for successful recovery; and constantly rehearse backup, disaster-recovery and failover testing as the production environment changes, including the disruptive test path of stopping the service, identifying the checkpoint state and triggering failover." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A failover and recovery path whose behavior is known from rehearsal, not assumption.", "Reduced recovery time through automation of the failover step." ;
     se:intent "Reduce recovery time after inevitable failures by preparing, automating and repeatedly testing the failover and recovery path." ;
     se:label "Rehearsed failover and recovery preparedness" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A production environment in which a disruptive failover test can be scheduled.", "Failure-handling logic designed into the application during development." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Disruptive rehearsal itself causes the outage it is meant to prevent if scheduled badly.", "Unrehearsed recovery paths silently rot as production drifts from the documented environment." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.5 at professional-BOK fidelity." ;
     se:steps "Decide backup/recovery methods, restore-point frequency, storage and retention from the recovery requirement.", "Design the failure-handling logic and automated failover path with the application.", "Record outcomes and repair the recovery path where the rehearsal fails.", "Run the disruptive test: stop the service, identify the checkpoint state, trigger failover, verify recovery.", "Schedule rehearsals triggered by production changes (SWEBOK's constant-rehearsal framing)." .

se:PRA-07D-RELIABILITY-TELEMETRY a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07D-OBSERVABILITY-FEEDBACK ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-EVIDENCE-NOT-HOPE ;
     se:applicability "Operated services whose reliability and capacity must be steered by evidence." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07D-MEASUREMENT-BASED-RELIABILITY, se:CLM-07D-SWEBOK-EVIDENCE-NOT-HOPE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Instrument the service with the measurement families the anchors name — telemetry, monitoring and alerting, dashboards, and release qualification measurements — and surface the resulting indicators to stakeholders in real time, so that unplanned nonavailability and capacity/availability trends are investigated from data rather than discovered by customers." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07D-CS2023-MEASUREMENT-TELEMETRY, se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-MONITOR-EVIDENCE ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Investigations triggered by measured deviations instead of stakeholder reports.", "Real-time indicator coverage of the operational-health dimensions the anchors name." ;
     se:intent "Give engineers and stakeholders real-time evidence about system quality and operational health so reliability, capacity, continuity and availability decisions rest on observation." ;
     se:label "Measurement-based reliability telemetry loop" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "An operating service with an observable runtime surface.", "Stakeholders defined to receive the indicators." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Indicator volume without curation becomes noise that hides real deviations.", "Telemetry gaps leave the very dimensions being claimed as observed actually unobserved." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes CS2023 SE-Reliability Non-core topic 11 together with SWEBOK KA6 sections 4.3 and 2.3; curriculum-elective and professional-norm bases are recorded separately in the underlying evidence." ;
     se:steps "Define alerting on the indicators that signify degrading reliability, capacity, continuity or availability.", "Feed observed failure and utilization data back into capacity planning and reliability measurement.", "Instrument production monitoring and product telemetry as the primary evidence source.", "Review capacity, continuity and availability indicators against service-level targets; investigate unplanned deviations.", "Select the instrument families (telemetry, monitoring/alerting, dashboards, release qualification metrics) and what each must reveal." .

se:PRA-07E-CI-PIPELINE a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-SCM ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07E-CI-PIPELINE-TRIGGER ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-KU, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:applicability "Teams with a shared repository and automated build capability; the configured checks scale with project context." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07E-CI-PIPELINE-TRIGGER, se:CLM-07E-CI-PRELIMINARY-VALIDATION ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Run an automated pipeline that is triggered when changes are committed to the shared source-control repository: retrieve a fresh copy of the complete source, compile and link, run the configured automated checks (static analysis of coding standards, unit tests, coverage metrics, documentation extraction as configured), and pass the resulting artifacts to the release management process, recording build and test results as the shared-code ground truth." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-CS2023-TOOLS-KU, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Build artifacts are produced consistently and flow to release management.", "Every committed change receives automated build-plus-test feedback on a fresh copy of the source.", "The current head/trunk state (build and test success) is visible to the team without manual verification." ;
     se:intent "Give the team early, automated, ground-truth indication of whether the current shared head/trunk revision builds and passes basic tests." ;
     se:label "Continuous-integration pipeline over the shared source" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A shared source-control repository with monitoring capability.", "Agreement on which branch/area of the repository triggers the pipeline.", "An automated build for the project and at least basic automated tests." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Non-hermetic builds may pass locally but behave differently in the pipeline (hermeticity is one of the curriculum's named build-systems values).", "Slow pipelines delay feedback and can discourage frequent integration.", "Unstable automated checks erode trust in the ground-truth signal." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes the SWEBOK KA8 6.1 commit-triggered pipeline description at BOK fidelity, with the CS2023 SE-Tools CI topic and ground-truth outcome as curriculum anchor; not a vendor-specific pipeline recipe." ;
     se:steps "Execute the commands to compile and link the code.", "Monitor the source control system for changes committed to the target branch or area.", "On commit, retrieve a fresh copy of the complete source code.", "Record build and test outcomes as the ground-truth state of the shared code; pass resulting artifacts to the release management process.", "Run the configured automated checks (static analysis, unit tests, coverage, documentation extraction)." .

se:PRA-07E-ENV-AUTOMATION a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:PRI-07E-BUILD-FROM-CONTROLLED-SOURCE ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS ;
     se:applicability "Multi-environment delivery pipelines; strongest where environment definitions are fully code-expressible." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07E-ENV-SINGLE-SOURCE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Define the delivery-path environments (development, testing/QA, preproduction, production), automate their creation, and build them from a single code repository so all environments share the same code source (single source of truth) and stay synchronized with the production environment — the direction SWEBOK KA6 2.2 associates with infrastructure as code." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Environment drift becomes visible as code divergence rather than undocumented difference.", "Verification performed in a non-production environment reflects the production environment's configuration." ;
     se:intent "Make development, testing/QA, preproduction and production environments coherent and synchronized so verification results transfer to production." ;
     se:label "Environment parity through automated single-source builds" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A single code repository holding environment and product sources.", "Environment definitions that can be expressed and executed as code.", "Identified environment stages along the delivery path." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Automated environment maintenance itself becomes a deliverable that must be staffed.", "Full production parity can be constrained by external systems or licensing not expressible as code." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK KA6 section 2.2 at BOK fidelity; the automation/single-source element is the section's DevOps recommendation." ;
     se:steps "Build all environments from the same single code source.", "Express environment creation as automated, code-controlled definitions.", "Identify the environments needed at the project's stages (development, testing/QA, preproduction, production).", "Keep environments synchronized with the production environment as changes land." .

se:PRA-07E-REHEARSED-ROLLBACK a se:Practice ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ROLLBACK ;
     se:applicability "Production deployments whose failure or degradation would require restoring the previous state." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07E-ROLLBACK-REHEARSED ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Before a new version is deployed to production, develop a rollback and data migration plan as part of release risk assessment and rehearse the rollback; where automation is available, let automated surveillance trigger the rollback when the deployed change fails its validation period." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-RELEASE-PLANNING, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ROLLBACK ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Production incidents from a new version can be answered by a rehearsed, quick return to the previous working state." ;
     se:intent "Ensure a production deployment that causes defects or degradation can be quickly and easily returned to a working state." ;
     se:label "Planned and rehearsed rollback before production deployment" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A defined rollback path (environment-based or application-based release strategy) covering the software and its data.", "A release or version whose deployment risk has been assessed.", "Capability to execute and rehearse the rollback before production deployment." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Data migrations without a rehearsed reverse path can make rollback impossible even when the plan exists.", "Unrehearsed rollback plans fail under incident pressure." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK KA6 sections 3.3 and 2.1.1 at BOK fidelity; the automation element reflects the section's DevOps framing." ;
     se:steps "After deployment, monitor the deployed change through its validation period and trigger the rehearsed rollback when problems arise.", "Assess the risk of the given release during release/version planning.", "Develop the rollback and data migration plan covering the new version and its database changes.", "Rehearse the rollback before deploying the new version to production." .

se:PRA-07F-CHANGE-IMPACT-ANALYSIS a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-IMPACT-ANALYSIS-ROLE ;
     se:applicability "Maintenance change requests routed through SCM-controlled change processes." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-IMPACT-ANALYSIS-ROLE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Translate the modification request or problem report into software terms, identify the systems and software products the change would affect, estimate the resources needed to accomplish it, determine the risks involved, and recommend a course of action — following SWEBOK KA7 2.1.3, which locates the analysis after the change request enters the SCM process and matches KA8 3.1's technical evaluation of an SCR." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-IMPACT-ANALYSIS, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-KA8-SCR-PROCESS ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A recommendation that the change authority can accept, modify, defer or reject on an informed basis.", "An impact statement covering affected items, resource estimate and risk determination attached to the change request." ;
     se:intent "Understand the detailed effects a proposed maintenance change has on existing software before implementing it, so that affected items, resource needs and risks are known when the change decision is made." ;
     se:label "Maintenance impact analysis before change" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A logged change request (MR or PR) with enough context to translate into software terms.", "Maintainer knowledge of the software's structure and content, supported where possible by maintainability-conscious design." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Analysis cost grows with system size; SWEBOK asks that it be conducted as cost-effectively as possible.", "Incomplete structural knowledge yields missed affected items." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK v4.0a KA7 2.1.3 and KA8 3.1 at BOD-described fidelity; not a tool-specific procedure." ;
     se:steps "Develop potential solutions and recommend a course of action to the change authority.", "Estimate the resources needed to accomplish the change and determine the risks involved.", "Identify all systems and software products that would be affected by the change.", "Receive the change request through the SCM/SCR process and record its type (defect or enhancement) and severity.", "Translate the request into software terms using knowledge of the software's structure and content." .

se:PRA-07F-REFACTORING-WITH-TOOLS a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-DEBT-REMEDY ;
     se:applicability "Maintained codebases with tool support and a behavior safety net." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-DEBT-REMEDY ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Identify code in need of refactoring with static-analysis tools, apply standard refactoring patterns (rename, inline, outline, etc.) using IDE refactoring tooling, and use refactoring before and after code changes during maintenance to reduce technical debt — combining the CS2023 SE-Refactoring tool topics (3a-3c) with SWEBOK KA7 4.2's placement of refactoring in maintenance activity." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-CS2023-REFACTORING-KU, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-REFACTORING ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Reduced technical debt and improved internal structure without intended behavior change, applied incrementally alongside maintenance work." ;
     se:intent "Reduce the technical debt of the codebase around functional change work by applying standard refactoring patterns with IDE and static-analysis tool support, without changing program behavior." ;
     se:label "Tool-assisted refactoring around maintenance changes" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "Behavior-preserving safety net (tests or verification) sufficient to detect unintended behavior change.", "IDE refactoring support and static-analysis tooling for the language and codebase." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Refactorings too large to commit safely require large-scale techniques (see se:PAT-07F-DUAL-API-LARGE-SCALE-REFACTORING).", "Tool-assisted changes can still alter observable behavior; behavior preservation is an aim, not a guarantee." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes CS2023 SE-Refactoring KA Core topics 3a-3c with SWEBOK KA7 4.2 maintenance framing; curriculum and BOK sources are combined at their respective fidelities." ;
     se:steps "Apply refactorings with IDE tool support around the planned functional change.", "Run static analysis to identify code in need of refactoring and to help generate candidate changes.", "Select standard refactoring patterns appropriate to the identified problem.", "Verify behavior is unchanged (regression testing is the maintenance-relevant check per SWEBOK KA7 2.1.2 context)." .

se:PRA-07F-TECH-DEBT-ASSESSMENT a se:Practice ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-07F-TECHNICAL-DEBT ;
     se:applicability "Organizations deciding whether and where to invest maintenance capacity in debt reduction." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-DEFINITION ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Use available tooling and indicators — size, complexity, engineering flaw counts and violations of good architectural design and coding practices — to measure technical debt, then determine the quality of current software, the extent of the debt, the potential savings from quality investment and the impact of current quality issues on the business, treating debt as one contributor to unplanned work and examining root causes rather than handling it in isolation, per SWEBOK KA7 2.3.1 with the urgency triage of 2.1.4." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINTAINABILITY-DEBT, se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-TECH-DEBT-COST ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A quantified debt picture with urgency and business-impact attribution that can drive prioritization." ;
     se:intent "Base technical-debt decisions on measured evidence about current quality, debt extent, potential savings and business impact instead of treating debt work as uniformly urgent or uniformly deferrable." ;
     se:label "Measured technical-debt assessment before debt decisions" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A business owner able to state the impact of quality issues on the business.", "Access to tooling that computes the debt indicators on the codebase (SWEBOK names static analysis and quality assessment tooling, including visualization tools with debt estimation and code-smell detection)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Indicator tooling measures proxies; numbers can be gamed or misread.", "Uncertain reward is a common barrier; measurement alone does not create stakeholder buy-in." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK v4.0a KA7 2.3.1 (with 2.1.4 triage and 4.3 tooling) at BOK-described fidelity." ;
     se:steps "Determine potential savings from investing in quality enhancement.", "Determine the impact of current quality issues on the business.", "Examine root causes, including team and process factors, before selecting remediation.", "Measure debt indicators (size, complexity, flaws, practice violations) with available tooling.", "Triage urgency: not all technical debt is urgent (code quality versus relevance)." .

se:PRA-07G-MULTI-TECHNIQUE-ESTIMATION a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MULTI-ESTIMATE-RECONCILE ;
     se:applicability "Estimates feeding significant decisions (scope, staffing, bid/no-bid, go/no-go); the Guide scales effort to the consequences of a wrong decision." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-ESTIMATES-UNCERTAIN, se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MULTI-ESTIMATE-RECONCILE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Estimate the same quantity with more than one general technique — expert judgment (optionally via group processes), estimation by analogy, bottom-up decomposition, and parametric estimation where a validated equation and historical data exist — possibly with different estimators; examine the estimates for convergence or divergence; on divergence, look for overlooked factors and reestimate toward convergence; express the result as a range with an uncertainty assessment and negotiate revisions with affected stakeholders until consensus is reached." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-ESTIMATION, se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-ESTIMATION ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A reconciled estimate with explicit uncertainty, or a documented list of unresolved divergence causes.", "Technique provenance recorded for later estimation improvement." ;
     se:intent "Counter the inherent uncertainty and error-proneness of software effort/cost estimation by triangulating techniques, investigating divergence and disclosing uncertainty instead of emitting a single number." ;
     se:label "Estimate with multiple techniques and reconcile" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A decision whose consequences justify the extra estimation effort.", "At least two estimation techniques feasible in context (e.g., a known analogy or historical data for a calibrated model)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Group estimation processes can converge on a dominant opinion rather than information.", "Multiple biased techniques can converge on the same wrong value, suggesting false accuracy.", "The extra effort is wasted on decisions with small consequences." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK KA15 section 8 (8.1-8.5) together with KA9 section 2.3 (multiple approaches and reconciliation); joining the two sections into one procedure is a lane composition disclosed here, not a procedure printed in either section." ;
     se:steps "Compare the estimates; on divergence, identify overlooked factors and reestimate.", "Express the reconciled result as a range with an uncertainty assessment.", "Produce estimates per technique, applying technique-specific care (difference lists for analogy; cross-cutting allowances for decomposition).", "Record the techniques used and revisit the estimate against actuals when the work completes.", "Select the techniques feasible from available inputs (experts, analogies, historical data, work breakdown).", "State the quantity to estimate and the decision it serves." .

se:PRA-07G-NEEDS-DRIVEN-MEASUREMENT a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07G-SEM-MEASUREMENT ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MEASUREMENT-PROCESS, se:PRI-07G-MEASUREMENT-INFORMED-MANAGEMENT ;
     se:applicability "Measurement programs at project, process or work-product level; organizational-unit characterization is context-specific." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-MEASUREMENT-PROCESS ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Apply the planning part of the SWEBOK KA9 section 6 measurement process: characterize the organizational unit (processes, application domains, technology, interfaces, structure); identify information needs based on the unit's goals, constraints, risks and problems, prioritized and communicated with stakeholders; select candidate measures with clear links to those needs, judged against criteria such as cost of collection, degree of process disruption, ease of obtaining accurate consistent data and ease of analysis and reporting; define data collection, analysis and reporting procedures and evaluation criteria for information products; then integrate the procedures into the processes being measured." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-MEASUREMENT ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A measurement plan in which every measure traces to a stated information need.", "Evaluation criteria against which information products can be judged." ;
     se:intent "Prevent unfocused or ritual data collection by deriving every measure from explicitly identified information needs of the organizational unit." ;
     se:label "Select measures from information needs" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "Organizational commitment and resources for measurement (KA9 6.1).", "Stakeholders able to articulate and prioritize information needs." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Collection procedures can disrupt the measured processes if disruption was not a selection criterion.", "Information needs drift as the unit's goals change, silently invalidating the measure set.", "Measures without committed resources decay into partial, untrustworthy data." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 6 (especially 6.2) as the Guide's characterization of the ISO/IEC/IEEE 15939 process; the standard's normative text is not cited." ;
     se:steps "Characterize the organizational unit and its constraints.", "Define collection, analysis and reporting procedures and information-product evaluation criteria.", "Evaluate information products and the process against the criteria; record lessons learned.", "Identify and prioritize information needs from goals, constraints, risks and problems.", "Integrate the procedures into the measured processes and communicate results.", "Select measures with clear links to the needs, judging candidates by collection cost, disruption, data accuracy and analysis ease." .

se:PRA-07G-RISK-REGISTER-MAINTENANCE a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07G-SEM-RISK-MANAGEMENT ;
     se:applicability "Software projects under any SDLC; the revisit cadence is context-dependent." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07G-SWEBOK-RISK-LIFECYCLE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:description "Operate the risk-management cycle presented in SWEBOK KA9 section 2.5: identify risk factors (supported by assessment methods such as expert judgment, historical data, decision trees and process simulations), analyze each factor's probability and potential impact, prioritize the factors, develop mitigation strategies that reduce event probability and minimize impact, and record risks with their information in a risk register that can also serve regulatory compliance; agree project abandonment conditions with stakeholders and pay particular attention to risks related to quality requirements such as safety or security." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA9-RISK-QUALITY ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A current project risk profile whose data lives in an auditable register.", "Named mitigation owners and strategies for prioritized risks." ;
     se:intent "Keep risk identification, analysis, prioritization and mitigation current across the whole project instead of treating risk management as a one-time startup exercise." ;
     se:label "Maintain a lifecycle-long risk register" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "Access to assessment inputs (expertise, historical data or models) of usable quality.", "An agreed risk-management procedure and an owner for the register." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Assessment quality depends on available expertise and history; simulations and trees inherit their input biases.", "Register paperwork can displace actual mitigation work.", "The register goes stale between revisits; periodic revisit discipline is the practice's load-bearing element." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 section 2.5 at BOK fidelity; the named assessment methods are the section's examples, not an exhaustive professional procedure." ;
     se:steps "Analyze each factor's probability and potential impact.", "Define mitigation strategies (probability reduction and impact minimization) per prioritized factor.", "Identify risk factors using the assessment methods available in context.", "Prioritize the factors by analysis results.", "Record each risk, its analysis and its mitigation status in the risk register.", "Revisit the risk profile and recompute risk exposures at predetermined intervals through the project life cycle, not only at initiation." .

se:PRA-ARC-API-CONTRACT-DOCUMENTATION a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-CS2023-SE-SOFTWARE-DESIGN ;
     se:applicability "API design in a curriculum or educational context; not a professional standard." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-ARC-0004 ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:description "For each API operation, describe its effect on the system rather than generally its implementation; state preconditions, postconditions and invariants; keep error reporting clear, predictable and actionable; produce an error for input that does not match the contract; and manage without reporting only those errors that can be reliably managed. CS2023 SE-Design KA Core topic 5 and learning outcome 5 anchor this practice." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0005 ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Callers can distinguish contract-matching from contract-violating input.", "Each operation carries a documented contract (effect, preconditions, postconditions, invariants) and error conditions." ;
     se:intent "Document each API operation's contract and error conditions so callers can use the operation without knowing its implementation." ;
     se:label "Documenting API contracts and error conditions" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:preconditions "An API surface with identified operations exists.", "Each operation's valid input domain can be characterized." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Documenting implementation details locks callers to internals and hurts expandability.", "Silently absorbing contract-violating input hides defects, contrary to the curriculum's error-reporting expectation." ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope (KA Core topic 5, learning outcome 5)." ;
     se:steps "Define error reports to be clear, predictable and actionable.", "Ensure input that does not match the contract produces an error; reliably manageable errors may be managed without reporting.", "For each operation, write its effect-on-system description and avoid documenting internal implementation.", "State preconditions, postconditions and invariants for the operation." .

se:PRA-ARC-BOUNDARY-IDENTIFICATION a se:Practice ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:PRI-ARC-SEPARATION-OF-CONCERNS ;
     se:applicability "Architectural-design-level analysis in a curriculum or educational context." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-ARC-0003 ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:description "Work from an architectural design description to enumerate components, mark the boundaries between them, and identify which components depend on which. CS2023 SE-Design lists this as CS Core topic 2c and its CS Core learning outcome 4 expects students to identify the dependencies among a set of software components in an architectural design." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0002, se:EVD-ARC-0004 ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A documented list of component boundaries and inter-component dependencies.", "Dependencies are explicit enough to support later design-impact analysis." ;
     se:intent "Identify the component boundaries and the dependency relationships among components in an architectural design." ;
     se:label "Identifying component boundaries and dependencies" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:preconditions "An architectural design description at a suitable abstraction level exists.", "Components are identifiable as design-level entities (not yet implementation modules)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Boundaries drawn without regard to concerns may couple unrelated responsibilities.", "Dependencies inferred from implementation details rather than the architectural design may overstate coupling." ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope (CS Core topics 2c and learning outcome 4)." ;
     se:steps "Enumerate the components visible in the design description.", "Identify dependencies among the components (learning outcome 4).", "Mark the boundary of each component: what it owns and what lies outside it.", "Record the boundary and dependency decisions for review." .

se:PRA-REQ-MULTI-SOURCE-ELICITATION a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-CS2023-SE-PRODUCT-REQUIREMENTS ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-REQ-ELICITATION-MULTI-SOURCE ;
     se:applicability "Products with multiple stakeholder groups; the example lists are illustrative, not exhaustive." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-REQ-ELICITATION-MULTI-SOURCE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:description "Elicit requirements from multiple source groups — users, administrators and support personnel are the curriculum's examples — using multiple gathering methods such as surveys, interviews and behavioral analysis, as presented in the CS2023 SE-Requirements elicitation topic." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-ELICITATION-SOURCES-METHODS ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A requirement set reflecting more than one source group and more than one gathering method.", "Recorded source and method provenance for elicited requirements." ;
     se:intent "Reduce single-source bias in the gathered requirement set by deliberately combining requirement sources and gathering methods." ;
     se:label "Multi-source, multi-method requirements elicitation" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A chosen combination of gathering methods appropriate to the product context.", "Access to at least two distinguishable requirement source groups." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Different methods yield requirements at incompatible levels of detail.", "Merging across sources surfaces conflicts that must be resolved, adding effort.", "Sources may be unavailable or unrepresentative." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes CS2023 SE-Requirements KA Core topic 3 (elicitation sources and methods) at curriculum-scope fidelity; not a professional-standard procedure." ;
     se:steps "Collect and record elicited requirements with source and method provenance.", "Compare and merge results across sources and methods, flagging conflicts for resolution.", "Identify the requirement source groups touching the product (for example users, administrators, support personnel).", "Select gathering methods (for example surveys, interviews, behavioral analysis) suited to each group." .

se:PRA-REQ-PROTOTYPE-VALIDATION a se:Practice ;
     se:DEPENDS_ON se:CON-REQ-REQUIREMENTS-ELICITATION ;
     se:applicability "Requirement sets whose correctness is hard to assess in the abstract and where stakeholders can inspect an artifact." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-REQ-PROTOTYPE-DUAL-ROLE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:description "Build a lightweight representation of the software system — the curriculum's learning-outcome examples include a mock-up or MVP — and use the same artifact for eliciting requirements and for validating or confirming them with stakeholders." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-REQ-PROTOTYPE-AND-CHANGE ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Requirements confirmed, refuted or newly elicited through stakeholder reaction to the prototype." ;
     se:intent "Use a prototype both to surface new requirements and to validate or confirm an existing requirement set." ;
     se:label "Prototype-based requirement validation" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:preconditions "A candidate requirement set to validate, or an elicitation target.", "Capacity to build and discard a minimal or throwaway artifact.", "Stakeholders able to react to a prototype." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Prototype effort can exceed the value of the validated decisions.", "Prototype fidelity may over- or under-represent the real system, biasing feedback.", "Stakeholders may mistake a throwaway prototype for the product direction." ;
     se:scope_description "Operationalizes CS2023 SE-Requirements Non-core topic 7 (prototyping as a tool for both eliciting and validating/confirming requirements); an elective curriculum topic, not a mandated practice." ;
     se:steps "Build the minimal prototype covering the requirements in question.", "Choose the prototype fidelity needed for the decision at hand (mock-up, MVP, etc.).", "Update the requirement set and record which requirements the prototype validated.", "Walk stakeholders through the prototype, recording reactions as newly elicited or confirmed/refuted requirements." .

se:PRA-TVV-CONTEXT-GATED-CODE-TESTING a se:Practice ;
     se:applicability "NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 recommendation context; adoption is governed by risk, cost, feasibility and applicability, not presumed universal." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-TVV-0004 ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:description "Operationalizes NIST SSDF v1.1 PW.8: task PW.8.1 gates the decision (should executable code testing be performed to find vulnerabilities not identified by previous reviews, analysis or testing, and which types), and task PW.8.2 executes the decided testing end-to-end including documentation and issue triage. The parallel PW.7.1 decision for human-readable code review/analysis illustrates the same staged, organization-defined choice." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0010, se:EVD-TVV-0012 ;
     se:expected_outcomes "A documented result set with triaged issues and recommended remediations.", "An explicit, recorded testing decision rather than testing by default." ;
     se:intent "Decide deliberately whether executable code testing should be performed and which types to use, then scope, design, execute and document that testing with recorded, triaged findings." ;
     se:label "Context-gated executable code testing" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:preconditions "A development workflow or issue tracking system exists for recording and triaging discovered issues.", "The organization has defined how such method choices are made (per PW.7.1's organization-defined determination for review versus analysis).", "The stage of the software and the results of previous reviews, analysis or testing are known (PW.8.1 frames the new testing against those findings)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Skipping the gating decision wastes testing effort already covered by prior reviews or analysis.", "Treating the tasks as a mandatory checklist or fixed sequence contradicts the framework's risk-based, non-checklist guidance." ;
     se:scope_description "Secure-development context per NIST SP 800-218 v1.1 PW.8 (with PW.7.1 as the parallel review/analysis decision)." ;
     se:steps "Design the tests (PW.8.2).", "Determine whether executable code testing should be performed, and which testing types, informed by the stage of the software (PW.8.1; notional example).", "Document the results, recording and triaging all discovered issues and recommended remediations in the development team's workflow or issue tracking system (PW.8.2).", "Perform the testing (PW.8.2).", "Scope the testing (PW.8.2)." .

se:PRA-TVV-DEFECT-TRACKING-REPRODUCIBILITY a se:Practice ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-CS2023-SE-VALIDATION ;
     se:IMPLEMENTS se:CLM-TVV-0004 ;
     se:applicability "Applies wherever defects flow into a tracking workflow; the NIST tasks are recommendations, while the curriculum presents the topic as learning content." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:description "Combines CS2023's SE-Validation KA Core topic 8e (defect tracking and prioritization, including reproducibility of reported defects) with the NIST SSDF record-and-triage tasks (PW.7.2, PW.8.2): every discovered issue and its recommended remediation is recorded and triaged in the development team's workflow or issue tracking system." ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0009, se:EVD-TVV-0010 ;
     se:expected_outcomes "Prioritized, reproducible defect records that can drive fix work and feed regression-test selection." ;
     se:intent "Keep defects visible and prioritizable by recording them with reproducibility information and triaging recommended remediations in the team's workflow." ;
     se:label "Defect tracking with reproducibility" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:preconditions "An issue tracking system or workflow integrated with development exists.", "Defect reports capture enough information to attempt reproduction." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:risks "Recording without triage accumulates an unactionable backlog.", "Unreproducible reports weaken prioritization and prevent verifying that a fix works." ;
     se:scope_description "Curriculum topic (CS2023 SE-Validation KA Core 8e) intersected with NIST SSDF record/triage tasks; secure-development and curriculum framing coexist." ;
     se:steps "Capture reproducibility information for reported defects to support prioritization and later fix verification.", "Record each discovered defect or vulnerability together with its recommended remediation in the team's issue tracking system or workflow.", "Triage recorded issues to establish priority." .

se:PRI-07A-KEEP-VIEWS-DISTINCT a se:Principle ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:applicability "Knowledge organization of SE process/lifecycle/quality content; usable as a modeling discipline for multi-view engineering taxonomies." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07A-MODELS-STRUCTURE-SE ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA11-MODELING-PRINCIPLES, se:EVD-07A-KA12-QUALITY-OVERLOAD ;
     se:label "Keep lifecycle, process and quality lenses distinct but explicitly related" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "A lane-level structuring rationale, not a source sentence; it inherits the coverage limits of the inspected sections." ;
     se:rationale "The inspected SWEBOK KAs keep the lenses apart while relating them: KA10 carries the process/lifecycle lens, KA12 discusses quality as a basic concept appearing across many knowledge areas and distinguishes product quality from process quality, and KA11 states models and methods span multiple life cycle phases regardless of the type of life cycle process model. A single merged tree would erase distinctions the sources themselves make." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "SEK-07A lane structuring principle (lane-level synthesis grounded in the inspected section scopes of KA10, KA11 and KA12); it governs how this lane's taxonomy views are modeled." ;
     se:statement "Lifecycle models, engineering processes and quality models classify the same engineering effort from different perspectives; they should be kept as distinct views connected by explicit cross-cutting relations rather than merged into one universal hierarchy." .

se:PRI-07A-TAILOR-DONT-ADOPT a se:Principle ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07A-KA10-PROCESS-DEFINITION ;
     se:applicability "Teams and organizations choosing or tailoring software life cycle models." ;
     se:claim_refs se:CLM-07A-ADAPTATION-DOCUMENTED, se:CLM-07A-NO-UNIVERSAL-PROCESS ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07A-KA10-PROCESS-DEFINITION, se:EVD-07A-KA10-STAGES-ADAPTATION ;
     se:exceptions "Contractual or regulatory contexts that prescribe a specific process standard may constrain the adaptation space (recorded here as an exception without admitting the normative text)." ;
     se:label "Select and adapt the life cycle; never adopt a model as a mandate" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "The principle's strength beyond the Guide's presentation is untested in this lane (no empirical case evidence cited)." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK KA10 states that no ideal process or set of processes exists and that processes must be selected, adapted and applied as appropriate for each project and organizational context; it presents life cycle adaptation as an explicit activity (identify characteristics, select standards/strategy/model/stages/processes, document decisions and rationale) that does not require keeping standard names or including all processes; and its practical considerations add that lifecycle discussions should be supported by realistic estimations and measurements." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Derived within SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA10 inspected sections (1.1, 2.8, 2.9); professional-BOK generally accepted knowledge, not a universal engineering law." ;
     se:statement "A software life cycle model should be selected and adapted to the differential characteristics of the software system, its stakeholders and the organization — with the adaptation decisions and their rationale documented — rather than any single model being adopted as a universal mandate." .

se:PRI-07B-MINIMIZE-COMPLEXITY a se:Principle ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:SUB-07B-SOFTWARE-CONSTRUCTION ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07B-SWEBOK-MINIMIZE-COMPLEXITY ;
     se:applicability "Construction of code intended to be read, tested and changed by people other than a single author." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:label "Minimize complexity; prefer simple, readable code" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "Strength of the principle beyond the Guide's assertion (for example measured maintainability effects) is untested in this lane; no empirical evidence is cited.", "The Guide states no exceptions to this goal in the inspected text; nothing in this lane may be read as licensing unnecessary complexity." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 1.1 grounds the goal in limited human working memory (especially over long periods), states the need applies to essentially every aspect of software construction and is particularly critical to testing, and names standards, modular design and construction-focused quality techniques as the supports through which reduced complexity is achieved." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Professional-BOK scope (SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA4 section 1.1, printed page 4-2); a guide to generally accepted knowledge, not an empirical effect claim." ;
     se:statement "Software construction should minimize complexity, favoring simple and readable code over clever code, because people's limited working-memory capacity constrains the complex structures they can hold, and reduced complexity benefits essentially every construction activity, testing in particular." .

se:PRI-07C-DESIGN-SECURITY-IN a se:Principle ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07C-SECURITY-LIFECYCLE ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07C-CS2023-SEC-FOUNDATIONS ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07C-SWEBOK-SECURITY-BY-DESIGN ;
     se:applicability "Software under active development with evolving requirements and a maintainable requirements/design record." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:exceptions "Already-released legacy software where only post-hoc vulnerability management is feasible; the sources' own maintenance and vulnerability-handling provisions cover that path." ;
     se:label "Design security in across the lifecycle" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical effectiveness evidence is cited in this lane; the SWEBOK grounding is explicitly a 'generally accepted belief'.", "Security remains one design goal among others (privacy, performance), to be optimized rather than maximized without tradeoff analysis." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK KA13 section 1 reports the design-in-over-patch preference as a generally accepted belief and requires every life cycle stage to be considered; SSDF PW.1 describes secure-by-design attention during design as key for improving software security, and PO.1 expects security requirements to be known at all times throughout the SDLC; the CS2023 SEC-Foundations security-mindset topic converges on lifecycle-crosscutting security thinking. The three authorities are kept distinct: belief, recommendation and curriculum framing." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Grounded in SWEBOK KA13 section 1, SSDF practices PO.1 and PW.1, and CS2023 SEC-Foundations at their respective authority levels; not asserted as an empirical law." ;
     se:statement "Security should be treated as a design-time concern carried through every development life cycle stage — with defined security requirements and properties, threat-informed design and architecture, and maintained traceability into implementation and verification — rather than something patched in only after development." .

se:PRI-07D-EVIDENCE-OVER-HOPE a se:Principle ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-MONITOR-EVIDENCE ;
     se:applicability "Operated services with stakeholders who consume operational health information." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:exceptions "Pre-operational artifacts with no runtime observation surface." ;
     se:label "Operate on measured evidence, not assumption" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "The CS2023 anchor is Non-core (elective); the SWEBOK norm is stated for a DevOps mindset, which the Guide itself presents as an approach rather than a universal requirement." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 4.3 states that in a DevOps mindset hope should not be a strategy and that engineers should be informed about system quality and operational health with real-time KPI evidence; CS2023's SE-Reliability unit lists measurement-based analysis (telemetry, monitoring and alerting, dashboards, release qualification metrics) as its reliability-analysis topic." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Combines a SWEBOK KA6 operations-control norm with a CS2023 elective topic; professional-guidance and curriculum framings are kept distinct in the underlying evidence." ;
     se:statement "Operational decisions about a software service's reliability, capacity, continuity and availability should be driven by observed evidence — telemetry, monitoring and alerting, dashboards and release qualification measurements available to stakeholders in real time — rather than by assumption or hope." .

se:PRI-07D-PLAN-FOR-FAILURE a se:Principle ;
     se:APPLIES_TO se:NODE-07D-RELIABILITY-RESILIENCE ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07D-SWEBOK-FAILOVER-INEVITABLE ;
     se:applicability "Long-lived operated software services where recovery time and failure consequences matter." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:exceptions "Throwaway or single-shot artifacts that are explicitly not expected to survive failure." ;
     se:label "Expect failure and engineer the response during development" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical case evidence in this lane measures how much rehearsed failover reduces recovery time; the Guide's 'drastically' is qualitative.", "The CS2023 anchor for context-dependence is a Non-core (elective) topic." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA6 section 2.5 states that failure is inevitable, that automated failover daemons can drastically reduce recovery time, that applications should include failure-handling logic which must be planned during development, and that preparedness must be constantly rehearsed as production changes; CS2023's fault-tolerance topic adds that the tolerated response is context-dependent (crash avoidance in flight-critical systems versus crash-before-corruption in data-processing systems)." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Derived within SWEBOK KA6 section 2.5 professional-BOK scope plus the CS2023 SE-Reliability Non-core topic 8 contrast; not asserted as a universal engineering law." ;
     se:statement "Failure of an operated software service should be treated as inevitable, and the failure response — failure-handling logic in the application, automated failover, and rehearsed backup/disaster-recovery procedures — should be planned during development rather than improvised after an outage, with the tolerated response matched to the failure's consequences in the specific domain." .

se:PRI-07E-BUILD-FROM-CONTROLLED-SOURCE a se:Principle ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:applicability "Products delivered through multi-environment pipelines where verification results must transfer to production and past versions may need re-creation." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA6-ENVIRONMENTS, se:EVD-07E-SWEBOK-KA8-BUILDING ;
     se:exceptions "Deliverables with no expected re-creation need (one-shot disposables) weaken the reproduction element, though the single-source element still applies." ;
     se:label "Build and deliver from one controlled source" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "No empirical evidence in this lane tests the principle's effect on delivery outcomes (see se:GAP-07E-NO-EMPIRICS).", "The single-repository automation element is presented in KA6 as a DevOps recommendation; the principle inherits that scoping." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK KA6 section 2.2 requires environments to be coherent and synchronized with production and reports the DevOps recommendation that environments be automated and built from a single code repository (single source of truth); SWEBOK KA8 section 6.1 defines building as constructing the correct SCI versions with appropriate configuration data, requires the ability to reproduce previous releases, and places supporting tools and build instructions under SCM control for exact rebuilds." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Derived within the scope of the inspected SWEBOK KA6 2.2 and KA8 6.1 sections; professional-BOK grounded but not asserted as a universal law of delivery." ;
     se:statement "Integration and delivery outputs should be produced by building the correct versions of the configuration items with the appropriate configuration data from a single controlled code source — with the supporting tools and build instructions under configuration control — so that what is verified is what is delivered and previous releases can be reproduced." .

se:PRI-07F-MANAGE-EVOLUTION-COMPLEXITY a se:Principle ;
     se:DERIVED_FROM se:CLM-07F-SWEBOK-EVOLUTION-LAWS ;
     se:applicability "Long-lived software products expected to evolve across multiple releases; short-lived or throwaway artifacts are out of scope." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:exceptions "Software approaching retirement, where investment in complexity reduction has no payback horizon." ;
     se:label "Actively counter complexity growth in evolving software" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "The laws are empirical research summaries reported by SWEBOK; their generalization is contested territory this lane does not adjudicate." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 1.5 reports Lehman's laws of Continuing Change (software must be continually adapted or it becomes progressively less satisfactory) and Increasing Complexity (complexity increases unless work is done to maintain or reduce it), and section 4.2 presents refactoring during maintenance as the behavior-preserving technique for improving internal structure and reducing technical debt." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Derived from SWEBOK KA7 sections 1.5 and 4.2 as BOK-reported findings; the underlying evolution research is cited secondhand and is not lane-verified." ;
     se:statement "In an evolving software product, growth in complexity must be actively countered — for example by refactoring to reduce technical debt — or the product becomes progressively less satisfactory to its users; change is continuous, so complexity management is a continuing activity rather than a one-time fix." .

se:PRI-07F-PRESERVE-INTEGRITY a se:Principle ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-KA8-SCR-PROCESS ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07F-SWEBOK-MAINT-DEFINITION ;
     se:applicability "Operational software products receiving maintenance modification, plan-based or Agile." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:exceptions "Emergency maintenance performs unscheduled modification to temporarily keep a system operational, with the formal process performed afterward (SWEBOK KA8 emergency path; KA7 1.6)." ;
     se:label "Preserve integrity while modifying operational software" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "The principle's strength beyond the inspected sections is untested in this lane; no case evidence is cited." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA7 section 1.1 reports the ISO/IEC/IEEE 14764 objective of software maintenance as modifying existing software while preserving its integrity, and KA8 section 3 describes the formal SCR evaluation and approval process with end-of-process audits that verify only approved changes have been made." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Derived within SWEBOK v4.0a KA7/KA8 inspected-section scope; not asserted as a universal law beyond professional-BOK report." ;
     se:statement "Modifications to operational software should be made in a way that preserves the integrity of the existing software product: the objective of maintenance is to modify existing software while preserving its integrity, and change should flow through analysis and control rather than unmanaged direct alteration." .

se:PRI-07G-CLOSE-ESTIMATION-LOOP a se:Principle ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-07G-CS2023-REQ-EFFORT ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-CS2023-REQ-EFFORT, se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS ;
     se:applicability "Any project that bases decisions on estimates and can observe the corresponding actuals." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-CS2023-REQ-EFFORT, se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-KA15-DECISION-PROCESS ;
     se:label "Close the loop on estimates" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "The CS2023 leg rests on a Non-core (elective) topic and is pedagogical.", "The principle presumes access to actual outcome data; where actuals are unobservable the loop cannot close." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA15 section 2.7 requires monitoring the performance of the selected alternative and closing the loop on estimates by comparing them to actual outcomes, stating that otherwise no one will ever know if the estimates were good, and that understanding what drives estimate-versus-actual differences helps refine estimation techniques over time; CS2023 teaches the same comparison pedagogically as the effort-estimation subtopic of practicing estimation and comparing it to how long tasks take." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Professional-BOK guidance (SWEBOK KA15 section 2.7) with a curriculum echo (CS2023 SE-Requirements Non-core topic 9b); cross-source principle at lane fidelity." ;
     se:statement "Estimates should be compared with the actual outcomes of the work they sized — both to judge the decisions the estimates supported and to refine the estimation techniques that produced them." .

se:PRI-07G-MEASUREMENT-INFORMED-MANAGEMENT a se:Principle ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-INTRO ;
     se:applicability "Software project and organizational management where measurement (qualitative or quantitative) is feasible." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-07" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-07G-SWEBOK-SEM-INTRO ;
     se:label "Pair management with measurement" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "The Guide asserts the principle without evidencing it in the inspected sections; no empirical support is cited by this lane." ;
     se:rationale "SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9's introduction frames measurement-informed management as a basic principle of any true engineering discipline, asserting that management without measurement (qualitative and quantitative) suggests a lack of discipline and measurement without management suggests a lack of purpose or context, and adopts working definitions of both management and measurement." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Professional-BOK principle as presented in the SWEBOK Guide v4.0a KA9 introduction (printed page 9-4); not an empirically validated law." ;
     se:statement "The management of software engineering work should be measurement-informed: qualitative and quantitative measurement supplies the information on which management acts, and management supplies the purpose and context that make measurement meaningful; using one without the other signals a discipline gap or a purpose gap." .

se:PRI-ARC-SEPARATION-OF-CONCERNS a se:Principle ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-ARC-0007 ;
     se:applicability "Software design education and curriculum-scoped design reasoning." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-ARC-0002 ;
     se:exceptions "The curriculum does not state that separation of concerns always dominates other goals; design-tradeoff evaluation is a separate Non-core topic (13)." ;
     se:label "Separation of concerns as a guiding design principle" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "Name-level curriculum anchor; the extracted text provides no detailed treatment of the principle.", "Not a universal prescription; the professional-BOK counterpart is carried separately by the reinspected SWEBOK records, not by this curriculum record." ;
     se:rationale "CS2023 SE-Design lists separation of concerns among system design principles (CS Core topic 1b) and its CS Core illustrative learning outcome 3 expects students to adapt a flawed system design to better follow principles such as separation of concerns or information hiding." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "CS2023 SE-Design curriculum scope; curriculum-taught guidance, not professional consensus." ;
     se:statement "A software design should be divided so that distinct parts address distinct concerns, and a design that violates this should be adapted toward it." .

se:PRI-REQ-EXPECT-CHANGE a se:Principle ;
     se:QUALIFIED_BY se:EVD-REQ-PROTOTYPE-AND-CHANGE ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-REQ-SE-PREAMBLE-MULTIVERSION ;
     se:applicability "Products with an expected multi-release lifespan in which requirements evolve." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:exceptions "Single-delivery or throwaway artifacts that are explicitly not expected to evolve." ;
     se:label "Expect requirements change" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PROPOSED" ;
     se:limitations "Product evolution is a Non-core (elective) topic in CS2023, so the curriculum itself does not make change-impact analysis mandatory for all programs.", "Strength of the principle beyond curriculum scope is untested in this lane (no empirical evidence cited)." ;
     se:rationale "The CS2023 SE preamble frames software engineering projects as usually multi-version, with features added or removed to meet product requirements over an expected lifespan, and the SE-Requirements unit includes product evolution (understanding the effect of changed requirements and what changes are needed) as a topic." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Derived within CS2023 SE curriculum scope (SE Preamble plus SE-Requirements Non-core topic 8); not asserted as a professional-consensus principle." ;
     se:statement "Requirements on a software product should be expected to change over the product's lifetime, and when they change the effect of the change — what must be modified as a result — needs to be understood." .

se:PRI-TVV-DAMP-OVER-DRY a se:Principle ;
     se:SUPPORTED_BY se:EVD-TVV-0008 ;
     se:applicability "Apply when writing or reviewing automated tests; it governs test code, not production code." ;
     se:created_on "2026-11-06" ;
     se:evidence_refs se:EVD-TVV-0008 ;
     se:label "DAMP over DRY in test code" ;
     se:lifecycle_status "PENDING_REVIEW" ;
     se:limitations "The curriculum states the rule without rationale, exceptions or scope limits; the rationale above is lane interpretation pending review." ;
     se:rationale "The source states the rule as a stylistic difference without elaborating a rationale. The working rationale offered for review is that locally duplicated test material keeps individual tests readable and independent, while abstraction introduced for production maintainability can obscure what a test actually exercises." ;
     se:record_version "0.1.0" ;
     se:scope_description "Test-code style within the CS2023 SE-Validation curriculum context (CS Core topic 5)." ;
     se:statement "Test code warrants more duplication than production code: CS2023's SE-Validation unit presents this as the stylistic difference between DAMP and DRY for tests versus production code." .

